/* 自动生成：python3 tools/build.py，勿手改。英文课程，中文仍以 content.md / data.js 为准。 */
window.SITE_DATA_EN = {"chapters": [{"id": "01", "title": "What is the crypto scene?", "emoji": "🌍", "intro": "Meet chains, coins, wallets, and exchanges", "tags": ["Basics"], "tip": "Note: Look at what it is and why it exists before you decide to take part.", "sections": [{"title": "What is the crypto scene?", "html": "<p><strong>The crypto scene is the market and community around cryptocurrency, and buying coins is only one way to take part.</strong></p>\n<p>\"Crypto scene\" is an everyday way of talking about the market and community around cryptocurrency, blockchains, and related apps.<br />\nSome people trade, some build apps, and some study the technology, payments, games, digital collectibles, and on-chain finance.</p>\n<p>Buying coins is only one way to take part.<br />\nYou can also study first, watch projects, and try a test network, without putting in real money.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. The crypto scene is a market and a community. Buying coins is only one way to take part.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Buying is not the only way. You can also learn, watch projects, and try a testnet before you put in real money.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Is buying coins the only way to take part in the crypto scene?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. If you do not buy, you are not taking part</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. Learning and watching also count as taking part</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Only building an app yourself counts</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "The crypto scene is the market and community around cryptocurrency, and buying coins is only one way to take part. \"Crypto scene\" is an everyday way of talking about the market and community around cryptocurrency, blockchains, and related apps. Some people trade, some build apps, and some study the technology, payments, games, digital collectibles, and on-chain finance. Buying coins is only one way to take part. You can also study first, watch projects, and try a test network, without putting in real money. Think it through Is buying coins the only way to take part in the crypto scene? A. Yes. If you do not buy, you are not taking part B. No. Learning and watching also count as taking part C. Only building an app yourself counts"}, {"title": "What is a blockchain?", "html": "<p><strong>A blockchain is a public ledger that many people keep together, and that is hard for one person to quietly change.</strong></p>\n<p>You can think of a blockchain as a record system maintained by more than one party.<br />\nTransactions are confirmed by the rules and written into the record. Participants agree on the record through a consensus mechanism.</p>\n<p>One important property is that it resists tampering.<br />\nWhen the network is running normally, and its consensus and security assumptions hold, a record that is already well confirmed cannot be changed on a whim by one person.</p>\n<p>A record that resists tampering does not mean the information that was entered, a project's marketing, or the other side of a trade is true.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A record that is hard to change only means a confirmed record is hard for one person to quietly rewrite. What was written can still be untrue.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Resisting tampering is not the same as the content being true. A project's marketing and the other side of a trade still have to be checked on their own.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>If a record is hard to change in secret, does that mean what was written is definitely true?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. Once it is on a blockchain, it is true</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. Resisting tampering is not the same as the content being true</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Only a record kept by one company can be true</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A blockchain is a public ledger that many people keep together, and that is hard for one person to quietly change. You can think of a blockchain as a record system maintained by more than one party. Transactions are confirmed by the rules and written into the record. Participants agree on the record through a consensus mechanism. One important property is that it resists tampering. When the network is running normally, and its consensus and security assumptions hold, a record that is already well confirmed cannot be changed on a whim by one person. A record that resists tampering does not mean the information that was entered, a project's marketing, or the other side of a trade is true. Think it through If a record is hard to change in secret, does that mean what was written is definitely true? A. Yes. Once it is on a blockchain, it is true B. No. Resisting tampering is not the same as the content being true C. Only a record kept by one company can be true"}, {"title": "What is a cryptocurrency?", "html": "<p><strong>A cryptocurrency is a digital asset on a chain; there are many kinds, and their risks differ.</strong></p>\n<p>Cryptocurrencies are digital assets managed with cryptography and the rules of a network.<br />\nUses, how they are issued, and risks differ a lot. Do not treat them all as the same kind of investment.</p>\n<p>BTC is the native asset of the Bitcoin network.<br />\nETH is the native asset of the Ethereum network, and it can be used to pay network fees, among other things.<br />\nMany tokens are issued by contracts on a chain that already exists.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. BTC is the native asset of the Bitcoin network. Many tokens are issued by contracts on a chain that already exists. Sharing the word coin does not make them the same thing.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Sharing the word coin does not mean the same issuance or the same risk. BTC belongs to the Bitcoin network itself. Many tokens are issued by contracts on a chain that already exists.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Are BTC and a token issued by a contract the same kind of thing?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. If the name says coin, they are the same</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. BTC is the Bitcoin network's native asset, and many tokens are issued by contracts</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. They are the same only when the price is the same</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A cryptocurrency is a digital asset on a chain; there are many kinds, and their risks differ. Cryptocurrencies are digital assets managed with cryptography and the rules of a network. Uses, how they are issued, and risks differ a lot. Do not treat them all as the same kind of investment. BTC is the native asset of the Bitcoin network. ETH is the native asset of the Ethereum network, and it can be used to pay network fees, among other things. Many tokens are issued by contracts on a chain that already exists. Think it through Are BTC and a token issued by a contract the same kind of thing? A. Yes. If the name says coin, they are the same B. No. BTC is the Bitcoin network's native asset, and many tokens are issued by contracts C. They are the same only when the price is the same"}, {"title": "Where BTC came from", "html": "<p><strong>Bitcoin was the first cryptocurrency; it started running in 2009, and its supply is capped at about 21 million coins.</strong></p>\n<p>October 31, 2008<br />\nAn author using the name \"Satoshi Nakamoto\" published the Bitcoin white paper :<br />\n《Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer Electronic Cash System》<br />\nThe usual Chinese translation is \"Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer Electronic Cash System\"</p>\n<p>The question in the white paper is direct: can two people pay each other online without relying on a financial institution to stop the same money from being spent twice?<br />\nIt combines digital signatures, proof of work, and network consensus, and designs a peer-to-peer electronic cash system.</p>\n<p>On January 3, 2009, the Bitcoin genesis block was created, and the Bitcoin network started running.<br />\nSatoshi Nakamoto's real identity has never been confirmed in a way people agree on.</p>\n<p>Under the current consensus rules, the issuance cap for BTC is about 21 million coins.<br />\nIt is not issued by a company whenever the company wants more. It is produced gradually under the network's rules.<br />\nScarcity is one of its properties. It does not guarantee that the price only rises.</p>\n<p>📄 Want to read the white paper? Start from the official bitcoin.org pages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>English PDF: <a class=\"ext\" href=\"https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf\">https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf</a><span class=\"ext-meta\"><span class=\"domain\">🌐 bitcoin.org</span><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></span></li>\n<li>Other languages: <a class=\"ext\" href=\"https://bitcoin.org/en/bitcoin-paper\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://bitcoin.org/en/bitcoin-paper\">https://bitcoin.org/en/bitcoin-paper</a><span class=\"ext-meta\"><span class=\"domain\">🌐 bitcoin.org</span><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://bitcoin.org/en/bitcoin-paper\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></span></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. Under the current consensus rules, the cap is about 21 million. That is an issuance rule. It does not guarantee that the price only rises.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. BTC is not printed by a company on demand. The cap is about 21 million, and scarcity does not mean the price only rises.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Under the current rules, about how many BTC can be issued at most?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. About 21 million, and the price is not guaranteed to only rise</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. A company prints more when demand rises</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. There is no cap. It can be issued without limit</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Bitcoin was the first cryptocurrency; it started running in 2009, and its supply is capped at about 21 million coins. October 31, 2008 An author using the name \"Satoshi Nakamoto\" published the Bitcoin white paper : 《Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer Electronic Cash System》 The usual Chinese translation is \"Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer Electronic Cash System\" The question in the white paper is direct: can two people pay each other online without relying on a financial institution to stop the same money from being spent twice? It combines digital signatures, proof of work, and network consensus, and designs a peer-to-peer electronic cash system. On January 3, 2009, the Bitcoin genesis block was created, and the Bitcoin network started running. Satoshi Nakamoto's real identity has never been confirmed in a way people agree on. Under the current consensus rules, the issuance cap for BTC is about 21 million coins. It is not issued by a company whenever the company wants more. It is produced gradually under the network's rules. Scarcity is one of its properties. It does not guarantee that the price only rises. 📄 Want to read the white paper? Start from the official bitcoin.org pages: English PDF: https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf 🌐 bitcoin.org ⏳ Not checked yet Other languages: https://bitcoin.org/en/bitcoin-paper 🌐 bitcoin.org ⏳ Not checked yet Think it through Under the current rules, about how many BTC can be issued at most? A. About 21 million, and the price is not guaranteed to only rise B. A company prints more when demand rises C. There is no cap. It can be issued without limit"}, {"title": "Coin or token?", "html": "<p><strong>A native coin belongs to a chain itself, and a token is issued on someone else's chain.</strong></p>\n<p>A common distinction: a native coin belongs to a network itself.<br />\nA token is issued on a network that already exists.<br />\nETH and an ERC-20 token on Ethereum are the example.</p>\n<p>In everyday talk, people often call all of them coins.<br />\nThe same name does not mean they were issued the same way.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. ETH belongs to the Ethereum network itself. An ERC-20 token is issued on that existing network.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Calling them all coins is only a way of speaking. ETH is the native coin. An ERC-20 token is not the chain itself.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>What is the main difference between ETH and an ERC-20 token on Ethereum?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. There is no difference. Being on Ethereum makes them the same</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. ETH belongs to the network itself. An ERC-20 token is issued on a network that already exists</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. ERC-20 is Ethereum's native coin</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A native coin belongs to a chain itself, and a token is issued on someone else's chain. A common distinction: a native coin belongs to a network itself. A token is issued on a network that already exists. ETH and an ERC-20 token on Ethereum are the example. In everyday talk, people often call all of them coins. The same name does not mean they were issued the same way. Think it through What is the main difference between ETH and an ERC-20 token on Ethereum? A. There is no difference. Being on Ethereum makes them the same B. ETH belongs to the network itself. An ERC-20 token is issued on a network that already exists C. ERC-20 is Ethereum's native coin"}, {"title": "What is a public chain?", "html": "<p><strong>A public chain is a blockchain anyone can use, and you cannot send between chains as if they were the same place.</strong></p>\n<p>A public chain is usually an open blockchain network, for example Bitcoin, Ethereum, TRON, BNB Chain, and Solana.<br />\nFees, speed, apps, and security assumptions differ from chain to chain.</p>\n<p>You can think of different networks as different transport systems.<br />\nAn address that looks the same does not mean the destination and the asset are compatible.<br />\nA transfer needs the recipient to explicitly support that network.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A similar-looking address does not mean the network and the asset are compatible. The recipient has to explicitly support that network.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Do not send just because the address looks similar, or because both sides are called public chains. Confirm the network the recipient supports first.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>If an address looks the same, can you send it straight onto another chain?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. If the address looks the same, you can send</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Not by default. The recipient has to explicitly support that network</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. If both are called public chains, they can send to each other</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A public chain is a blockchain anyone can use, and you cannot send between chains as if they were the same place. A public chain is usually an open blockchain network, for example Bitcoin, Ethereum, TRON, BNB Chain, and Solana. Fees, speed, apps, and security assumptions differ from chain to chain. You can think of different networks as different transport systems. An address that looks the same does not mean the destination and the asset are compatible. A transfer needs the recipient to explicitly support that network. Think it through If an address looks the same, can you send it straight onto another chain? A. Yes. If the address looks the same, you can send B. Not by default. The recipient has to explicitly support that network C. If both are called public chains, they can send to each other"}, {"title": "What is Web3?", "html": "<p><strong>Web3 means internet apps you sign in to with a wallet and use on a chain.</strong></p>\n<p>Web3 is often used for internet apps built with blockchains, wallets, and smart contracts.<br />\nA user can manage digital assets, sign actions, and use on-chain apps through a wallet.</p>\n<p>But a Web3 label does not mean the project is actually decentralized.<br />\nIt also does not mean the app is usable or safe.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Web3 is only a label. It does not guarantee decentralization, and it does not guarantee that something is usable or safe.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. A Web3 label, or a wallet sign-in, does not by itself prove safety or decentralization.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>If an app is labeled Web3, does that mean it is already decentralized and safe?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. The label is the proof</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. The label does not guarantee decentralization, usability, or safety</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. If you can sign in with a wallet, it is definitely safe</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Web3 means internet apps you sign in to with a wallet and use on a chain. Web3 is often used for internet apps built with blockchains, wallets, and smart contracts. A user can manage digital assets, sign actions, and use on-chain apps through a wallet. But a Web3 label does not mean the project is actually decentralized. It also does not mean the app is usable or safe. Think it through If an app is labeled Web3, does that mean it is already decentralized and safe? A. Yes. The label is the proof B. No. The label does not guarantee decentralization, usability, or safety C. If you can sign in with a wallet, it is definitely safe"}, {"title": "What is a smart contract?", "html": "<p><strong>A smart contract is a program on a chain that runs by its code, and if the code has a bug, things can go wrong.</strong></p>\n<p>A smart contract is a program deployed on a blockchain that executes by its code.<br />\nSwaps, lending, and token issuance are often done by one.</p>\n<p>Code can have bugs, and it can also be given admin permissions.<br />\nDo not assume it is reliable just because it is called smart.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. It is a program that runs by its code. The code can have bugs, and it may also have admin permissions.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Calling it smart does not mean it cannot fail. A swap contract and other contracts can both have bugs or admin permissions.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Can a smart contract be treated as something that will not go wrong?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes, because it is called smart</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. The code can have bugs, and it may also have admin permissions</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Only swap contracts can go wrong</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A smart contract is a program on a chain that runs by its code, and if the code has a bug, things can go wrong. A smart contract is a program deployed on a blockchain that executes by its code. Swaps, lending, and token issuance are often done by one. Code can have bugs, and it can also be given admin permissions. Do not assume it is reliable just because it is called smart. Think it through Can a smart contract be treated as something that will not go wrong? A. Yes, because it is called smart B. No. The code can have bugs, and it may also have admin permissions C. Only swap contracts can go wrong"}, {"title": "What is a stablecoin?", "html": "<p><strong>A stablecoin aims to stay 1:1 with the dollar, but it is not a bank deposit, and it can lose that peg.</strong></p>\n<p>A stablecoin aims to track a reference value, often 1 US dollar, for example USDT and USDC.<br />\nDifferent stablecoins may use fiat reserves, crypto collateral, or other mechanisms.</p>\n<p>A stablecoin can be used for pricing, transfers, and settling trades.<br />\nIt is not a bank deposit, and it is not guaranteed to stay redeemable at 1 dollar.<br />\nYou need to understand the reserves, the redemption terms, freeze permissions, and the risk of losing the peg.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. It aims to track a reference value, such as 1 dollar. It is not a bank deposit, and it can lose the peg.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. USD in the name does not mean it is a bank deposit, or that it will always equal 1 dollar.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Is a stablecoin a bank deposit, and is it guaranteed to always equal 1 US dollar?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes, so it can be treated as a deposit</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. It aims to track a reference value, but it is not a bank deposit, and it can lose the peg</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. If the name contains USD, it always equals 1 dollar</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A stablecoin aims to stay 1:1 with the dollar, but it is not a bank deposit, and it can lose that peg. A stablecoin aims to track a reference value, often 1 US dollar, for example USDT and USDC. Different stablecoins may use fiat reserves, crypto collateral, or other mechanisms. A stablecoin can be used for pricing, transfers, and settling trades. It is not a bank deposit, and it is not guaranteed to stay redeemable at 1 dollar. You need to understand the reserves, the redemption terms, freeze permissions, and the risk of losing the peg. Think it through Is a stablecoin a bank deposit, and is it guaranteed to always equal 1 US dollar? A. Yes, so it can be treated as a deposit B. No. It aims to track a reference value, but it is not a bank deposit, and it can lose the peg C. If the name contains USD, it always equals 1 dollar"}, {"title": "One table of how the pieces fit", "html": "<p><strong>One table: what the chain, the coin, the wallet, the exchange, and the app each do.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th>Main job</th>\n<th>Simple example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Blockchain</td>\n<td>Records and carries out network activity</td>\n<td>Ethereum, TRON</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coin / token</td>\n<td>A digital asset inside a network</td>\n<td>BTC, ETH, USDT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wallet</td>\n<td>Manages the account and signs actions</td>\n<td>Software wallet, hardware wallet</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exchange</td>\n<td>Buys, sells, or swaps assets</td>\n<td>CEX, DEX</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DApp</td>\n<td>An application on a blockchain</td>\n<td>Swap, lending, or game apps</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>A wallet manages the account and the signing.<br />\nIt does not keep the blockchain's books, and it does not guarantee the price on an exchange.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A wallet mainly manages the account, and it is what you use to sign actions.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Keeping the books is the blockchain network's job. An exchange price is not guaranteed by the wallet. The wallet manages the account and the signing.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>What does a wallet mainly do?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Keep the books in place of the blockchain</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Manage the account, and sign actions</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Guarantee that the price on an exchange will not change</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "One table: what the chain, the coin, the wallet, the exchange, and the app each do. Name Main job Simple example Blockchain Records and carries out network activity Ethereum, TRON Coin / token A digital asset inside a network BTC, ETH, USDT Wallet Manages the account and signs actions Software wallet, hardware wallet Exchange Buys, sells, or swaps assets CEX, DEX DApp An application on a blockchain Swap, lending, or game apps A wallet manages the account and the signing. It does not keep the blockchain's books, and it does not guarantee the price on an exchange. Think it through What does a wallet mainly do? A. Keep the books in place of the blockchain B. Manage the account, and sign actions C. Guarantee that the price on an exchange will not change"}, {"title": "Why do prices move?", "html": "<p><strong>The price is set by buyers and sellers; a rise does not mean it is worth more, and a drop does not mean it is cheaper.</strong></p>\n<p>The trading price is formed by buying and selling in the market.<br />\nIt can be moved by demand, liquidity, expectations, changes in supply, leverage, the broader economy, and news.</p>\n<p>A rising price does not necessarily mean the project is more valuable.<br />\nA falling price does not automatically prove that it is cheap.</p>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. The price is formed by buying and selling. A rise does not mean the project is more valuable, and a drop does not automatically mean it is cheap.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. A price move by itself cannot prove that a project is more valuable, or that it is already cheap.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>If the price rises, does that mean the project is more valuable?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. If it rose, it is more valuable</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Not necessarily. The price is formed by buying and selling, and a rise does not mean the project is more valuable</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Only a drop can show that it has no value</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "The price is set by buyers and sellers; a rise does not mean it is worth more, and a drop does not mean it is cheaper. The trading price is formed by buying and selling in the market. It can be moved by demand, liquidity, expectations, changes in supply, leverage, the broader economy, and news. A rising price does not necessarily mean the project is more valuable. A falling price does not automatically prove that it is cheap. Note: Look at what it is and why it exists before you decide to take part. Think it through If the price rises, does that mean the project is more valuable? A. Yes. If it rose, it is more valuable B. Not necessarily. The price is formed by buying and selling, and a rise does not mean the project is more valuable C. Only a drop can show that it has no value"}]}, {"id": "02", "title": "Primary and secondary markets", "emoji": "🌱", "intro": "Are you joining an issuance, or trading coins already out?", "tags": ["Basics"], "tip": "Note: First say which asset you are buying, and who issued it, before you talk about primary or secondary.", "sections": [{"title": "Primary market: issuance and early funding", "html": "<p><strong>The primary market is the subscription when a coin is first issued, and early does not mean easier money.</strong></p>\n<p>The primary market usually means the first issuance, the funding, or the subscription.<br />\nIn crypto, common forms include private sales, public sales, token issuances, and some Launchpad events.<br />\nThe exact category depends on what the participant is buying, who the seller is, and how the deal is structured.</p>\n<p>Fictional teaching example: a project opens a token subscription to participants for the first time, and the participants receive the tokens from the issuer.<br />\nThat is usually a primary issuance.<br />\nIt is not a real project, and it does not mean taking part this way will make money.</p>\n<p>Early participation can mean a longer lockup, less public information, and a harder exit.<br />\n\"Early\" only means earlier in time. It does not mean easier money.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Early only means earlier in time. It can also mean a longer lockup, less information, and a harder exit.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Early does not mean easier money. A private sale is not more profitable just because it is earlier.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Does \"early\" mean it is easier to make money?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. The earlier it is, the easier it is to profit</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. Early only means earlier in time, and the exit can be harder</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Only a private sale is easier money because it is earlier</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "The primary market is the subscription when a coin is first issued, and early does not mean easier money. The primary market usually means the first issuance, the funding, or the subscription. In crypto, common forms include private sales, public sales, token issuances, and some Launchpad events. The exact category depends on what the participant is buying, who the seller is, and how the deal is structured. Fictional teaching example: a project opens a token subscription to participants for the first time, and the participants receive the tokens from the issuer. That is usually a primary issuance. It is not a real project, and it does not mean taking part this way will make money. Early participation can mean a longer lockup, less public information, and a harder exit. \"Early\" only means earlier in time. It does not mean easier money. Think it through Does \"early\" mean it is easier to make money? A. Yes. The earlier it is, the easier it is to profit B. No. Early only means earlier in time, and the exit can be harder C. Only a private sale is easier money because it is earlier"}, {"title": "Secondary market: trading coins already issued", "html": "<p><strong>The secondary market is buying and selling coins that someone else already holds.</strong></p>\n<p>The secondary market usually means assets that have already been issued, moving between holders.<br />\nBuying BTC that someone else is selling on an exchange, or swapping an already issued token on a DEX, is generally a secondary trade.</p>\n<p>So primary / secondary describes issuance and later circulation.<br />\nCEX / DEX describes the venue and the mechanism.<br />\nThey are not the same classification.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Buying BTC that someone else already holds is generally a secondary trade. It does not become a primary issuance just because it happens on a CEX.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Primary is the issuance step. Buying already issued BTC on an exchange is generally a secondary trade. CEX names the venue, not primary or secondary.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Buying BTC that someone else is selling on an exchange is generally which kind?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. A primary issuance</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. A secondary trade</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Unrelated to primary or secondary. It only matters whether it is a CEX</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "The secondary market is buying and selling coins that someone else already holds. The secondary market usually means assets that have already been issued, moving between holders. Buying BTC that someone else is selling on an exchange, or swapping an already issued token on a DEX, is generally a secondary trade. So primary / secondary describes issuance and later circulation. CEX / DEX describes the venue and the mechanism. They are not the same classification. Think it through Buying BTC that someone else is selling on an exchange is generally which kind? A. A primary issuance B. A secondary trade C. Unrelated to primary or secondary. It only matters whether it is a CEX"}, {"title": "Common ways to take part early", "html": "<p><strong>Private sales, IDOs, airdrops, and testnets are all early ways to take part, and each has its own trap.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Way</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Check carefully</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Private sale</td>\n<td>Raising money from specific participants</td>\n<td>Eligibility, the contract, the price, the lockup, and delivery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Public sale</td>\n<td>A raise open to people who qualify</td>\n<td>Regional limits, subscription rules, and the issuance plan</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IDO</td>\n<td>An issuance organized through a decentralized platform</td>\n<td>Whether it is genuine, the contract, liquidity, and the allocation rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Launchpad</td>\n<td>A project issuance organized by a platform</td>\n<td>Platform rules, the cost of taking part, the allocation, and the unlock</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Airdrop</td>\n<td>Assets allocated to addresses or users who qualify</td>\n<td>The conditions, the cost of claiming, and approval risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Testnet</td>\n<td>Trying a product on a test network</td>\n<td>Test assets are usually not real assets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Points campaign</td>\n<td>A points system that records user activity</td>\n<td>Whether points can be redeemed, and whether the rules can change</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>An airdrop, points, and a testnet are not automatically a primary investment.<br />\nThey also do not guarantee a future token or a reward.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A testnet is for trying the product. Test assets are usually not real assets.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Test assets usually cannot be treated as real assets. A high points score does not mean the test assets have become real assets.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Are test assets on a testnet usually the same as real assets?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. Once the test passes, you can use them as real assets</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Usually not</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. If the points are high enough, they are real assets</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Private sales, IDOs, airdrops, and testnets are all early ways to take part, and each has its own trap. Way Meaning Check carefully Private sale Raising money from specific participants Eligibility, the contract, the price, the lockup, and delivery Public sale A raise open to people who qualify Regional limits, subscription rules, and the issuance plan IDO An issuance organized through a decentralized platform Whether it is genuine, the contract, liquidity, and the allocation rules Launchpad A project issuance organized by a platform Platform rules, the cost of taking part, the allocation, and the unlock Airdrop Assets allocated to addresses or users who qualify The conditions, the cost of claiming, and approval risk Testnet Trying a product on a test network Test assets are usually not real assets Points campaign A points system that records user activity Whether points can be redeemed, and whether the rules can change An airdrop, points, and a testnet are not automatically a primary investment. They also do not guarantee a future token or a reward. Think it through Are test assets on a testnet usually the same as real assets? A. Yes. Once the test passes, you can use them as real assets B. Usually not C. If the points are high enough, they are real assets"}, {"title": "What are lockups and unlocks?", "html": "<p><strong>A lockup means you cannot sell for a while, and after the unlock, more of the coin can be sold.</strong></p>\n<p>A lockup means the asset cannot be transferred or withdrawn for a time, under certain conditions.<br />\nAn unlock means the restriction is lifted on a schedule.<br />\nAn early subscription may be delivered in batches. Finishing the subscription does not mean you can sell immediately.</p>\n<p>An unlock changes the supply that can circulate.<br />\nThe actual effect on price still depends on what holders do, on demand, and on market liquidity.<br />\nAn unlock does not mean the price will definitely rise.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. During a lockup you may be unable to transfer for a while. Finishing the subscription does not mean you can sell right away.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Finishing the subscription does not mean you can sell immediately. An unlock increases the amount that can be sold, but it does not guarantee a higher price.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Once the subscription is finished, can you sell immediately?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. Once you have paid, you can sell</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Not necessarily. During a lockup you may be unable to transfer for a while</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. After the unlock, the price definitely rises</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A lockup means you cannot sell for a while, and after the unlock, more of the coin can be sold. A lockup means the asset cannot be transferred or withdrawn for a time, under certain conditions. An unlock means the restriction is lifted on a schedule. An early subscription may be delivered in batches. Finishing the subscription does not mean you can sell immediately. An unlock changes the supply that can circulate. The actual effect on price still depends on what holders do, on demand, and on market liquidity. An unlock does not mean the price will definitely rise. Think it through Once the subscription is finished, can you sell immediately? A. Yes. Once you have paid, you can sell B. Not necessarily. During a lockup you may be unable to transfer for a while C. After the unlock, the price definitely rises"}, {"title": "CEX and DEX", "html": "<p><strong>CEX / DEX is about where you trade, and it is not the same thing as a primary or secondary market.</strong></p>\n<p>CEX and DEX classify the venue. They are not the primary / secondary distinction.<br />\nThe difference is in <a href=\"#/c/05\">Chapter 05, &quot;How is a CEX different from a DEX?&quot;</a>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. CEX and DEX distinguish the venue and the mechanism. Primary and secondary distinguish issuance and later circulation.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. CEX / DEX is not primary versus secondary, and it is not coin versus token. It says where you trade, and by what mechanism.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>What do the words CEX and DEX mainly distinguish?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. The primary market and the secondary market</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. The venue and the mechanism</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. A native coin and a token</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "CEX / DEX is about where you trade, and it is not the same thing as a primary or secondary market. CEX and DEX classify the venue. They are not the primary / secondary distinction. The difference is in Chapter 05, \"How is a CEX different from a DEX?\" . Note: First say which asset you are buying, and who issued it, before you talk about primary or secondary. Think it through What do the words CEX and DEX mainly distinguish? A. The primary market and the secondary market B. The venue and the mechanism C. A native coin and a token"}]}, {"id": "03", "title": "What to know before you buy", "emoji": "📚", "intro": "Price, market cap, liquidity, and fees", "tags": ["Basics"], "sections": [{"title": "Trading pairs", "html": "<p>BTC/USDT prices the BTC on the left in the USDT on the right; you can buy a fraction, and a sale usually pays you the quote asset.</p>\n<p>You see \"BTC/USDT: 60,000\" on an exchange and wonder whether you must buy a whole BTC.</p>\n<p>You do not.<br />\n<strong>BTC/USDT means the BTC is priced in USDT.</strong><br />\nThe BTC on the left is the asset you buy or sell, and the USDT on the right is the quote asset.<br />\nFictional teaching example: 60,000 means that, at this quote, 1 BTC is about 60,000 USDT.</p>\n<p>Fictional teaching example: suppose you only want 0.01 BTC.<br />\n60,000 × 0.01 = <strong>600 USDT</strong>, before fees.<br />\nA sale reverses the direction: you sell BTC and usually receive USDT.<br />\nExchanges often allow a fractional amount, but they set a minimum order size or value.</p>\n<p><strong>Easy to mix up:</strong> BTC/USDT and BTC/USD are not necessarily the same market;<br />\nUSDT is a stablecoin, not dollars in a bank account.<br />\nBefore you trade, look at the quote asset on the right.</p>\n<div class=\"ch03-lab\" data-lab=\"pair\">\n<h3>Split the pair</h3>\n<p class=\"lab-kicker\">Fictional teaching example. BTC/USDT uses 60,000, ETH/BTC uses 0.05, and SOL/USDT uses 150. None of these is a live price.</p>\n<div class=\"lab-tabs\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Choose a pair\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-pair=\"BTC/USDT\" data-base=\"BTC\" data-quote=\"USDT\" data-price=\"60000\" aria-pressed=\"true\">BTC/USDT</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-pair=\"ETH/BTC\" data-base=\"ETH\" data-quote=\"BTC\" data-price=\"0.05\" aria-pressed=\"false\">ETH/BTC</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-pair=\"SOL/USDT\" data-base=\"SOL\" data-quote=\"USDT\" data-price=\"150\" aria-pressed=\"false\">SOL/USDT</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"lab-cols\">\n<div><span>Asset traded</span><strong data-base>BTC</strong></div>\n<div><span>Quote asset</span><strong data-quote>USDT</strong></div>\n</div>\n<label data-qty-wrap>Quantity to buy (<span data-unit>BTC</span>)\n<input data-qty type=\"number\" min=\"0\" step=\"any\" value=\"0.01\" inputmode=\"decimal\">\n</label>\n<p class=\"lab-result\" aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"lab-reset\" data-reset>Reset</button>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. In ETH/BTC, buying ETH usually spends the BTC on the right.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Look at the quote asset on the right of the pair.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>In the ETH/BTC pair, what do you usually spend to buy ETH?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\" data-note=\"Not quite. Look at the quote asset on the right. The quote asset of ETH/BTC is BTC, not USDT.\">A. USDT</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. BTC</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\" data-note=\"Not quite. ETH is the asset you receive, not the quote asset you spend.\">C. ETH</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "BTC/USDT prices the BTC on the left in the USDT on the right; you can buy a fraction, and a sale usually pays you the quote asset. You see \"BTC/USDT: 60,000\" on an exchange and wonder whether you must buy a whole BTC. You do not. BTC/USDT means the BTC is priced in USDT. The BTC on the left is the asset you buy or sell, and the USDT on the right is the quote asset. Fictional teaching example: 60,000 means that, at this quote, 1 BTC is about 60,000 USDT. Fictional teaching example: suppose you only want 0.01 BTC. 60,000 × 0.01 = 600 USDT , before fees. A sale reverses the direction: you sell BTC and usually receive USDT. Exchanges often allow a fractional amount, but they set a minimum order size or value. Easy to mix up: BTC/USDT and BTC/USD are not necessarily the same market; USDT is a stablecoin, not dollars in a bank account. Before you trade, look at the quote asset on the right. Split the pair Fictional teaching example. BTC/USDT uses 60,000, ETH/BTC uses 0.05, and SOL/USDT uses 150. None of these is a live price. BTC/USDT ETH/BTC SOL/USDT Asset traded BTC Quote asset USDT Quantity to buy ( BTC ) Reset Think it through In the ETH/BTC pair, what do you usually spend to buy ETH? A. USDT B. BTC C. ETH"}, {"title": "Price, market cap, and FDV", "html": "<p>A low unit price is not the same as cheap; look at circulating market cap and fully diluted valuation together, and neither number is the cash people actually put in.</p>\n<p>Coin A sells for 0.01 dollars, and coin B sells for 100 dollars.<br />\nWhich one is cheaper?<br />\n<strong>The unit price alone cannot tell you.</strong></p>\n<p>Start with market cap.<br />\nA common formula is: <strong>circulating market cap = current price × circulating supply.</strong></p>\n<p>Fictional teaching example: coin A has 10 billion coins in circulation.<br />\n0.01 × 10 billion = <strong>100 million dollars</strong>.<br />\nCoin B has 100,000 coins in circulation.<br />\n100 × 100,000 = <strong>10 million dollars</strong>.<br />\nA has the lower unit price, and its circulating market cap is ten times B's.</p>\n<p>Then look at <strong>FDV (fully diluted valuation)</strong>: it usually multiplies the current price by total supply or max supply, and the data site decides the exact definition.<br />\nFictional teaching example: suppose coin A now has 10 billion circulating, and the future total supply might reach 100 billion. At 0.01 dollars, the FDV is about <strong>1 billion dollars</strong>.<br />\nThat is a valuation measure. <strong>It does not mean 1 billion dollars actually flowed into the project.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Easy to mix up:</strong> market cap is not \"the total cash every holder put in\";<br />\nFDV is not a price or a size the project is guaranteed to reach.</p>\n<div class=\"ch03-lab\" data-lab=\"mcap\">\n<h3>The cheap-price test</h3>\n<p class=\"lab-kicker\">Fictional teaching example. Guess which circulating market cap is higher, and the tap shows the math. The units are dollars and coins.</p>\n<div class=\"lab-stats\">\n<div><span>A: unit price 0.01 dollars</span><b>Circulating 10 billion</b><span>Total 100 billion</span></div>\n<div><span>B: unit price 100 dollars</span><b>Circulating 100,000</b><span>Total 200,000</span></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Which circulating market cap is higher\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-guess=\"a\" aria-pressed=\"false\">A has the higher market cap</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-guess=\"b\" aria-pressed=\"false\">B has the higher market cap</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-guess=\"tie\" aria-pressed=\"false\">They are the same</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-feedback\" hidden aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"lab-reset\" data-reset>Reset</button>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A lower unit price is not the same as undervalued. Also look at supply, use, and risk.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Do not look only at the unit price, and do not look only at FDV.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>If a coin's unit price falls to 0.001 dollars, is it certainly undervalued?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\" data-note=\"Not quite. That treats the price as the valuation.\">A. Yes</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Not necessarily. Also look at supply, use, and risk</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\" data-note=\"Not quite. Looking only at FDV ignores demand and what is actually circulating.\">C. FDV alone is enough</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A low unit price is not the same as cheap; look at circulating market cap and fully diluted valuation together, and neither number is the cash people actually put in. Coin A sells for 0.01 dollars, and coin B sells for 100 dollars. Which one is cheaper? The unit price alone cannot tell you. Start with market cap. A common formula is: circulating market cap = current price × circulating supply. Fictional teaching example: coin A has 10 billion coins in circulation. 0.01 × 10 billion = 100 million dollars . Coin B has 100,000 coins in circulation. 100 × 100,000 = 10 million dollars . A has the lower unit price, and its circulating market cap is ten times B's. Then look at FDV (fully diluted valuation) : it usually multiplies the current price by total supply or max supply, and the data site decides the exact definition. Fictional teaching example: suppose coin A now has 10 billion circulating, and the future total supply might reach 100 billion. At 0.01 dollars, the FDV is about 1 billion dollars . That is a valuation measure. It does not mean 1 billion dollars actually flowed into the project. Easy to mix up: market cap is not \"the total cash every holder put in\"; FDV is not a price or a size the project is guaranteed to reach. The cheap-price test Fictional teaching example. Guess which circulating market cap is higher, and the tap shows the math. The units are dollars and coins. A: unit price 0.01 dollars Circulating 10 billion Total 100 billion B: unit price 100 dollars Circulating 100,000 Total 200,000 A has the higher market cap B has the higher market cap They are the same Reset Think it through If a coin's unit price falls to 0.001 dollars, is it certainly undervalued? A. Yes B. Not necessarily. Also look at supply, use, and risk C. FDV alone is enough"}, {"title": "Supply", "html": "<p>Separate circulating supply, total supply, and max supply; an unlock changes whether coins can be sold, and issuance is what adds new coins to supply.</p>\n<p>Imagine a cake cut into 100 slices. You hold 1 slice, which is 1%.<br />\nThe project rules later add another 100 slices, and you still hold 1, so your share becomes 0.5%.</p>\n<p>Tokens also change in quantity, and three ideas have to stay separate:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Circulating supply:</strong> by a data site's definition, the amount currently counted as circulating in the market.</li>\n<li><strong>Total supply:</strong> usually the issued amount after some burned amount is removed, and the definition can differ.</li>\n<li><strong>Max supply:</strong> if the protocol sets a cap, that is the most that can be issued;<br />\nsome coins have no fixed cap.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Also separate <strong>issuance</strong> and <strong>unlock</strong>.<br />\nIssuance puts new tokens into the supply;<br />\nan unlock usually removes a transfer restriction from tokens that were already allocated or issued.<br />\nBoth can change how much can be sold, and they are not the same event.</p>\n<p><strong>What you need to check is not simply \"how many coins exist.\" Who can issue more? Whose coins unlock, and when? Who might they sell to after the unlock?</strong></p>\n<div class=\"ch03-lab\" data-lab=\"supply\">\n<h3>Supply timeline</h3>\n<p class=\"lab-kicker\">Fictional teaching example. The two buttons can be tapped in either order. Reset to start again. Max supply is 100 million coins.</p>\n<div class=\"lab-stats\">\n<div><span>Circulating now</span><b data-circ>20 million</b></div>\n<div><span>Still locked</span><b data-locked>30 million</b></div>\n<div><span>Issued total</span><b data-issued>50 million</b></div>\n<div><span>Max supply</span><b>100 million</b></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-unlock>Unlock 5 million</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-mint>Issue 10 million into circulation</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-result\" hidden aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"lab-reset\" data-reset>Reset</button>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. An unlock can change only what is circulating. Issued supply does not have to increase.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. An unlock is not a new issue, and it does not guarantee a price drop.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>After 5 million locked coins unlock, must issued supply rise by 5 million?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\" data-note=\"Not quite. That mixes issuance and unlock into one event.\">A. It must</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Not necessarily. It may only change what can circulate</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\" data-note=\"Not quite. A supply change is not a price guarantee.\">C. The price must fall</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Separate circulating supply, total supply, and max supply; an unlock changes whether coins can be sold, and issuance is what adds new coins to supply. Imagine a cake cut into 100 slices. You hold 1 slice, which is 1%. The project rules later add another 100 slices, and you still hold 1, so your share becomes 0.5%. Tokens also change in quantity, and three ideas have to stay separate: Circulating supply: by a data site's definition, the amount currently counted as circulating in the market. Total supply: usually the issued amount after some burned amount is removed, and the definition can differ. Max supply: if the protocol sets a cap, that is the most that can be issued; some coins have no fixed cap. Also separate issuance and unlock . Issuance puts new tokens into the supply; an unlock usually removes a transfer restriction from tokens that were already allocated or issued. Both can change how much can be sold, and they are not the same event. What you need to check is not simply \"how many coins exist.\" Who can issue more? Whose coins unlock, and when? Who might they sell to after the unlock? Supply timeline Fictional teaching example. The two buttons can be tapped in either order. Reset to start again. Max supply is 100 million coins. Circulating now 20 million Still locked 30 million Issued total 50 million Max supply 100 million Unlock 5 million Issue 10 million into circulation Reset Think it through After 5 million locked coins unlock, must issued supply rise by 5 million? A. It must B. Not necessarily. It may only change what can circulate C. The price must fall"}, {"title": "Liquidity and volume", "html": "<p>Volume is how much already traded, and liquidity is whether a large order now would punch through the price.</p>\n<p>You want to sell 50,000 dollars of a coin, and the page says \"24-hour volume 100 million dollars,\" which sounds busy.<br />\nYou open the book and find very few people willing to take your sell order right now.</p>\n<p><strong>Volume is how much traded over a past period; liquidity is whether you can trade now without moving the price much.</strong><br />\nThe two numbers are related, and they are not the same thing.</p>\n<p>Fictional teaching example: a coin traded 100 million dollars over the last 24 hours, but only 2,000 dollars of bids sit near the best bid.<br />\nA market sell of 50,000 dollars may walk down into cheaper bids, and the average fill can be far from the price on the screen.</p>\n<p>Some pairs can also show wash trading or unreliable volume statistics.<br />\nA large volume does not mean the book is deep enough.</p>\n<div class=\"ch03-lab\" data-lab=\"book\">\n<h3>Can the book take the order?</h3>\n<p class=\"lab-kicker\">Fictional teaching example. This is not a live order book. Tap a size and see the proceeds and the average price at once.</p>\n<ul class=\"lab-book\">\n<li data-px=\"1\"><span>1.00 dollars</span><span>1,000 coins</span></li>\n<li data-px=\"0.98\"><span>0.98 dollars</span><span>2,000 coins</span></li>\n<li data-px=\"0.95\"><span>0.95 dollars</span><span>5,000 coins</span></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Choose a sell size\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-qty=\"500\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Sell 500</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-qty=\"2500\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Sell 2,500</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-qty=\"6000\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Sell 6,000</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-result\" hidden aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"lab-reset\" data-reset>Reset</button>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"c\" data-ok=\"Correct. Whether a large order can fill depends on the current book depth and how much it can take.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Yesterday's rise and a fan count cannot replace the bids and asks that are there now.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>What should you look at first to judge whether a large order can fill easily?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\" data-note=\"Not quite. Yesterday's rise cannot replace the depth of the book right now.\">A. Yesterday's rise</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\" data-note=\"Not quite. A fan count cannot replace the depth of the book right now.\">B. The project's fan count</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Current book depth and how much it can take</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Volume is how much already traded, and liquidity is whether a large order now would punch through the price. You want to sell 50,000 dollars of a coin, and the page says \"24-hour volume 100 million dollars,\" which sounds busy. You open the book and find very few people willing to take your sell order right now. Volume is how much traded over a past period; liquidity is whether you can trade now without moving the price much. The two numbers are related, and they are not the same thing. Fictional teaching example: a coin traded 100 million dollars over the last 24 hours, but only 2,000 dollars of bids sit near the best bid. A market sell of 50,000 dollars may walk down into cheaper bids, and the average fill can be far from the price on the screen. Some pairs can also show wash trading or unreliable volume statistics. A large volume does not mean the book is deep enough. Can the book take the order? Fictional teaching example. This is not a live order book. Tap a size and see the proceeds and the average price at once. 1.00 dollars 1,000 coins 0.98 dollars 2,000 coins 0.95 dollars 5,000 coins Sell 500 Sell 2,500 Sell 6,000 Reset Think it through What should you look at first to judge whether a large order can fill easily? A. Yesterday's rise B. The project's fan count C. Current book depth and how much it can take"}, {"title": "Spread, price impact, and slippage", "html": "<p>The spread, price impact, and slippage are three related but different things, and the price on the screen is not necessarily your fill.</p>\n<p>On the trade page, the best bid might be 0.99 dollars and the best ask 1.00 dollars.<br />\nThe 0.01 dollar gap is the <strong>bid-ask spread</strong>.</p>\n<p>If you want to buy a lot, the cheapest ask is not enough, so you keep buying the next, more expensive level.<br />\nThat change in fill price, caused by order size and book depth, is <strong>price impact</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Slippage</strong> is the gap between the price you expected and the price you actually got. It can come from the market moving, thin liquidity, and other causes.<br />\nThe three are related, and they are not one word.</p>\n<p>Fictional teaching example: the ask book has two levels, 100 coins at 1.00 dollars and 100 coins at 1.05 dollars.<br />\nA market buy of 150 coins costs 100 × 1.00 + 50 × 1.05 = <strong>152.50 dollars</strong>, about <strong>1.0167 dollars</strong> on average, not 1 dollar for every coin.</p>\n<div class=\"ch03-lab\" data-lab=\"sweep\">\n<h3>A market order walking the book</h3>\n<p class=\"lab-kicker\">Fictional teaching example. Tap a size and the asks that get taken light up. The gap is not \"all slippage.\"</p>\n<ul class=\"lab-book\">\n<li data-px=\"1\"><span>1.00 dollars</span><span>100 coins</span></li>\n<li data-px=\"1.05\"><span>1.05 dollars</span><span>100 coins</span></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Choose a buy size\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-qty=\"50\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Buy 50</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-qty=\"100\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Buy 100</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-qty=\"150\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Buy 150</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-qty=\"200\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Buy 200</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-result\" hidden aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"lab-reset\" data-reset>Reset</button>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. A limit buy has a maximum price. If the book is not deep enough, it may fill only in part, or not yet.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. A limit does not guarantee an immediate full fill, and it will not raise your price to 1.05 by itself.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>A limit buy is set at a maximum of 1.00 dollars. Does that guarantee you immediately buy 150 coins?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. No. It may fill only in part, or keep waiting</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\" data-note=\"Not quite. That ignores the depth of the book.\">B. Yes, it is guaranteed</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\" data-note=\"Not quite. A limit buy will not raise the price to 1.05 by itself. That would break the maximum you set.\">C. It can automatically raise the price to 1.05</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "The spread, price impact, and slippage are three related but different things, and the price on the screen is not necessarily your fill. On the trade page, the best bid might be 0.99 dollars and the best ask 1.00 dollars. The 0.01 dollar gap is the bid-ask spread . If you want to buy a lot, the cheapest ask is not enough, so you keep buying the next, more expensive level. That change in fill price, caused by order size and book depth, is price impact . Slippage is the gap between the price you expected and the price you actually got. It can come from the market moving, thin liquidity, and other causes. The three are related, and they are not one word. Fictional teaching example: the ask book has two levels, 100 coins at 1.00 dollars and 100 coins at 1.05 dollars. A market buy of 150 coins costs 100 × 1.00 + 50 × 1.05 = 152.50 dollars , about 1.0167 dollars on average, not 1 dollar for every coin. A market order walking the book Fictional teaching example. Tap a size and the asks that get taken light up. The gap is not \"all slippage.\" 1.00 dollars 100 coins 1.05 dollars 100 coins Buy 50 Buy 100 Buy 150 Buy 200 Reset Think it through A limit buy is set at a maximum of 1.00 dollars. Does that guarantee you immediately buy 150 coins? A. No. It may fill only in part, or keep waiting B. Yes, it is guaranteed C. It can automatically raise the price to 1.05"}, {"title": "Trading fees and network fees", "html": "<p>The price can stay still while fees, the spread, slippage, a withdrawal fee, and network fees still eat the balance a little at a time.</p>\n<p>What beginners most often miss is not the rise or the fall. It is the cost of each step.</p>\n<p>Buying can include a payment-channel quote gap and a trading fee;<br />\ntrading on an exchange can include the spread and slippage;<br />\nwithdrawing from an exchange can include a withdrawal fee;<br />\nan on-chain approval or swap can also create a network fee or a protocol fee.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>an exchange withdrawal fee is not necessarily the on-chain network fee</strong>.<br />\nAn internal transfer on the platform is usually not an on-chain transfer, and the fee structure is different.</p>\n<p>Here is a <strong>purely fictional teaching</strong> calculation:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Assumed fee</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Value left</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Start</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">—</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">100 USDT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quote gap on the buy</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">1 USDT</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">99 USDT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Trading fee and slippage</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.5 USDT</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">98.5 USDT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exchange withdrawal fee</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">1 USDT</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">97.5 USDT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>On-chain approval and swap network fee</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">1.5 USDT</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">96 USDT</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Fictional teaching example: the different fees are all converted into a USDT equivalent so you can practice the cost.<br />\nIn real life the network fee is often paid in the chain's native coin, and asset value can also move with the market.</p>\n<div class=\"ch03-lab\" data-lab=\"fees\">\n<h3>Peel the costs</h3>\n<p class=\"lab-kicker\">Fictional teaching example. Start at 100 USDT. Turn an item on and the balance changes at once. All four on leaves 96 USDT, a 4% cost.</p>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Turn a fee on or off\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-fee=\"1\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Quote gap 1</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-fee=\"0.5\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Trade fee and slippage 0.5</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-fee=\"1\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Withdrawal fee 1</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-fee=\"1.5\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Network fee 1.5</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-result\" aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"lab-reset\" data-reset>Reset</button>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Moving from the funding account to the spot account on the same platform is usually an internal book entry, not an on-chain transfer.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. An internal transfer is usually not an on-chain transfer, and it is not a cross-chain transfer.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>If you move funds from the funding account to the spot account inside an exchange, must you pay a blockchain network fee?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\" data-note=\"Not quite. An internal transfer and an on-chain transfer are not the same thing.\">A. You must</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Not necessarily. It is usually an internal book entry</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\" data-note=\"Not quite. A transfer between accounts on the same platform is usually not cross-chain either.\">C. It must create a cross-chain fee</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "The price can stay still while fees, the spread, slippage, a withdrawal fee, and network fees still eat the balance a little at a time. What beginners most often miss is not the rise or the fall. It is the cost of each step. Buying can include a payment-channel quote gap and a trading fee; trading on an exchange can include the spread and slippage; withdrawing from an exchange can include a withdrawal fee; an on-chain approval or swap can also create a network fee or a protocol fee. Note: an exchange withdrawal fee is not necessarily the on-chain network fee . An internal transfer on the platform is usually not an on-chain transfer, and the fee structure is different. Here is a purely fictional teaching calculation: Step Assumed fee Value left Start — 100 USDT Quote gap on the buy 1 USDT 99 USDT Trading fee and slippage 0.5 USDT 98.5 USDT Exchange withdrawal fee 1 USDT 97.5 USDT On-chain approval and swap network fee 1.5 USDT 96 USDT Fictional teaching example: the different fees are all converted into a USDT equivalent so you can practice the cost. In real life the network fee is often paid in the chain's native coin, and asset value can also move with the market. Peel the costs Fictional teaching example. Start at 100 USDT. Turn an item on and the balance changes at once. All four on leaves 96 USDT, a 4% cost. Quote gap 1 Trade fee and slippage 0.5 Withdrawal fee 1 Network fee 1.5 Reset Think it through If you move funds from the funding account to the spot account inside an exchange, must you pay a blockchain network fee? A. You must B. Not necessarily. It is usually an internal book entry C. It must create a cross-chain fee"}, {"title": "How do profit and loss work?", "html": "<p>After a drop, the percentage rise needed to get back is larger than the drop, because the base of the calculation is already smaller.</p>\n<p>Fictional teaching example: you buy a coin with 100 USDT, it falls 50%, and 50 USDT is left.<br />\nIf it then rises 50%, that is only 50 × 1.5 = <strong>75 USDT</strong>, still 25 USDT short.</p>\n<p>That is because <strong>the base of the percentage changed</strong>.<br />\nTo get from 50 back to 100, it has to rise 50 ÷ 50 = <strong>100%</strong>.</p>\n<p>The simplest spot profit and loss, before fees:</p>\n<p><strong>Profit before fees = proceeds of the sale − cost of the buy.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Return before fees = profit ÷ cost of the buy × 100%.</strong></p>\n<p>Fictional teaching example: you put in 100 USDT and sell for 110 USDT. Before fees you made 10 USDT, a 10% return.<br />\nWhat you actually keep still has to subtract trading fees, withdrawal fees, and the rest.<br />\nBuys and sells in several lots, a different quote asset, or a contract position need a separate calculation.</p>\n<div class=\"ch03-lab\" data-lab=\"recover\">\n<h3>Recovery calculator</h3>\n<p class=\"lab-kicker\">Fictional teaching example. The start is fixed at 100. A 100% drop leaves a value of zero, and the percentage formula cannot recover from 0.</p>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Choose a drop\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-drop=\"0.1\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Down 10%</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-drop=\"0.2\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Down 20%</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-drop=\"0.5\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Down 50%</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-drop=\"0.8\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Down 80%</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-result\" hidden aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"lab-reset\" data-reset>Reset</button>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"c\" data-ok=\"Correct. Fictional teaching example: getting from 20 back to 100 takes a rise of 80, and 80 ÷ 20 = 400%.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. The rise to recover uses the remaining 20 as the base. Do not reuse the original drop as that rise.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Fictional teaching example: 100 falls to 20. What rise is needed to get back to 100?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\" data-note=\"Not quite. 80% treats the original drop as the rise needed to recover.\">A. 80%</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\" data-note=\"Not quite. 100% does not use the remaining 20 as the base.\">B. 100%</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. 400%</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "After a drop, the percentage rise needed to get back is larger than the drop, because the base of the calculation is already smaller. Fictional teaching example: you buy a coin with 100 USDT, it falls 50%, and 50 USDT is left. If it then rises 50%, that is only 50 × 1.5 = 75 USDT , still 25 USDT short. That is because the base of the percentage changed . To get from 50 back to 100, it has to rise 50 ÷ 50 = 100% . The simplest spot profit and loss, before fees: Profit before fees = proceeds of the sale − cost of the buy. Return before fees = profit ÷ cost of the buy × 100%. Fictional teaching example: you put in 100 USDT and sell for 110 USDT. Before fees you made 10 USDT, a 10% return. What you actually keep still has to subtract trading fees, withdrawal fees, and the rest. Buys and sells in several lots, a different quote asset, or a contract position need a separate calculation. Recovery calculator Fictional teaching example. The start is fixed at 100. A 100% drop leaves a value of zero, and the percentage formula cannot recover from 0. Down 10% Down 20% Down 50% Down 80% Reset Think it through Fictional teaching example: 100 falls to 20. What rise is needed to get back to 100? A. 80% B. 100% C. 400%"}, {"title": "Before you trade, you should be able to answer", "html": "<p>Before you press buy, say what you are buying, what it costs in total, whether you can get out, and whether a total loss would affect your life.</p>\n<p>You do not need a dozen indicators before you are allowed to buy a first coin.<br />\nBefore you press \"buy,\" you should be able to say four things:</p>\n<p><strong>First, what am I buying?</strong><br />\nIs it BTC, a stablecoin, or a token from a project you do not know?<br />\nWhat is it quoted in?</p>\n<p><strong>Second, what do I actually pay?</strong><br />\nBesides the coin price, are there fees, a spread, slippage, a withdrawal fee, or a network fee?</p>\n<p><strong>Third, can I get out?</strong><br />\nIs there a lockup, an unlock, a platform withdrawal limit, thin liquidity, or a contract-permission risk?</p>\n<p><strong>Fourth, what is the worst case?</strong><br />\nCan this money be lost entirely?<br />\nIf it is, would that affect your life?</p>\n<p>If you cannot answer one of them, look it up first. You do not have to force an order to chase the market.</p>\n<div class=\"ch03-lab\" data-lab=\"check\">\n<h3>A check before a simulated order</h3>\n<p class=\"lab-kicker\">Fictional teaching project ABC. There is no real buy button, and this does not connect to an exchange.</p>\n<ul class=\"lab-facts\">\n<li>Quoted as ABC/USDT, unit price 0.1 dollars</li>\n<li>Circulating supply 100 million</li>\n<li data-miss>50 million unlock in three days</li>\n<li data-miss>The book is thin right now</li>\n<li data-miss>Withdrawals are in maintenance</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-buy>Buy now</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-check>Check first</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-feedback\" hidden aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"lab-reset\" data-reset>Reset</button>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Answer whether you can get out before you decide to buy.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. A rally and a candle break do not answer whether you can get out.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>A coin is rallying, but the exchange has paused withdrawals and the book is thin. What is the steadier next step?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\" data-note=\"Not quite. You have not answered whether you can get out.\">A. Go all in first</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Find out the exit limits and the liquidity, then decide</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\" data-note=\"Not quite. A candle break does not answer whether you can get out.\">C. Look only at the candle break</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Before you press buy, say what you are buying, what it costs in total, whether you can get out, and whether a total loss would affect your life. You do not need a dozen indicators before you are allowed to buy a first coin. Before you press \"buy,\" you should be able to say four things: First, what am I buying? Is it BTC, a stablecoin, or a token from a project you do not know? What is it quoted in? Second, what do I actually pay? Besides the coin price, are there fees, a spread, slippage, a withdrawal fee, or a network fee? Third, can I get out? Is there a lockup, an unlock, a platform withdrawal limit, thin liquidity, or a contract-permission risk? Fourth, what is the worst case? Can this money be lost entirely? If it is, would that affect your life? If you cannot answer one of them, look it up first. You do not have to force an order to chase the market. A check before a simulated order Fictional teaching project ABC. There is no real buy button, and this does not connect to an exchange. Quoted as ABC/USDT, unit price 0.1 dollars Circulating supply 100 million 50 million unlock in three days The book is thin right now Withdrawals are in maintenance Buy now Check first Reset Think it through A coin is rallying, but the exchange has paused withdrawals and the book is thin. What is the steadier next step? A. Go all in first B. Find out the exit limits and the liquidity, then decide C. Look only at the candle break"}]}, {"id": "04", "title": "A first buy, and moving money in and out", "emoji": "🛒", "intro": "From sign-up to buying, then selling and withdrawing", "tags": ["Basics"], "sections": [{"title": "What are deposits and withdrawals?", "html": "<p>A deposit puts money onto an exchange; selling a coin is not fiat in the bank, and an on-chain withdrawal and a bank withdrawal are two different paths.</p>\n<p>Your bank account has 100 units of fiat, and you want a first BTC.<br />\nPaying that fiat through a compliant channel, and ending with an asset in the exchange account, is what people usually call a <strong>deposit</strong>.<br />\nLater you sell the coin and, through a channel your location supports, withdraw the fiat back to the bank. That completes a <strong>fiat withdrawal</strong>.</p>\n<p>\"Withdrawal\" can also mean <strong>withdrawing the coin</strong>: sending the exchange's USDT to your own wallet.<br />\nThat is not the same thing as withdrawing to a bank.</p>\n<p>Keep three places in mind:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bank account:</strong> holds fiat, handled by the bank and the payment system.</li>\n<li><strong>Exchange account:</strong> the platform records your balance;<br />\nthe platform holds the matching assets.</li>\n<li><strong>Self-custody wallet:</strong> you control the on-chain assets with a key. A wrong transfer or a leaked key often cannot be undone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Teaching simulation: you sell BTC on the exchange and receive 100 USDT.<br />\nWhat you hold is still USDT, <strong>not 100 dollars that have already arrived in the bank</strong>.<br />\nYou still need a fiat conversion and withdrawal channel your location allows, and you check the rate, the fees, and the limits.</p>\n<p><strong>Easy to miss:</strong> \"the exchange says the withdrawal succeeded\" is not \"the bank has credited it\";<br />\n\"the coin has been sold\" is not \"the fiat has arrived.\"</p>\n<div class=\"ch03-lab ch04-lab\" data-lab=\"route\">\n<h3>Two routes for the money</h3>\n<p class=\"lab-kicker\">Teaching simulation. Tap each node. Route A and route B stay separate. There is no single withdrawal button.</p>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"From the bank to the exchange\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-node=\"bank\">Bank fiat</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-node=\"channel\">A supported deposit channel</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-node=\"ex\">Exchange funding / spot account</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-kicker\">Route A: sell, then fiat</p>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Fiat withdrawal route\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-node=\"sell\">Sell</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-node=\"fiat\">Fiat withdrawal channel</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-node=\"bank2\">Bank</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-kicker\">Route B: on-chain withdrawal</p>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"On-chain withdrawal route\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-node=\"net\">Choose a network</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-node=\"chain\">On-chain withdrawal</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-node=\"wallet\">Self-custody wallet</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-result\" hidden aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"lab-reset\" data-reset>Reset</button>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. To receive fiat in the bank, use a fiat conversion and withdrawal channel your location supports.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. USDT does not turn into fiat by itself, and it cannot be sent straight to a bank account number.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>You sold BTC and received USDT. You next want fiat in the bank. What should you do?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\" data-note=\"Not quite. USDT does not turn into fiat by itself.\">A. Wait for the USDT to become fiat on its own</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Use a fiat conversion and withdrawal channel your location supports</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\" data-note=\"Not quite. A bank account number is usually not an on-chain receiving address.\">C. Send the USDT straight to the bank account number</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A deposit puts money onto an exchange; selling a coin is not fiat in the bank, and an on-chain withdrawal and a bank withdrawal are two different paths. Your bank account has 100 units of fiat, and you want a first BTC. Paying that fiat through a compliant channel, and ending with an asset in the exchange account, is what people usually call a deposit . Later you sell the coin and, through a channel your location supports, withdraw the fiat back to the bank. That completes a fiat withdrawal . \"Withdrawal\" can also mean withdrawing the coin : sending the exchange's USDT to your own wallet. That is not the same thing as withdrawing to a bank. Keep three places in mind: Bank account: holds fiat, handled by the bank and the payment system. Exchange account: the platform records your balance; the platform holds the matching assets. Self-custody wallet: you control the on-chain assets with a key. A wrong transfer or a leaked key often cannot be undone. Teaching simulation: you sell BTC on the exchange and receive 100 USDT. What you hold is still USDT, not 100 dollars that have already arrived in the bank . You still need a fiat conversion and withdrawal channel your location allows, and you check the rate, the fees, and the limits. Easy to miss: \"the exchange says the withdrawal succeeded\" is not \"the bank has credited it\"; \"the coin has been sold\" is not \"the fiat has arrived.\" Two routes for the money Teaching simulation. Tap each node. Route A and route B stay separate. There is no single withdrawal button. Bank fiat A supported deposit channel Exchange funding / spot account Route A: sell, then fiat Sell Fiat withdrawal channel Bank Route B: on-chain withdrawal Choose a network On-chain withdrawal Self-custody wallet Reset Think it through You sold BTC and received USDT. You next want fiat in the bank. What should you do? A. Wait for the USDT to become fiat on its own B. Use a fiat conversion and withdrawal channel your location supports C. Send the USDT straight to the bank account number"}, {"title": "Step 1: platform and region", "html": "<p>Exchanges that share a name in different regions may not share one account system, and the same UID is not the same person.</p>\n<p>Some exchange brands are run by different companies in different countries.<br />\nA similar name and a similar logo do not mean the accounts, UIDs, deposit systems, or support desks connect.</p>\n<p>Before you register, ask: <strong>which operating company is offering this service? Does it support the place where I actually live? How can I deposit and withdraw?</strong></p>\n<p>Teaching simulation: a friend sends you a receiving UID for an app related to Binance.<br />\nYou cannot look only at the brand and decide it is the same account system as the exchange you are logged into.<br />\n<strong>A UID means something only inside its own platform</strong>;<br />\neven when the digits match, they can point to different people.</p>\n<p><strong>Easy to miss:</strong> do not use a fake identity or a fake residence to get around a regional limit;<br />\ndo not skip the operating company and the official domain because an event is ending.</p>\n<div class=\"ch03-lab ch04-lab\" data-lab=\"uid\">\n<h3>Do these systems connect?</h3>\n<p class=\"lab-kicker\">Teaching simulation. Alpha Global and Alpha Local are not real platforms. Both show UID 123456, and the logos look the same.</p>\n<div class=\"lab-stats\">\n<div><span>Alpha Global</span><b>UID 123456</b><span>Operator: Global Learn Ltd</span></div>\n<div><span>Alpha Local</span><b>UID 123456</b><span>Operator: Local Learn Pte</span></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-uid=\"move\">Take UID 123456 to the other side</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-uid=\"check\">Check the official docs first</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-feedback\" hidden aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"lab-reset\" data-reset>Reset</button>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"c\" data-ok=\"Correct. A UID means something only inside its own platform. Matching digits still do not prove it is the same person.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Matching digits and a matching brand are not an identity that works across systems.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Two exchange apps in different regions both show UID 123456. Can you conclude it is the same person?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\" data-note=\"Not quite. Matching digits are not an identity credential across systems.\">A. Yes</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\" data-note=\"Not quite. The same brand does not mean the account systems connect.\">B. Yes, if the brand is the same</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. No. Check the operating company and the payee's identity</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Exchanges that share a name in different regions may not share one account system, and the same UID is not the same person. Some exchange brands are run by different companies in different countries. A similar name and a similar logo do not mean the accounts, UIDs, deposit systems, or support desks connect. Before you register, ask: which operating company is offering this service? Does it support the place where I actually live? How can I deposit and withdraw? Teaching simulation: a friend sends you a receiving UID for an app related to Binance. You cannot look only at the brand and decide it is the same account system as the exchange you are logged into. A UID means something only inside its own platform ; even when the digits match, they can point to different people. Easy to miss: do not use a fake identity or a fake residence to get around a regional limit; do not skip the operating company and the official domain because an event is ending. Do these systems connect? Teaching simulation. Alpha Global and Alpha Local are not real platforms. Both show UID 123456, and the logos look the same. Alpha Global UID 123456 Operator: Global Learn Ltd Alpha Local UID 123456 Operator: Local Learn Pte Take UID 123456 to the other side Check the official docs first Reset Think it through Two exchange apps in different regions both show UID 123456. Can you conclude it is the same person? A. Yes B. Yes, if the brand is the same C. No. Check the operating company and the payee's identity"}, {"title": "Step 2: sign up and lock the account down", "html": "<p>Lock the official entrance, the email, the login, withdrawals, and recovery before you put money into a new account.</p>\n<p>Depositing the moment you finish registering is like moving valuables into a new house before the keys are stored.</p>\n<p>Follow this order:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Confirm the official entrance:</strong> enter through a reliable official channel, and check the full domain, the app developer, and the platform prompt;<br />\nan ad ranking is not an official certification.</li>\n<li><strong>Protect the email:</strong> email is an important way back into the account. Use a separate strong password, and turn on the two-factor option the email supports.</li>\n<li><strong>Protect the exchange login:</strong> set a strong password that is different from the email. Prefer a passkey or authenticator the platform supports, and store the recovery method safely.</li>\n<li><strong>Set withdrawal protection:</strong> turn on an address allowlist, an anti-phishing code, device management, and unusual-activity alerts when the platform offers them.</li>\n<li><strong>Complete any required KYC:</strong> submit identity documents only in an official flow you have confirmed. Do not send an ID to \"support\" that messaged you first.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"keep-lines\">Teaching simulation<br>\nYou receive a text: \"Account unusual. Verify again now.\"<br>\nThe right move is not to tap the link in the text.<br>\nOpen the official app you already installed and checked, and look at the notice there.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Easy to miss:</strong> do not give a text-message code to anyone;<br />\nturning on two-factor does not mean you can ignore phishing links and device security.</p>\n<div class=\"ch03-lab ch04-lab\" data-lab=\"secure\">\n<h3>Security setup list</h3>\n<p class=\"lab-kicker\">Open an item to see why it matters, how to check it, and the common mistake. The count is only study progress, not a 100% safe account. This collects no password, code, or ID.</p>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Five security settings\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-sec=\"0\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Official entrance</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-sec=\"1\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Email security</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-sec=\"2\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Account two-factor</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-sec=\"3\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Withdrawal protection</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-sec=\"4\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Recovery</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-result\" hidden aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"lab-reset\" data-reset>Reset</button>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. Do not hand over the code. Open the official app you already checked, and look at the notice.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Do not give the code to the other person, and sending coins does not prove they are support.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Someone claiming to be support asks you to send a login code to \"lift a risk control.\" What should you do?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Refuse, and check inside the official app instead</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\" data-note=\"Not quite. The code itself can be used to take over the account.\">B. Send the code, but not the password</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\" data-note=\"Not quite. Sending coins does not prove the other person is support.\">C. Send a small amount of coins to test them</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Lock the official entrance, the email, the login, withdrawals, and recovery before you put money into a new account. Depositing the moment you finish registering is like moving valuables into a new house before the keys are stored. Follow this order: Confirm the official entrance: enter through a reliable official channel, and check the full domain, the app developer, and the platform prompt; an ad ranking is not an official certification. Protect the email: email is an important way back into the account. Use a separate strong password, and turn on the two-factor option the email supports. Protect the exchange login: set a strong password that is different from the email. Prefer a passkey or authenticator the platform supports, and store the recovery method safely. Set withdrawal protection: turn on an address allowlist, an anti-phishing code, device management, and unusual-activity alerts when the platform offers them. Complete any required KYC: submit identity documents only in an official flow you have confirmed. Do not send an ID to \"support\" that messaged you first. Teaching simulation You receive a text: \"Account unusual. Verify again now.\" The right move is not to tap the link in the text. Open the official app you already installed and checked, and look at the notice there. Easy to miss: do not give a text-message code to anyone; turning on two-factor does not mean you can ignore phishing links and device security. Security setup list Open an item to see why it matters, how to check it, and the common mistake. The count is only study progress, not a 100% safe account. This collects no password, code, or ID. Official entrance Email security Account two-factor Withdrawal protection Recovery Reset Think it through Someone claiming to be support asks you to send a login code to \"lift a risk control.\" What should you do? A. Refuse, and check inside the official app instead B. Send the code, but not the password C. Send a small amount of coins to test them"}, {"title": "Step 3: how you will deposit", "html": "<p>Do not compare deposits by whether the fee says 0; look at what you finally receive, plus time, refunds, and limits.</p>\n<p>Common ways in are a bank card or bank transfer the platform supports, a third-party payment provider, and P2P.<br />\nYou may already hold a digital asset and deposit that straight to the exchange.</p>\n<p>When you compare ways to deposit, do not look only at \"0% fee.\"<br />\nWhat matters is <strong>what you pay, how many coins you finally receive, how long it takes, whether it can be refunded, source-of-funds rules, and local limits</strong>.</p>\n<p>Teaching simulation: you plan to pay 100 units of fiat.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Channel A: the stated fee is zero, but you finally receive 19.6 USDT.</li>\n<li>Channel B: it openly charges 2 units of fiat, but you finally receive 20.1 USDT.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If everything else is the same, B shows a fee and you still receive more.<br />\nThis only compares a simulated final amount. It does not mean one kind of channel is always better.</p>\n<p><strong>If you already hold coins, be especially careful:</strong> confirm the deposit address, the asset, the network, and any Memo / Tag on the <strong>receiving platform's own page</strong>.<br />\nAn exchange that supports USDT does not support every USDT network.</p>\n<div class=\"ch03-lab ch04-lab\" data-lab=\"quote\">\n<h3>Compare the quotes</h3>\n<p class=\"lab-kicker\">Teaching simulation. Tap the two sorts. The order flips. Neither side is labeled best.</p>\n<div class=\"lab-stats\" data-rows></div>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-sort=\"fee\">Sort by stated fee</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-sort=\"got\">Sort by final USDT</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-result\" aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"c\" data-ok=\"Correct. Look at what you pay in total, what you finally receive, and the limits and risks.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. A 0 fee and a fast arrival cannot, by themselves, decide which one is the better deal.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>One channel says 0 fee and the other says it charges a fee. What is the more reasonable comparison?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\" data-note=\"Not quite. A 0 fee can hide a worse quote.\">A. Always pick the 0 fee</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\" data-note=\"Not quite. Faster does not mean cheaper, and it does not mean you are allowed to use it.\">B. Always pick the fastest</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Check the total paid, the amount received, the limits, and the risk</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Do not compare deposits by whether the fee says 0; look at what you finally receive, plus time, refunds, and limits. Common ways in are a bank card or bank transfer the platform supports, a third-party payment provider, and P2P. You may already hold a digital asset and deposit that straight to the exchange. When you compare ways to deposit, do not look only at \"0% fee.\" What matters is what you pay, how many coins you finally receive, how long it takes, whether it can be refunded, source-of-funds rules, and local limits . Teaching simulation: you plan to pay 100 units of fiat. Channel A: the stated fee is zero, but you finally receive 19.6 USDT. Channel B: it openly charges 2 units of fiat, but you finally receive 20.1 USDT. If everything else is the same, B shows a fee and you still receive more. This only compares a simulated final amount. It does not mean one kind of channel is always better. If you already hold coins, be especially careful: confirm the deposit address, the asset, the network, and any Memo / Tag on the receiving platform's own page . An exchange that supports USDT does not support every USDT network. Compare the quotes Teaching simulation. Tap the two sorts. The order flips. Neither side is labeled best. Sort by stated fee Sort by final USDT Think it through One channel says 0 fee and the other says it charges a fee. What is the more reasonable comparison? A. Always pick the 0 fee B. Always pick the fastest C. Check the total paid, the amount received, the limits, and the risk"}, {"title": "P2P, in particular", "html": "<p>In P2P a screenshot is not a credit; check the real bank credit and the payer inside the order, then follow the platform's rules.</p>\n<p>A common P2P case is you trading with another user while the platform provides the order and an escrow mechanism under its rules.<br />\n<strong>Escrow does not mean a bank transfer has no risk.</strong></p>\n<p>If you are the one selling coins and the other person sends a \"transfer succeeded\" screenshot, do not release the coins just because you are in a hurry.<br />\nA screenshot can be forged, a text can be faked, and some credits can still be disputed later.</p>\n<p>A safer order of steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Talk only inside the platform order, and check who you are trading with and what the payment requires.</li>\n<li>Use an account in your own name, as the platform rules require;<br />\nstay alert to a third-party payment or a name that does not match.</li>\n<li>Log in to the bank's official channel yourself, and check the real available balance, the transfer details, and the payer.</li>\n<li>Only after that matches the order rules do you confirm the release, through the platform's process.</li>\n<li>If there is a dispute, stop, keep the order, the chat, and the bank record, and appeal on the platform.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Easy to miss:</strong> \"release first, I will pay in a moment,\" or a request to move the chat off the platform, is a reason to pause.<br />\nDo not read a freeze, a chargeback, or fraud-related funds as \"if the balance shows the money, it is absolutely safe.\"</p>\n<div class=\"ch03-lab ch04-lab\" data-lab=\"p2p\">\n<h3>Three P2P messages</h3>\n<p class=\"lab-kicker\">Teaching simulation. All three cards are on this screen. There is no one-tap release.</p>\n<div class=\"ch04-card\">\n<p>1. They send a transfer screenshot, and your bank has not received it.</p>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-card=\"shot\" data-act=\"check\">Keep checking</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-card=\"shot\" data-act=\"pause\">Pause and appeal</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-card=\"shot\" data-act=\"rule\">Follow the platform rules</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-feedback\" hidden aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"ch04-card\">\n<p>2. The bank shows a credit, but the payer's name does not match the order.</p>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-card=\"name\" data-act=\"check\">Keep checking</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-card=\"name\" data-act=\"pause\">Pause and appeal</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-card=\"name\" data-act=\"rule\">Follow the platform rules</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-feedback\" hidden aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"ch04-card\">\n<p>3. The bank's official channel confirms the credit, and the payer matches the order.</p>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-card=\"paid\" data-act=\"check\">Keep checking</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-card=\"paid\" data-act=\"pause\">Pause and appeal</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-card=\"paid\" data-act=\"rule\">Follow the platform rules</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-feedback\" hidden aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n</div>\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"lab-reset\" data-reset>Reset</button>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. The bank has not seen the money, so do not release the coins. Check inside the order.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. A screenshot does not prove payment, and leaving the order also loses the record the platform holds.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>The buyer sends a payment screenshot, but you do not see the money in your bank account. What should you do?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\" data-note=\"Not quite. A screenshot does not prove the money has arrived.\">A. Release the coins to show trust</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Do not release them. Check, and follow the order rules</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\" data-note=\"Not quite. Leaving the order loses the record and the protection inside the platform.\">C. Confirm in a chat outside the platform</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "In P2P a screenshot is not a credit; check the real bank credit and the payer inside the order, then follow the platform's rules. A common P2P case is you trading with another user while the platform provides the order and an escrow mechanism under its rules. Escrow does not mean a bank transfer has no risk. If you are the one selling coins and the other person sends a \"transfer succeeded\" screenshot, do not release the coins just because you are in a hurry. A screenshot can be forged, a text can be faked, and some credits can still be disputed later. A safer order of steps: Talk only inside the platform order, and check who you are trading with and what the payment requires. Use an account in your own name, as the platform rules require; stay alert to a third-party payment or a name that does not match. Log in to the bank's official channel yourself, and check the real available balance, the transfer details, and the payer. Only after that matches the order rules do you confirm the release, through the platform's process. If there is a dispute, stop, keep the order, the chat, and the bank record, and appeal on the platform. Easy to miss: \"release first, I will pay in a moment,\" or a request to move the chat off the platform, is a reason to pause. Do not read a freeze, a chargeback, or fraud-related funds as \"if the balance shows the money, it is absolutely safe.\" Three P2P messages Teaching simulation. All three cards are on this screen. There is no one-tap release. 1. They send a transfer screenshot, and your bank has not received it. Keep checking Pause and appeal Follow the platform rules 2. The bank shows a credit, but the payer's name does not match the order. Keep checking Pause and appeal Follow the platform rules 3. The bank's official channel confirms the credit, and the payer matches the order. Keep checking Pause and appeal Follow the platform rules Reset Think it through The buyer sends a payment screenshot, but you do not see the money in your bank account. What should you do? A. Release the coins to show trust B. Do not release them. Check, and follow the order rules C. Confirm in a chat outside the platform"}, {"title": "Step 4: buy a little spot", "html": "<p>A first spot buy is for learning the flow; a market order does not guarantee the fill price, and a limit order is not guaranteed to fill.</p>\n<p>The goal of the first trade is to <strong>understand the whole flow</strong>, not to make money on that first order.</p>\n<p>Teaching simulation: suppose you plan to try with 20 USDT.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Open <strong>spot</strong>, and confirm the pair, for example BTC/USDT.</li>\n<li>Confirm you are buying BTC and spending USDT, not the reverse.</li>\n<li>Look at the order type: a market order can slip;<br />\na limit order may not fill.</li>\n<li>Enter the amount, and check the estimated quantity, the minimum order, and the fees.</li>\n<li>Read the confirmation page once more before you submit;<br />\nafter it fills, check the <strong>actual quantity and the fee</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Teaching simulation: suppose BTC is quoted at 50,000 USDT. Before fees, 20 USDT buys about 0.0004 BTC.<br />\nThe real quantity can differ because of fees, price moves, and the platform's precision rules.</p>\n<p><strong>Easy to miss:</strong> \"the order was submitted\" is not \"it filled completely\";<br />\na limit order can sit in the book, with the asset frozen.</p>\n<div class=\"ch03-lab ch04-lab\" data-lab=\"order\">\n<h3>A simulated order, with no funds</h3>\n<p class=\"lab-kicker\">Teaching simulation. It does not connect to a trading API, and it does not ask you to log in. Tap market or limit, then tap a state.</p>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Order type\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-side=\"market\" aria-pressed=\"true\">Market</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-side=\"limit\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Limit</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Simulated state\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-state=\"filled\">Filled</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-state=\"partial\">Partially filled</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-state=\"open\">Unfilled</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-result\" aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"lab-reset\" data-reset>Reset</button>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. Unfilled usually means it is still waiting for the other side. It is not already bought, and it does not mean the money is lost.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Unfilled does not mean the money is lost, and it does not mean the coins are already bought.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>You submit a limit buy, and the page says \"unfilled.\" What is the more reasonable reading?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. The order may still be waiting for a match</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\" data-note=\"Not quite. Unfilled does not mean the assets disappeared. The limit order may still be open.\">B. The money is certainly lost</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\" data-note=\"Not quite. Unfilled means it has not been bought.\">C. The coins are bought and the screen just hides them</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A first spot buy is for learning the flow; a market order does not guarantee the fill price, and a limit order is not guaranteed to fill. The goal of the first trade is to understand the whole flow , not to make money on that first order. Teaching simulation: suppose you plan to try with 20 USDT. Open spot , and confirm the pair, for example BTC/USDT. Confirm you are buying BTC and spending USDT, not the reverse. Look at the order type: a market order can slip; a limit order may not fill. Enter the amount, and check the estimated quantity, the minimum order, and the fees. Read the confirmation page once more before you submit; after it fills, check the actual quantity and the fee . Teaching simulation: suppose BTC is quoted at 50,000 USDT. Before fees, 20 USDT buys about 0.0004 BTC. The real quantity can differ because of fees, price moves, and the platform's precision rules. Easy to miss: \"the order was submitted\" is not \"it filled completely\"; a limit order can sit in the book, with the asset frozen. A simulated order, with no funds Teaching simulation. It does not connect to a trading API, and it does not ask you to log in. Tap market or limit, then tap a state. Market Limit Filled Partially filled Unfilled Reset Think it through You submit a limit buy, and the page says \"unfilled.\" What is the more reasonable reading? A. The order may still be waiting for a match B. The money is certainly lost C. The coins are bought and the screen just hides them"}, {"title": "Step 5: selling and withdrawing", "html": "<p>Selling into USDT, withdrawing to your own wallet, and converting to fiat in the bank are three different things, and a sale does not put money in the bank.</p>\n<p>Beginners often treat these three steps as one thing:</p>\n<p><strong>Selling:</strong> turn BTC into a quote asset such as USDT.</p>\n<p><strong>Withdrawing the coin:</strong> send the exchange asset to an external chain address. That involves the network, the address, and possibly a Memo / Tag.</p>\n<p><strong>Fiat withdrawal:</strong> convert and settle into fiat by a method your location allows, then send it to a bank or another receiving account.</p>\n<p>Teaching simulation: you sell 0.001 BTC and receive about 50 USDT.<br />\nIf you then tap \"on-chain withdrawal,\" you enter a wallet address, not a bank account;<br />\nif you want fiat, look for whether the platform or a provider offers a local fiat withdrawal path, and compare the rate, the fees, and the time.</p>\n<p><strong>Easy to miss:</strong> when you withdraw, check the asset, the network, the address, any Memo / Tag, and whether the receiving platform supports it;<br />\ntest a small amount before a large transfer, and a successful test still does not guarantee the next one is safe.<br />\nKeep the trade record and the record of where the funds came from, and handle tax and compliance the way your location requires.</p>\n<p>Note: The goal of the first trade is to understand the process, not to prove you can catch a rally.</p>\n<div class=\"ch03-lab ch04-lab\" data-lab=\"goals\">\n<h3>Three kinds of sell and withdraw</h3>\n<p class=\"lab-kicker\">Teaching simulation. Tap a destination under each goal. On a phone, tap. There is nothing to drag.</p>\n<div class=\"ch04-card\" data-row>\n<p>I want to turn BTC into USDT</p>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-goal=\"swap\" data-to=\"spot\">Spot sale</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-goal=\"swap\" data-to=\"chain\">On-chain withdrawal</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-goal=\"swap\" data-to=\"fiat\">Fiat withdrawal</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-feedback\" hidden aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"ch04-card\" data-row>\n<p>I want to send USDT to my own wallet</p>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-goal=\"wallet\" data-to=\"spot\">Spot sale</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-goal=\"wallet\" data-to=\"chain\">On-chain withdrawal</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-goal=\"wallet\" data-to=\"fiat\">Fiat withdrawal</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-feedback\" hidden aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"ch04-card\" data-row>\n<p>I want the value as fiat in the bank</p>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-goal=\"bank\" data-to=\"spot\">Spot sale</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-goal=\"bank\" data-to=\"chain\">On-chain withdrawal</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-goal=\"bank\" data-to=\"fiat\">Fiat withdrawal</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-feedback\" hidden aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n</div>\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"lab-reset\" data-reset>Reset</button>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"c\" data-ok=\"Correct. The USDT is still on the exchange. To receive fiat in the bank, you still need a supported conversion and withdrawal.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. USDT on an exchange is not a bank deposit, and the coin price cannot turn it into one.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>After you sell BTC and receive USDT, has the USDT automatically become a bank deposit?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\" data-note=\"Not quite. USDT on an exchange is not a deposit in the bank.\">A. Yes</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\" data-note=\"Not quite. The coin price cannot turn an exchange asset into a bank deposit.\">B. It depends on the coin price</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. No. You still need a supported fiat conversion and withdrawal</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Selling into USDT, withdrawing to your own wallet, and converting to fiat in the bank are three different things, and a sale does not put money in the bank. Beginners often treat these three steps as one thing: Selling: turn BTC into a quote asset such as USDT. Withdrawing the coin: send the exchange asset to an external chain address. That involves the network, the address, and possibly a Memo / Tag. Fiat withdrawal: convert and settle into fiat by a method your location allows, then send it to a bank or another receiving account. Teaching simulation: you sell 0.001 BTC and receive about 50 USDT. If you then tap \"on-chain withdrawal,\" you enter a wallet address, not a bank account; if you want fiat, look for whether the platform or a provider offers a local fiat withdrawal path, and compare the rate, the fees, and the time. Easy to miss: when you withdraw, check the asset, the network, the address, any Memo / Tag, and whether the receiving platform supports it; test a small amount before a large transfer, and a successful test still does not guarantee the next one is safe. Keep the trade record and the record of where the funds came from, and handle tax and compliance the way your location requires. Note: The goal of the first trade is to understand the process, not to prove you can catch a rally. Three kinds of sell and withdraw Teaching simulation. Tap a destination under each goal. On a phone, tap. There is nothing to drag. I want to turn BTC into USDT Spot sale On-chain withdrawal Fiat withdrawal I want to send USDT to my own wallet Spot sale On-chain withdrawal Fiat withdrawal I want the value as fiat in the bank Spot sale On-chain withdrawal Fiat withdrawal Reset Think it through After you sell BTC and receive USDT, has the USDT automatically become a bank deposit? A. Yes B. It depends on the coin price C. No. You still need a supported fiat conversion and withdrawal"}, {"title": "The accounts inside an exchange", "html": "<p>When the spot page shows no balance, check whether the coins are in another account; an internal transfer on the platform usually does not go on-chain.</p>\n<p>You clearly have 100 USDT. Why does the order page say the balance is not enough?</p>\n<p>Often the coins are not gone. They are in the <strong>funding account</strong>, and you opened <strong>spot trading</strong>;<br />\nor they are in the <strong>contract account</strong>, and they cannot be used for this spot order.</p>\n<p>Think of the accounts on one exchange as drawers with different jobs:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Place</th>\n<th>Main use</th>\n<th>A common reminder</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Funding account</td>\n<td>Deposits, P2P, withdrawals, and similar</td>\n<td>The exact features differ by platform</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spot account</td>\n<td>Spot buys and sells</td>\n<td>Confirm the available balance before you order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Contract account</td>\n<td>Margin for derivatives</td>\n<td>Liquidation, fees, and the account mode can apply</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unified trading account</td>\n<td>Some trading functions combined</td>\n<td>The rules differ by platform</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transfer between accounts</td>\n<td>Move assets between accounts on the same platform</td>\n<td>Usually not on-chain, and usually no blockchain network fee</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>On-chain withdrawal</td>\n<td>Send coins to an external address</td>\n<td>Check the network and the address. After it arrives, it cannot be undone</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>A transfer between accounts</strong> is usually an internal book entry on the same platform, not an on-chain transfer, and it generally does not create a blockchain network fee;<br />\n<strong>an on-chain withdrawal</strong> sends the asset onto an external network, with a real address and network confirmations.</p>\n<p>Teaching simulation: 100 USDT shows in the funding account, and the spot page shows 0 available.<br />\nYou confirm \"from funding to spot\" on the platform's internal transfer page, and after it succeeds you check the spot available balance again.<br />\nSome platforms use a unified account and do not have the same entrance.</p>\n<p><strong>Easy to miss:</strong> do not deposit again just because you see \"insufficient balance\";<br />\nfirst check the account type, frozen orders, collateral for a loan, and the available balance.<br />\nDo not mistake \"transfer\" for \"send to a UID on another platform.\"</p>\n<div class=\"ch03-lab ch04-lab\" data-lab=\"drawers\">\n<h3>Account drawers</h3>\n<p class=\"lab-kicker\">Teaching simulation. Internal transfer and on-chain withdrawal are two buttons. The amount cannot go below 0, and it cannot exceed the available balance.</p>\n<div class=\"lab-stats\">\n<div><span>Funding</span><b data-fund>100 USDT</b></div>\n<div><span>Spot</span><b data-spot>0 USDT</b></div>\n<div><span>Contract</span><b data-perp>0 USDT</b></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"lab-choices\">\n<button type=\"button\" data-move>Transfer 40 USDT internally to spot</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-undo>Undo the last step</button>\n<button type=\"button\" data-chain>On-chain withdrawal (check separately)</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-result\" aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. First see whether it is still in the funding account, then decide whether an internal transfer is needed.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Check the account first. Do not deposit again, and do not send the coins to support.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>The exchange spot page shows 0 USDT, but the funding account has 100 USDT. What is the first step?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\" data-note=\"Not quite. It may only be sitting in another account. Depositing again can double the deposit.\">A. Deposit another 100</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Check the account type, and whether an internal transfer is needed</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\" data-note=\"Not quite. Do not send the coins to support. That hands the assets over.\">C. Send the coins to support's UID</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "When the spot page shows no balance, check whether the coins are in another account; an internal transfer on the platform usually does not go on-chain. You clearly have 100 USDT. Why does the order page say the balance is not enough? Often the coins are not gone. They are in the funding account , and you opened spot trading ; or they are in the contract account , and they cannot be used for this spot order. Think of the accounts on one exchange as drawers with different jobs: Place Main use A common reminder Funding account Deposits, P2P, withdrawals, and similar The exact features differ by platform Spot account Spot buys and sells Confirm the available balance before you order Contract account Margin for derivatives Liquidation, fees, and the account mode can apply Unified trading account Some trading functions combined The rules differ by platform Transfer between accounts Move assets between accounts on the same platform Usually not on-chain, and usually no blockchain network fee On-chain withdrawal Send coins to an external address Check the network and the address. After it arrives, it cannot be undone A transfer between accounts is usually an internal book entry on the same platform, not an on-chain transfer, and it generally does not create a blockchain network fee; an on-chain withdrawal sends the asset onto an external network, with a real address and network confirmations. Teaching simulation: 100 USDT shows in the funding account, and the spot page shows 0 available. You confirm \"from funding to spot\" on the platform's internal transfer page, and after it succeeds you check the spot available balance again. Some platforms use a unified account and do not have the same entrance. Easy to miss: do not deposit again just because you see \"insufficient balance\"; first check the account type, frozen orders, collateral for a loan, and the available balance. Do not mistake \"transfer\" for \"send to a UID on another platform.\" Account drawers Teaching simulation. Internal transfer and on-chain withdrawal are two buttons. The amount cannot go below 0, and it cannot exceed the available balance. Funding 100 USDT Spot 0 USDT Contract 0 USDT Transfer 40 USDT internally to spot Undo the last step On-chain withdrawal (check separately) Think it through The exchange spot page shows 0 USDT, but the funding account has 100 USDT. What is the first step? A. Deposit another 100 B. Check the account type, and whether an internal transfer is needed C. Send the coins to support's UID"}]}, {"id": "05", "title": "CEX exchange guide", "emoji": "🏦", "intro": "Official entrances, and how to choose", "tags": ["Guide"], "tip": "Note: Check the domain before you click, read the rules before you open an account, and think about platform risk before you deposit.", "sections": [{"title": "What can a CEX do for you?", "html": "<p>An exchange account's coins are held by the platform, not in a wallet you control yourself.</p>\n<p>A CEX usually offers accounts, deposits and withdrawals, and trading. Some platforms also offer fiat channels, derivatives, or other products.<br />\nWhether it is open to you depends on the region, identity, and the rules of the specific product.</p>\n<p>A platform account balance is usually an internal record of the platform.<br />\nYou rely on the operator to hold the assets and process withdrawals. Do not treat it as a wallet whose private key you control.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. On a CEX, the platform usually holds the assets.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. On a CEX, the platform usually holds the assets.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Who usually holds the assets behind a CEX balance?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. The platform</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. The user's own private key</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Miners</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "An exchange account's coins are held by the platform, not in a wallet you control yourself. A CEX usually offers accounts, deposits and withdrawals, and trading. Some platforms also offer fiat channels, derivatives, or other products. Whether it is open to you depends on the region, identity, and the rules of the specific product. A platform account balance is usually an internal record of the platform. You rely on the operator to hold the assets and process withdrawals. Do not treat it as a wallet whose private key you control. Think it through Who usually holds the assets behind a CEX balance? A. The platform B. The user's own private key C. Miners"}, {"title": "How is a CEX different from a DEX?", "html": "<p>A CEX is a company keeping the books for you, and a DEX is you trading on-chain directly from a wallet.</p>\n<p>A CEX is a centralized exchange.<br />\nThe operator usually manages accounts, orders, and the asset records inside the platform, and users trade through the platform.</p>\n<p>A DEX is a decentralized trading protocol or venue.<br />\nMany DEXes complete an on-chain swap through a smart contract. The user signs from a wallet, and matching and settlement can work in different ways.</p>\n<p>A DEX still has centralized pieces.<br />\nThe front end, an admin, an oracle, and other components can create risk too.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. A DEX can have contract risk.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. A DEX can have contract risk.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Can a DEX have smart-contract risk?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. It can</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. It never can</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. There is no permission risk</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A CEX is a company keeping the books for you, and a DEX is you trading on-chain directly from a wallet. A CEX is a centralized exchange. The operator usually manages accounts, orders, and the asset records inside the platform, and users trade through the platform. A DEX is a decentralized trading protocol or venue. Many DEXes complete an on-chain swap through a smart contract. The user signs from a wallet, and matching and settlement can work in different ways. A DEX still has centralized pieces. The front end, an admin, an oracle, and other components can create risk too. Think it through Can a DEX have smart-contract risk? A. It can B. It never can C. There is no permission risk"}, {"title": "Referral sign-up links", "html": "<p>Check whether the platform is open in your region before you register, then enter from the links below.</p>\n<p>Only Binance, Bybit, Gate, and HTX are shown here.<br>\nThe order is not a safety ranking or a recommendation.<br>\nBefore you use one, confirm that registration and the related services are supported where you are.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. A referral link is not a safety guarantee.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. A referral link is not a safety guarantee.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Is a referral link a safety guarantee?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. No</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Yes</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. No need to check</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div><div data-widget=\"exchanges\"></div>", "text": "Check whether the platform is open in your region before you register, then enter from the links below. Only Binance, Bybit, Gate, and HTX are shown here. The order is not a safety ranking or a recommendation. Before you use one, confirm that registration and the related services are supported where you are. Think it through Is a referral link a safety guarantee? A. No B. Yes C. No need to check | Platform | Referral sign-up link | |---|---| | Binance | https://accounts.binance.info/zh-CN/register?ref=I1GN6RMJ | | Bybit | https://partner.bybit.com/b/0xmay | | Gate | https://www.gatewebsite.app/share/u1dgb10j | | HTX | https://www.htx.com/invite/zh-cn/1h?invite_code=jdz98223 | Card label: a dedicated referral link. Card note: this is the platform's promotional sign-up domain. After you register, check the account in the official app."}, {"title": "On-chain trading tools", "html": "<p>DeBot and Binance Web3 Wallet are for buying and selling tokens that are already issued; they are not a primary subscription, and new on-chain tokens are high risk.</p>\n<p><strong>If you want to buy and sell tokens directly on-chain, these two tools can do that.</strong></p>\n<p>DeBot and Binance Web3 Wallet are both for buying and selling tokens that are already issued on-chain (the secondary market), not a primary-market subscription.<br>\nNew on-chain tokens are high risk. Read chapter 09 before you act.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. Buying an already issued token on a DEX is generally secondary-market trading.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Buying an already issued token on a DEX is generally secondary-market trading.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>What is buying an already issued token on a DEX?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Generally secondary-market trading</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Always a private sale</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. A bank deposit</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div><div data-widget=\"onchain\"></div>", "text": "DeBot and Binance Web3 Wallet are for buying and selling tokens that are already issued; they are not a primary subscription, and new on-chain tokens are high risk. If you want to buy and sell tokens directly on-chain, these two tools can do that. DeBot and Binance Web3 Wallet are both for buying and selling tokens that are already issued on-chain (the secondary market), not a primary-market subscription. New on-chain tokens are high risk. Read chapter 09 before you act. Think it through What is buying an already issued token on a DEX? A. Generally secondary-market trading B. Always a private sale C. A bank deposit"}, {"title": "How do you choose?", "html": "<p>Look first at whether you can use it, then at safety, fees, and withdrawals, not only at a sign-up reward.</p>\n<p>First check whether your location can use it, then the trading you need, the deposit method, the depth of the market you care about, the fees, the security settings, the withdrawal rules, and the support channel.<br />\nDo not decide only because of a sign-up reward or one KOL's recommendation.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. First check whether your region can use it, and how withdrawals work.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. First check whether your region can use it, and how withdrawals work.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>What should you look at first when choosing a platform?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Whether your region can use it, and withdrawals</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. A rebate slogan</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. A KOL ranking</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Look first at whether you can use it, then at safety, fees, and withdrawals, not only at a sign-up reward. First check whether your location can use it, then the trading you need, the deposit method, the depth of the market you care about, the fees, the security settings, the withdrawal rules, and the support channel. Do not decide only because of a sign-up reward or one KOL's recommendation. Think it through What should you look at first when choosing a platform? A. Whether your region can use it, and withdrawals B. A rebate slogan C. A KOL ranking"}, {"title": "What does proof of reserves mean?", "html": "<p>Proof of reserves is a snapshot of one moment, not proof that the platform is safe forever.</p>\n<p>Proof of reserves can provide a check of assets or liabilities at a specific time and within a specific scope. What it is worth depends on the method and the coverage.<br />\nIt is not necessarily a full financial audit, and it cannot guarantee that nothing will go wrong later or that you can withdraw at any time.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>📰 <strong>A real case: the FTX collapse (2022)</strong><br />\nBefore the 2022 bankruptcy, FTX had ranked about third in the world by volume (according to a November report that year by The Verge). On November 2, 2022, balance-sheet problems were reported. Six days later, on November 8, it stopped all crypto withdrawals. Users had withdrawn about 6 billion dollars in the first 72 hours. On November 11 it filed for bankruptcy protection. Founder SBF was later sentenced to 25 years.<br />\n<strong>The lesson: a large platform can fail too. Keep an eye on exchange news and announcements. If withdrawals slow down or the bad news piles up, move the assets out first.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. Proof of reserves does not guarantee you can withdraw at any time.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Proof of reserves does not guarantee you can withdraw at any time.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Does proof of reserves guarantee you can withdraw at any time?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. It cannot guarantee that</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. It certainly guarantees that</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. It is the same as deposit insurance</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Proof of reserves is a snapshot of one moment, not proof that the platform is safe forever. Proof of reserves can provide a check of assets or liabilities at a specific time and within a specific scope. What it is worth depends on the method and the coverage. It is not necessarily a full financial audit, and it cannot guarantee that nothing will go wrong later or that you can withdraw at any time. 📰 A real case: the FTX collapse (2022) Before the 2022 bankruptcy, FTX had ranked about third in the world by volume (according to a November report that year by The Verge). On November 2, 2022, balance-sheet problems were reported. Six days later, on November 8, it stopped all crypto withdrawals. Users had withdrawn about 6 billion dollars in the first 72 hours. On November 11 it filed for bankruptcy protection. Founder SBF was later sentenced to 25 years. The lesson: a large platform can fail too. Keep an eye on exchange news and announcements. If withdrawals slow down or the bad news piles up, move the assets out first. Note: Check the domain before you click, read the rules before you open an account, and think about platform risk before you deposit. Think it through Does proof of reserves guarantee you can withdraw at any time? A. It cannot guarantee that B. It certainly guarantees that C. It is the same as deposit insurance"}]}, {"id": "06", "title": "Spot orders for beginners", "emoji": "🧾", "intro": "Place an order, read a candle, and keep your nerve", "tags": ["Basics"], "sections": [{"title": "What is spot?", "html": "<p>Spot is buying the coin directly, without borrowing and without leverage.</p>\n<p>Spot is buying and selling the asset itself.<br />\nAn ordinary spot position with no loan is not liquidated because the price falls, but the price can still fall close to zero.</p>\n<p>For leverage and contracts, see <a href=\"#/c/07\">Chapter 07, &quot;Going further: leverage and risk&quot;</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. Unlevered spot is not usually liquidated automatically because the price fell.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Unlevered spot is not usually liquidated automatically because the price fell.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Will unlevered spot be liquidated automatically because the price fell?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Usually not</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Always</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. There is no loss</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Spot is buying the coin directly, without borrowing and without leverage. Spot is buying and selling the asset itself. An ordinary spot position with no loan is not liquidated because the price falls, but the price can still fall close to zero. For leverage and contracts, see Chapter 07, \"Going further: leverage and risk\" . Think it through Will unlevered spot be liquidated automatically because the price fell? A. Usually not B. Always C. There is no loss"}, {"title": "Order types", "html": "<p>A market order fills soon but does not fix the price, and a limit order fixes the price but may not fill.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>An easy misunderstanding</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Market</td>\n<td>Fills as soon as it can at the available quote</td>\n<td>It does not guarantee one fixed price</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Limit</td>\n<td>Sets a maximum buy price or a minimum sell price</td>\n<td>It may not fill, or it may fill only in part</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stop market</td>\n<td>Submits a market order after a trigger</td>\n<td>An extreme move can create large slippage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stop limit</td>\n<td>Submits a limit order after a trigger</td>\n<td>A trigger still may not produce a fill</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The orders and trigger prices a platform supports differ.<br />\nRead the description before you submit.</p>\n<p><strong>Fictional teaching example:</strong> a market order walks the book, and a limit order waits for a fill. Those are two timelines.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. A limit order is not guaranteed to fill.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. A limit order is not guaranteed to fill.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Is a limit order guaranteed to fill?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Not necessarily</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Always</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. It needs no other side</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A market order fills soon but does not fix the price, and a limit order fixes the price but may not fill. Type What it does An easy misunderstanding Market Fills as soon as it can at the available quote It does not guarantee one fixed price Limit Sets a maximum buy price or a minimum sell price It may not fill, or it may fill only in part Stop market Submits a market order after a trigger An extreme move can create large slippage Stop limit Submits a limit order after a trigger A trigger still may not produce a fill The orders and trigger prices a platform supports differ. Read the description before you submit. Fictional teaching example: a market order walks the book, and a limit order waits for a fill. Those are two timelines. Think it through Is a limit order guaranteed to fill? A. Not necessarily B. Always C. It needs no other side"}, {"title": "Candle basics", "html": "<p>One candle records the open, high, low, and close of a period.</p>\n<p>A candle usually records the open, the high, the low, and the close of that period.<br />\n1 minute, 15 minutes, 1 hour, and 1 day are different scales of looking.</p>\n<p>The same asset can show a different trend on a different timeframe.<br />\nThe platform can set which color means up or down, so do not judge by red and green alone.</p>\n<p><strong>Fictional teaching example:</strong> an OHLC candle with four labels, switching between 1 minute and the daily.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. The four candle prices are open, high, low, and close.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. The four candle prices are open, high, low, and close.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>What are the four prices of a candle?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Open, high, low, close</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. The funding rate</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Contract leverage</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "One candle records the open, high, low, and close of a period. A candle usually records the open, the high, the low, and the close of that period. 1 minute, 15 minutes, 1 hour, and 1 day are different scales of looking. The same asset can show a different trend on a different timeframe. The platform can set which color means up or down, so do not judge by red and green alone. Fictional teaching example: an OHLC candle with four labels, switching between 1 minute and the daily. Think it through What are the four prices of a candle? A. Open, high, low, close B. The funding rate C. Contract leverage"}, {"title": "A trade log", "html": "<p>Write the reason before the order and the review after it; that helps more than watching one more indicator.</p>\n<p>Before the order, write why you are buying, what would show the judgment was wrong, where you plan to exit, the size, the fees, and the possible loss.<br />\nAfter the trade, write whether the execution matched the plan, where it drifted, and whether the result came from the strategy or from luck.</p>\n<p>Practice first with a simulated trade.<br />\nSimulated profit also does not directly represent real trading. Real money adds emotion, depth, and execution cost.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. A trade log should record the plan and where execution drifted.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. A trade log should record the plan and where execution drifted.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>What should a trade log record?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. The plan and where execution drifted</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Only the profits</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Only the coin name</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Write the reason before the order and the review after it; that helps more than watching one more indicator. Before the order, write why you are buying, what would show the judgment was wrong, where you plan to exit, the size, the fees, and the possible loss. After the trade, write whether the execution matched the plan, where it drifted, and whether the result came from the strategy or from luck. Practice first with a simulated trade. Simulated profit also does not directly represent real trading. Real money adds emotion, depth, and execution cost. Think it through What should a trade log record? A. The plan and where execution drifted B. Only the profits C. Only the coin name"}, {"title": "Three moments when people lose their head", "html": "<p>Fear of missing out, rushing to get back to even, and a winning streak are when impulse is easiest.</p>\n<p>Fear of missing out: other people are making money, and you rush to chase.<br />\nRushing to get back to even: after a loss, you increase the size or the leverage.<br />\nA winning streak: you treat short-term luck as a stable skill.</p>\n<p>When you are in one of these states, pausing and rereading the plan helps more than hunting for one more indicator.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. Rushing to add size after a loss can magnify the loss.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Rushing to add size after a loss can magnify the loss.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>What is the risk of rushing to add size after a loss?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. It magnifies the loss</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. It guarantees you get back to even</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. There is no risk</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Fear of missing out, rushing to get back to even, and a winning streak are when impulse is easiest. Fear of missing out: other people are making money, and you rush to chase. Rushing to get back to even: after a loss, you increase the size or the leverage. A winning streak: you treat short-term luck as a stable skill. When you are in one of these states, pausing and rereading the plan helps more than hunting for one more indicator. Think it through What is the risk of rushing to add size after a loss? A. It magnifies the loss B. It guarantees you get back to even C. There is no risk"}]}, {"id": "07", "title": "Going further: leverage and risk", "emoji": "⚖️", "intro": "Leverage, contracts, liquidation, and position size", "tags": ["Advanced"], "sections": [{"title": "Leverage and contracts", "html": "<p>Leverage magnifies both gains and losses, so beginners should leave it alone at first.</p>\n<p>Leveraged trading widens the risk through borrowing or margin.<br />\nBoth gains and losses get larger, and there are borrowing costs and liquidation risk.</p>\n<p>Contract trading is a derivatives exposure. You do not necessarily hold the matching spot asset.<br />\nA perpetual contract has no traditional fixed expiry. A funding fee and similar mechanisms are often used to keep its price near a reference price.</p>\n<p><strong>Fictional teaching example:</strong> the same 1,000 USDT notional position, comparing the margin used at 1x and at 10x.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. Leverage magnifies profit and loss relative to the margin.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Leverage magnifies profit and loss relative to the margin.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>What does leverage magnify?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Profit and loss relative to the margin</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. The win rate</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. The coin price itself</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Leverage magnifies both gains and losses, so beginners should leave it alone at first. Leveraged trading widens the risk through borrowing or margin. Both gains and losses get larger, and there are borrowing costs and liquidation risk. Contract trading is a derivatives exposure. You do not necessarily hold the matching spot asset. A perpetual contract has no traditional fixed expiry. A funding fee and similar mechanisms are often used to keep its price near a reference price. Fictional teaching example: the same 1,000 USDT notional position, comparing the margin used at 1x and at 10x. Think it through What does leverage magnify? A. Profit and loss relative to the margin B. The win rate C. The coin price itself"}, {"title": "Long and short", "html": "<p>A long bets on a rise, a short bets on a fall, and a short often means borrowing the coin or using a contract.</p>\n<p>A long hopes to profit if the price rises;<br />\na short hopes to profit if the price falls.<br />\nA short is not as simple as selling spot you already hold. It often involves borrowing or a derivatives mechanism.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. A short can involve borrowing or liquidation.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. A short can involve borrowing or liquidation.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>What can a short involve?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Borrowing or liquidation</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. A loss that can never happen</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. No fees</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A long bets on a rise, a short bets on a fall, and a short often means borrowing the coin or using a contract. A long hopes to profit if the price rises; a short hopes to profit if the price falls. A short is not as simple as selling spot you already hold. It often involves borrowing or a derivatives mechanism. Think it through What can a short involve? A. Borrowing or liquidation B. A loss that can never happen C. No fees"}, {"title": "Margin, cross, and isolated", "html": "<p>Cross can drag in the whole account, and isolated can lose only that position's margin.</p>\n<p>Margin is the funds supporting a position.<br />\nIsolated margin limits the margin to a specific position;<br />\ncross margin lets more of the account's available funds support the position.<br />\nThe exact rules depend on the platform and the account mode.</p>\n<p>Cross can put more of the account's funds at risk;<br />\nisolated is not risk-free either.</p>\n<p><strong>Fictional teaching example:</strong> one box for isolated margin, and a shared box for the cross account, with the risk boundary marked.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. Cross margin can affect more of the account's funds.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Cross margin can affect more of the account's funds.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>What can cross margin affect?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. More of the account's funds</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Only one fixed amount</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Zero risk</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Cross can drag in the whole account, and isolated can lose only that position's margin. Margin is the funds supporting a position. Isolated margin limits the margin to a specific position; cross margin lets more of the account's available funds support the position. The exact rules depend on the platform and the account mode. Cross can put more of the account's funds at risk; isolated is not risk-free either. Fictional teaching example: one box for isolated margin, and a shared box for the cross account, with the risk boundary marked. Think it through What can cross margin affect? A. More of the account's funds B. Only one fixed amount C. Zero risk"}, {"title": "Liquidation", "html": "<p>Liquidation depends on whether the margin is enough, not on the leverage multiple alone; the platform closes the position when margin is insufficient.</p>\n<p><strong>When the margin is not enough, the platform closes the position for you. That is a liquidation.</strong></p>\n<p>When margin and the other conditions fall below what the platform requires, the system may reduce or close the position.<br />\nA liquidation trigger usually follows rules such as the mark price, not the last trade price you happen to see.</p>\n<p>Do not treat \"10x leverage means a 10% drop liquidates you\" as an exact formula.<br />\nMaintenance margin, fees, position size, and the platform's rules all change the result.</p>\n<p><strong>Fictional teaching example:</strong> the mark price, maintenance margin, and fees together move the liquidation line.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. Liquidation is not judged by the leverage multiple alone.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Liquidation is not judged by the leverage multiple alone.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Is liquidation judged only by the leverage multiple?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. No</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Yes</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Only by the candle color</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Liquidation depends on whether the margin is enough, not on the leverage multiple alone; the platform closes the position when margin is insufficient. When the margin is not enough, the platform closes the position for you. That is a liquidation. When margin and the other conditions fall below what the platform requires, the system may reduce or close the position. A liquidation trigger usually follows rules such as the mark price, not the last trade price you happen to see. Do not treat \"10x leverage means a 10% drop liquidates you\" as an exact formula. Maintenance margin, fees, position size, and the platform's rules all change the result. Fictional teaching example: the mark price, maintenance margin, and fees together move the liquidation line. Think it through Is liquidation judged only by the leverage multiple? A. No B. Yes C. Only by the candle color"}, {"title": "Funding rate", "html": "<p>The direction of the funding fee is not fixed, and if you hold a contract for a long time that fee keeps changing the balance.</p>\n<p><strong>Hold a perpetual for a long time, and the funding fee can keep eating the profit (sometimes you are the one who receives it).</strong></p>\n<p>A perpetual settles a funding fee on a schedule between long and short holders.<br />\nThe direction, the interval, and the rules differ by platform.<br />\nOn a long hold, that fee clearly changes the result.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. The direction of the funding fee is not fixed.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. The direction of the funding fee is not fixed.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Is the direction of the funding payment fixed?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. It is not fixed</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Longs always pay shorts</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. The platform always pays the user</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "The direction of the funding fee is not fixed, and if you hold a contract for a long time that fee keeps changing the balance. Hold a perpetual for a long time, and the funding fee can keep eating the profit (sometimes you are the one who receives it). A perpetual settles a funding fee on a schedule between long and short holders. The direction, the interval, and the rules differ by platform. On a long hold, that fee clearly changes the result. Think it through Is the direction of the funding payment fixed? A. It is not fixed B. Longs always pay shorts C. The platform always pays the user"}, {"title": "Position size and a risk budget", "html": "<p>Decide the most this trade may lose, then work out how much to buy.</p>\n<p>First decide \"if this judgment is wrong, how much loss am I prepared to take,\" then work out how much to buy.<br />\nThe risk amount here is not the amount you invest, and it is not the margin.</p>\n<p>Suppose the account has <strong>10,000 USDT (often written 10,000 U)</strong>, and the planned risk for this trade is 1%.<br />\nThe calculation has three steps:</p>\n<p><strong>A study rule for risk: set the planned risk of one trade at 1% of the account; a more aggressive cap is 2%.</strong> This is not a safety standard that fits everyone. If you are unsure, use a lower share or do not trade.</p>\n<p>Suppose the account has <strong>10,000 USDT (often written 10,000 U)</strong>, and the planned risk for this trade is 1%.<br />\nThe calculation has three steps:</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1: how much are you allowed to lose?</strong><br />\n10,000 × 1% = <strong>100 USDT</strong>.<br />\nThat is the loss you hope to keep this trade near, if the planned stop is hit.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: if you buy a whole BTC, how much do you lose down to the stop?</strong><br />\nThe plan is to buy at 60,000 USDT and stop at 59,000 USDT.<br />\nThe gap per BTC is 60,000 − 59,000 = <strong>1,000 USDT</strong>.<br />\nSo 1 BTC, bought and sold at those two prices, loses 1,000 USDT;<br />\n0.1 BTC loses 100 USDT.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: so how much should you buy?</strong><br />\nYou are allowed to lose 100 USDT, and each coin might lose 1,000 USDT.<br />\n100 ÷ 1,000 = <strong>0.1 BTC</strong>.<br />\nAt entry, the notional value of this position is 0.1 × 60,000 = <strong>6,000 USDT</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Formula: position size = (account funds × planned risk share for one trade) ÷ |entry price − stop price|.</strong><br />\nThe result is a quantity of the asset, for example a number of BTC;<br />\nmultiply by the entry price to get the notional amount.<br />\nThe absolute value keeps the gap positive for both a buy and a short.<br />\nThis fits a simplified spot or linear-contract calculation. An inverse contract and similar products need their own rules.</p>\n<p><strong>What does leverage change here?</strong><br />\nFor the same 0.1 BTC position, in a simplified case, 2x needs about 3,000 USDT of initial margin, and 10x needs about 600 USDT.<br />\nThe position size and the buy and sell prices are the same, so the profit and loss from the price move is the same. Using only 600 USDT of margin does not make this loss smaller.</p>\n<p>Higher leverage also brings liquidation closer.<br />\nThe planned risk above holds only if the position stays the same, it is not liquidated early, and the stop fills as expected.<br />\nFees, funding, slippage, and a failed execution also change the real loss.<br />\nLeave room in the budget for costs, and check where liquidation sits. Do not treat \"100 U\" as a guaranteed ceiling.</p>\n<p><strong>Fictional teaching example:</strong> 10,000 × 1% = 100;<br />\nenter at 60,000, stop at 59,000, position 0.1 BTC.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. The example position is 0.1 BTC.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. The example position is 0.1 BTC.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>What is the example position?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. 0.1 BTC</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. 1 BTC</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. 10 BTC</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Decide the most this trade may lose, then work out how much to buy. First decide \"if this judgment is wrong, how much loss am I prepared to take,\" then work out how much to buy. The risk amount here is not the amount you invest, and it is not the margin. Suppose the account has 10,000 USDT (often written 10,000 U) , and the planned risk for this trade is 1%. The calculation has three steps: A study rule for risk: set the planned risk of one trade at 1% of the account; a more aggressive cap is 2%. This is not a safety standard that fits everyone. If you are unsure, use a lower share or do not trade. Suppose the account has 10,000 USDT (often written 10,000 U) , and the planned risk for this trade is 1%. The calculation has three steps: Step 1: how much are you allowed to lose? 10,000 × 1% = 100 USDT . That is the loss you hope to keep this trade near, if the planned stop is hit. Step 2: if you buy a whole BTC, how much do you lose down to the stop? The plan is to buy at 60,000 USDT and stop at 59,000 USDT. The gap per BTC is 60,000 − 59,000 = 1,000 USDT . So 1 BTC, bought and sold at those two prices, loses 1,000 USDT; 0.1 BTC loses 100 USDT. Step 3: so how much should you buy? You are allowed to lose 100 USDT, and each coin might lose 1,000 USDT. 100 ÷ 1,000 = 0.1 BTC . At entry, the notional value of this position is 0.1 × 60,000 = 6,000 USDT . Formula: position size = (account funds × planned risk share for one trade) ÷ |entry price − stop price|. The result is a quantity of the asset, for example a number of BTC; multiply by the entry price to get the notional amount. The absolute value keeps the gap positive for both a buy and a short. This fits a simplified spot or linear-contract calculation. An inverse contract and similar products need their own rules. What does leverage change here? For the same 0.1 BTC position, in a simplified case, 2x needs about 3,000 USDT of initial margin, and 10x needs about 600 USDT. The position size and the buy and sell prices are the same, so the profit and loss from the price move is the same. Using only 600 USDT of margin does not make this loss smaller. Higher leverage also brings liquidation closer. The planned risk above holds only if the position stays the same, it is not liquidated early, and the stop fills as expected. Fees, funding, slippage, and a failed execution also change the real loss. Leave room in the budget for costs, and check where liquidation sits. Do not treat \"100 U\" as a guaranteed ceiling. Fictional teaching example: 10,000 × 1% = 100; enter at 60,000, stop at 59,000, position 0.1 BTC. Think it through What is the example position? A. 0.1 BTC B. 1 BTC C. 10 BTC"}, {"title": "Indicators", "html": "<p>An indicator only looks at past data, and it cannot predict tomorrow.</p>\n<p>Volume helps you see how much participation there was.<br />\nSupport and resistance are areas where the market has shown, or might show, buying or selling. They are not a wall that guarantees a turn.</p>\n<p>One breakout, one large up candle, or one bounce cannot by itself confirm the future path.</p>\n<p>EMA: an exponential moving average, a weighted way to watch the price trend.<br />\nRSI: the relative strength index, watching the relative strength of price changes over a period.<br />\nMACD: watching trend and momentum through the relationship of averages and similar inputs.<br />\nStoch RSI: a stochastic calculation applied to RSI, and it is usually more sensitive.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. A high RSI does not mean the price must fall immediately.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. A high RSI does not mean the price must fall immediately.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>If RSI is high, must the price fall immediately?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Not necessarily</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Always</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. It cannot show history</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "An indicator only looks at past data, and it cannot predict tomorrow. Volume helps you see how much participation there was. Support and resistance are areas where the market has shown, or might show, buying or selling. They are not a wall that guarantees a turn. One breakout, one large up candle, or one bounce cannot by itself confirm the future path. EMA: an exponential moving average, a weighted way to watch the price trend. RSI: the relative strength index, watching the relative strength of price changes over a period. MACD: watching trend and momentum through the relationship of averages and similar inputs. Stoch RSI: a stochastic calculation applied to RSI, and it is usually more sensitive. Think it through If RSI is high, must the price fall immediately? A. Not necessarily B. Always C. It cannot show history"}]}, {"id": "08", "title": "Wallets, cold wallets, and CEX", "emoji": "👛", "intro": "Where the coins sit, and who holds the keys", "tags": ["Basics"], "sections": [{"title": "What is a wallet?", "html": "<p><strong>A wallet does not hold the coins. It holds the keys that can move them.</strong></p>\n<p>A wallet helps you manage an account, see assets, and sign actions. The asset record lives on the relevant chain. What the wallet usually manages is the credential that accesses and signs for those assets, not a pile of coins stored inside the phone.</p>\n<p>Different wallets may use a seed phrase, a smart account, multi-party computation, or another recovery method. You need to understand the specific scheme you are using.</p>", "text": "A wallet does not hold the coins. It holds the keys that can move them. A wallet helps you manage an account, see assets, and sign actions. The asset record lives on the relevant chain. What the wallet usually manages is the credential that accesses and signs for those assets, not a pile of coins stored inside the phone. Different wallets may use a seed phrase, a smart account, multi-party computation, or another recovery method. You need to understand the specific scheme you are using."}, {"title": "Address, private key, seed phrase", "html": "<p><strong>You can give someone an address. You cannot give anyone a private key or a seed phrase.</strong></p>\n<p>Address: used to receive funds or to identify an on-chain account. You can share it, but that creates a privacy link.<br />\nPrivate key: the secret that authorizes actions on the account. Do not share it.<br />\nSeed phrase: on some wallets, the word list that generates and restores a set of keys. If it leaks, it can affect several accounts under that phrase.</p>\n<p>A wallet password only protects the local app or file. It is not control of the chain. Changing the app password does not invalidate a seed phrase that has already leaked.</p>", "text": "You can give someone an address. You cannot give anyone a private key or a seed phrase. Address: used to receive funds or to identify an on-chain account. You can share it, but that creates a privacy link. Private key: the secret that authorizes actions on the account. Do not share it. Seed phrase: on some wallets, the word list that generates and restores a set of keys. If it leaks, it can affect several accounts under that phrase. A wallet password only protects the local app or file. It is not control of the chain. Changing the app password does not invalidate a seed phrase that has already leaked."}, {"title": "Two different ways to classify wallets", "html": "<p><strong>Custody is about who holds the keys. Hot and cold are about whether those keys are online.</strong></p>\n<p>Custodial / self-custody: the question is who controls the keys and the actions on the assets.<br />\nHot / cold wallet: the question is whether the keys are in an environment that is online.</p>\n<p>A hardware wallet is a common cold-storage and signing tool. Do not treat \"any hardware device\" as automatically a trustworthy cold wallet.</p>", "text": "Custody is about who holds the keys. Hot and cold are about whether those keys are online. Custodial / self-custody: the question is who controls the keys and the actions on the assets. Hot / cold wallet: the question is whether the keys are in an environment that is online. A hardware wallet is a common cold-storage and signing tool. Do not treat \"any hardware device\" as automatically a trustworthy cold wallet."}, {"title": "CEX, software wallet, hardware wallet", "html": "<p><strong>A platform account is convenient, and you depend on the platform. Self-custody is freer, and the responsibility is yours.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>CEX account</th>\n<th>Self-custody software wallet</th>\n<th>Self-custody hardware wallet</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Who is in control</td>\n<td>Usually the platform holds the assets</td>\n<td>You manage the keys under that wallet's scheme</td>\n<td>You manage them; the device helps you sign</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Day to day</td>\n<td>Trading inside the platform is relatively convenient</td>\n<td>On-chain use is relatively convenient</td>\n<td>Signing has an extra check on the device</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How you recover</td>\n<td>The platform's account recovery and review</td>\n<td>A seed phrase, or that wallet's own recovery</td>\n<td>A backup, plus recovery that is specific to the device</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main risks</td>\n<td>The platform, the account, withdrawal limits, and similar</td>\n<td>Malware, phishing, the backup, and signing the wrong thing</td>\n<td>Supply chain, the backup, a bad signature, and using the device wrong</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What you are responsible for</td>\n<td>Account security and choosing the platform</td>\n<td>Keys, backup, and safe interaction</td>\n<td>Keys, backup, the device, and safe interaction</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>", "text": "A platform account is convenient, and you depend on the platform. Self-custody is freer, and the responsibility is yours. Item CEX account Self-custody software wallet Self-custody hardware wallet Who is in control Usually the platform holds the assets You manage the keys under that wallet's scheme You manage them; the device helps you sign Day to day Trading inside the platform is relatively convenient On-chain use is relatively convenient Signing has an extra check on the device How you recover The platform's account recovery and review A seed phrase, or that wallet's own recovery A backup, plus recovery that is specific to the device Main risks The platform, the account, withdrawal limits, and similar Malware, phishing, the backup, and signing the wrong thing Supply chain, the backup, a bad signature, and using the device wrong What you are responsible for Account security and choosing the platform Keys, backup, and safe interaction Keys, backup, the device, and safe interaction"}, {"title": "A keyless wallet beginners can start with 🧸", "html": "<p><strong>Don't want to write down a seed phrase? A keyless wallet splits the key into three shares. That can be easier for a beginner.</strong></p>\n<p>Worried you will mishandle a private key, or lose a seed phrase? Look at Binance Wallet (keyless).</p>\n<p>It uses MPC (multi-party computation). The key is split into 3 shares: one on your device, one in a personal cloud backup, and one held by Binance. Any two shares together can use the wallet. Two of them are in your hands, so <strong>Binance alone cannot move your money</strong>. You do not write down a traditional seed phrase when you create it.</p>\n<p>Creating Binance Wallet (keyless) requires a Binance account that has already passed identity checks. It can also import an existing seed phrase or private-key wallet, and you can convert the keyless wallet into a private-key wallet and hold that yourself.</p>\n<h3>How is it different from a Binance exchange account?</h3>\n<p>The exchange account is for buying, selling, and managing funds inside the platform. Binance Wallet (keyless) is for holding on-chain assets, transferring them, and using Web3 apps. You can reach both from the Binance app. They are different ways of holding assets.</p>\n<h3>How do you start?</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Open the official Binance app and go to Wallet.</li>\n<li>Choose Create wallet, and follow the prompts to create a keyless wallet.</li>\n<li>Finish the backup. Binance officially offers quick backup, cloud backup, and QR backup. Finish the one you chose as the app instructs. Confirm which accounts, passwords, or materials recovery needs. Then deposit assets.</li>\n<li>Practice receiving, sending, and on-chain actions with a small amount first.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Note: an encrypted backup in the wallet's official flow is not the same as screenshotting a seed phrase and uploading it to a cloud drive. Still do not photograph, screenshot, or upload a seed phrase or a private key.</p>\n<p>Sources: <a class=\"src\" href=\"https://www.binance.com/en/support/faq/detail/048ee79532494c03918dc4004214ad11\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://www.binance.com/en/support/faq/detail/048ee79532494c03918dc4004214ad11\">Binance FAQ: What is Binance Wallet ↗</a>, <a class=\"src\" href=\"https://www.binance.com/en/blog/ecosystem/7898832595548730429\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://www.binance.com/en/blog/ecosystem/7898832595548730429\">Binance blog: self-custody Web3 wallet ↗</a></p>\n<div data-widget=\"binancewallet\"></div>", "text": "Don't want to write down a seed phrase? A keyless wallet splits the key into three shares. That can be easier for a beginner. Worried you will mishandle a private key, or lose a seed phrase? Look at Binance Wallet (keyless). It uses MPC (multi-party computation). The key is split into 3 shares: one on your device, one in a personal cloud backup, and one held by Binance. Any two shares together can use the wallet. Two of them are in your hands, so Binance alone cannot move your money. You do not write down a traditional seed phrase when you create it. Creating Binance Wallet (keyless) requires a Binance account that has already passed identity checks. It can also import an existing seed phrase or private-key wallet, and you can convert the keyless wallet into a private-key wallet and hold that yourself. ### How is it different from a Binance exchange account? The exchange account is for buying, selling, and managing funds inside the platform. Binance Wallet (keyless) is for holding on-chain assets, transferring them, and using Web3 apps. You can reach both from the Binance app. They are different ways of holding assets. ### How do you start? 1. Open the official Binance app and go to Wallet. 2. Choose Create wallet, and follow the prompts to create a keyless wallet. 3. Finish the backup. Binance officially offers quick backup, cloud backup, and QR backup. Finish the one you chose as the app instructs. Confirm which accounts, passwords, or materials recovery needs. Then deposit assets. 4. Practice receiving, sending, and on-chain actions with a small amount first. Note: an encrypted backup in the wallet's official flow is not the same as screenshotting a seed phrase and uploading it to a cloud drive. Still do not photograph, screenshot, or upload a seed phrase or a private key. Sources: [Binance FAQ: What is Binance Wallet](https://www.binance.com/en/support/faq/detail/048ee79532494c03918dc4004214ad11), [Binance blog: self-custody Web3 wallet](https://www.binance.com/en/blog/ecosystem/7898832595548730429) Buttons: binance-wallet"}, {"title": "Do you need a hardware wallet?", "html": "<p><strong>You do not have to buy a hardware wallet on day one. Learn to back up, and learn to read what you are signing.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>You do not need to buy one the moment you arrive. Protecting the account, backing up correctly, and understanding signatures matter more at the start.</strong></p>\n<p>If you are only learning with a small amount, and you trade fairly often, you may not need a hardware wallet immediately. You can start with a platform account or a software wallet that fits the job, and understand the custody and device risks of each.</p>\n<p>Whether you need one should not be decided by a single line like \"only if you are worth hundreds of millions\". If an amount already matters a lot to you, and you plan to self-custody it for a long time, it is worth studying a hardware wallet even when the amount is far below that.</p>\n<p>A hardware wallet can reduce some of the risk of keys touching an online environment. It is not a magic safe. A leaked backup, a swapped device, or you actively signing a malicious action can still lose the assets.</p>\n<p><strong>What decides it is how much that sum matters to you, how long you will hold it, and whether you can operate the setup. Not one universal money threshold.</strong></p>", "text": "You do not have to buy a hardware wallet on day one. Learn to back up, and learn to read what you are signing. You do not need to buy one the moment you arrive. Protecting the account, backing up correctly, and understanding signatures matter more at the start. If you are only learning with a small amount, and you trade fairly often, you may not need a hardware wallet immediately. You can start with a platform account or a software wallet that fits the job, and understand the custody and device risks of each. Whether you need one should not be decided by a single line like \"only if you are worth hundreds of millions\". If an amount already matters a lot to you, and you plan to self-custody it for a long time, it is worth studying a hardware wallet even when the amount is far below that. A hardware wallet can reduce some of the risk of keys touching an online environment. It is not a magic safe. A leaked backup, a swapped device, or you actively signing a malicious action can still lose the assets. What decides it is how much that sum matters to you, how long you will hold it, and whether you can operate the setup. Not one universal money threshold."}, {"title": "How do you separate funds?", "html": "<p><strong>Practice, trading, and long-term storage belong in different wallets. Do not keep them together.</strong></p>\n<p>Learning / interaction wallet: a small amount you can afford to lose, for trying apps.<br />\nEveryday trading funds: kept where you trade, with the custody risk understood.<br />\nLong-term savings wallet: fewer interactions, and a storage setup that fits you.</p>\n<p>Creating several accounts under the same seed phrase does not isolate you from a leaked phrase. To separate that risk, you need an independent key or recovery scheme.</p>", "text": "Practice, trading, and long-term storage belong in different wallets. Do not keep them together. Learning / interaction wallet: a small amount you can afford to lose, for trying apps. Everyday trading funds: kept where you trade, with the custody risk understood. Long-term savings wallet: fewer interactions, and a storage setup that fits you. Creating several accounts under the same seed phrase does not isolate you from a leaked phrase. To separate that risk, you need an independent key or recovery scheme."}, {"title": "What if the device is lost?", "html": "<p><strong>A lost phone is not the scary part. Having no backup is.</strong></p>\n<p>Losing the device does not automatically mean the assets are gone. Whether you can recover depends on the backup and the wallet's scheme. <strong>So finish the backup when you create the wallet, and confirm by the official method that the backup can actually restore it. Do not wait until the phone breaks or the device is gone.</strong> For a seed-phrase wallet, the words, the order, and the related recovery details all have to be exact. Restore in a trusted environment, following the official instructions. If you suspect someone else has the device or the backup, consider moving what remains to an account that uses a brand-new key.</p>", "text": "A lost phone is not the scary part. Having no backup is. Losing the device does not automatically mean the assets are gone. Whether you can recover depends on the backup and the wallet's scheme. So finish the backup when you create the wallet, and confirm by the official method that the backup can actually restore it. Do not wait until the phone breaks or the device is gone. For a seed-phrase wallet, the words, the order, and the related recovery details all have to be exact. Restore in a trusted environment, following the official instructions. If you suspect someone else has the device or the backup, consider moving what remains to an account that uses a brand-new key."}]}, {"id": "09", "title": "Safety and scam guide", "emoji": "🛡️", "intro": "Protect your account, seed phrase, and approvals", "tags": ["Must-read"], "tip": "Note: Safety is not just one cold wallet. It is a set of habits you keep.", "sections": [{"title": "Seed phrase and private key: do not share, do not upload", "html": "<p><strong>Do not give a seed phrase or a private key to anyone, and real support will not ask for it either.</strong></p>\n<p>Do not send it to support, to a friend, to a project, or to an AI.<br />\nDo not type it into any web form.<br />\nDo not photograph it and upload it to a cloud drive, and do not paste it into a chat tool or an online note.</p>\n<p>Back it up with the offline method the wallet officially supports.<br />\nAlso think about fire, water, loss, and the chance that someone else could reach that backup.</p>\n<p>Real support does not need your seed phrase to look up a transaction.<br />\nIf anyone asks for it, stop, and do not keep talking.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Real support does not need a seed phrase. If someone asks, stop the conversation.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Do not give a seed phrase to support, and do not photograph it and upload it first. If someone asks, stop.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Someone claiming to be support says they must have your seed phrase before they can look up your transaction. Should you give it?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes, or they cannot look it up</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. Real support does not need a seed phrase</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Photograph it, upload it to a cloud drive, then send it</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Do not give a seed phrase or a private key to anyone, and real support will not ask for it either. Do not send it to support, to a friend, to a project, or to an AI. Do not type it into any web form. Do not photograph it and upload it to a cloud drive, and do not paste it into a chat tool or an online note. Back it up with the offline method the wallet officially supports. Also think about fire, water, loss, and the chance that someone else could reach that backup. Real support does not need your seed phrase to look up a transaction. If anyone asks for it, stop, and do not keep talking. Think it through Someone claiming to be support says they must have your seed phrase before they can look up your transaction. Should you give it? A. Yes, or they cannot look it up B. No. Real support does not need a seed phrase C. Photograph it, upload it to a cloud drive, then send it"}, {"title": "Fake sites and fake apps", "html": "<p><strong>Links in search results and ads can be fake, so use only an official entrance you have already checked.</strong></p>\n<p>Search results, ads, social accounts, and pinned group messages can all carry lookalike links.<br />\nRead the full domain first, then cross-check it with a trusted official channel.<br />\nOnce an entrance is confirmed, save it and use that one later.</p>\n<p>When you download an app, check the developer and the source.<br />\nDo not install a package a stranger sends you in private.<br />\nBuy a hardware wallet from the official channel, or from a channel you can verify.<br />\nDo not use a seed phrase that someone else set up in advance.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. An ad rank or a search rank does not prove it is official. Check the domain and the developer, and use an entrance you have already verified.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Ranking first, or a package sent in private, does not make it official. Check the full domain and the developer first.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>An \"official download\" sits at the top of a search ad. Can you install it directly?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. First place means it is official</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. Check the full domain and the developer, and use only an entrance you have already verified</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. An installer a stranger sends you is safer</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Links in search results and ads can be fake, so use only an official entrance you have already checked. Search results, ads, social accounts, and pinned group messages can all carry lookalike links. Read the full domain first, then cross-check it with a trusted official channel. Once an entrance is confirmed, save it and use that one later. When you download an app, check the developer and the source. Do not install a package a stranger sends you in private. Buy a hardware wallet from the official channel, or from a channel you can verify. Do not use a seed phrase that someone else set up in advance. Think it through An \"official download\" sits at the top of a search ad. Can you install it directly? A. Yes. First place means it is official B. No. Check the full domain and the developer, and use only an entrance you have already verified C. An installer a stranger sends you is safer"}, {"title": "Connecting, signing, and approving are not the same thing", "html": "<p><strong>Connecting a wallet does not usually move the coins, but a careless signature or a careless approval can.</strong></p>\n<p>Connecting a wallet usually lets an app see the address and request a permission.<br />\nThat does not by itself mean the assets have already been moved.</p>\n<p>A signature confirms a piece of information or one action.<br />\nSome off-chain signatures can also authorize a later transfer of assets.<br />\nDo not treat \"no gas fee\" as \"no risk\".</p>\n<p>An approval lets a contract use an asset under conditions, for example an ERC-20 token allowance.<br />\nDifferent networks and different assets use different permission mechanisms.</p>\n<p>If you cannot read what the request is asking, do not confirm it.<br />\nDo not sign blindly.<br />\nA page titled \"log in\" does not mean it is only a login.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>📰 <strong>A reported case: one signature, and about 1.39 million dollars was gone (October 2024)</strong></p>\n<p>A PEPE holder signed an off-chain Uniswap Permit2 signature on a phishing site (it cost no gas).<br />\nAbout an hour later, PEPE and other tokens in the wallet, worth about 1.39 million dollars, were transferred out.<br />\nAround October 10 of the same month, someone else lost 15,079 fwDETH to a Permit phishing signature (then worth about 35 to 36 million dollars).</p>\n<p><strong>The lesson: a signature that costs no gas can still move the coins. If you cannot read it, do not sign.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. No gas fee does not mean no risk. If you cannot read the request, do not confirm it.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. No gas fee, or a title that says log in, does not prove it is safe. If you cannot read it, do not sign.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>A signature costs no gas. Can you treat it as safe?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. If it costs no gas, it cannot touch the coins</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. Some off-chain signatures can still authorize assets to be moved</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. If the page title says log in, it is definitely only a login</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Connecting a wallet does not usually move the coins, but a careless signature or a careless approval can. Connecting a wallet usually lets an app see the address and request a permission. That does not by itself mean the assets have already been moved. A signature confirms a piece of information or one action. Some off-chain signatures can also authorize a later transfer of assets. Do not treat \"no gas fee\" as \"no risk\". An approval lets a contract use an asset under conditions, for example an ERC-20 token allowance. Different networks and different assets use different permission mechanisms. If you cannot read what the request is asking, do not confirm it. Do not sign blindly. A page titled \"log in\" does not mean it is only a login. 📰 A reported case: one signature, and about 1.39 million dollars was gone (October 2024) A PEPE holder signed an off-chain Uniswap Permit2 signature on a phishing site (it cost no gas). About an hour later, PEPE and other tokens in the wallet, worth about 1.39 million dollars, were transferred out. Around October 10 of the same month, someone else lost 15,079 fwDETH to a Permit phishing signature (then worth about 35 to 36 million dollars). The lesson: a signature that costs no gas can still move the coins. If you cannot read it, do not sign. Think it through A signature costs no gas. Can you treat it as safe? A. Yes. If it costs no gas, it cannot touch the coins B. No. Some off-chain signatures can still authorize assets to be moved C. If the page title says log in, it is definitely only a login"}, {"title": "What can help you inspect a signature?", "html": "<p><strong>A security extension can warn you, and you still have to understand the request yourself.</strong></p>\n<p>GoPlus can help check tokens, approvals, and some on-chain risks.<br />\nThe result is only a reference. It cannot guarantee that assets are absolutely safe.<br />\nIt also offers a browser extension that, in the situations it supports, flags suspicious requests.<br />\nThe wallet's own transaction preview and risk warnings are worth reading too.</p>\n<p><strong>GoPlus official entrance:</strong> <a class=\"ext\" href=\"https://gopluslabs.io/en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://gopluslabs.io/en\">https://gopluslabs.io/en</a><span class=\"ext-meta\"><span class=\"domain\">🌐 gopluslabs.io</span><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://gopluslabs.io/en\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></span></p>\n<p>From the official site, open the browser-extension page.<br />\nCheck the publisher, the wallets it works with, the networks, the permissions, and what it currently supports.<br />\nA signature-decoding API and a browser extension are not the same feature.<br />\nDo not assume the extension supports every chain and every kind of signature.</p>\n<p>A tool's warning is only a helper.<br />\nNo warning does not mean it is definitely safe.<br />\nYou still have to check the counterparty, the change in assets, and the scope of the approval.<br />\nNo check requires you to give the tool your seed phrase.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. No warning is only a helper. You still have to check the counterparty, the change in assets, and the scope of the approval yourself.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. No warning does not mean it is safe. Do not give a seed phrase to a checking tool.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>The checking extension showed no warning. Is this signature definitely safe?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. No warning means you can sign</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. The tool is only a helper, and no warning does not mean it is definitely safe</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Give the extension your seed phrase, and then it can check thoroughly</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A security extension can warn you, and you still have to understand the request yourself. GoPlus can help check tokens, approvals, and some on-chain risks. The result is only a reference. It cannot guarantee that assets are absolutely safe. It also offers a browser extension that, in the situations it supports, flags suspicious requests. The wallet's own transaction preview and risk warnings are worth reading too. GoPlus official entrance: https://gopluslabs.io/en 🌐 gopluslabs.io ⏳ Not checked yet From the official site, open the browser-extension page. Check the publisher, the wallets it works with, the networks, the permissions, and what it currently supports. A signature-decoding API and a browser extension are not the same feature. Do not assume the extension supports every chain and every kind of signature. A tool's warning is only a helper. No warning does not mean it is definitely safe. You still have to check the counterparty, the change in assets, and the scope of the approval. No check requires you to give the tool your seed phrase. Think it through The checking extension showed no warning. Is this signature definitely safe? A. Yes. No warning means you can sign B. No. The tool is only a helper, and no warning does not mean it is definitely safe C. Give the extension your seed phrase, and then it can check thoroughly"}, {"title": "Disconnecting is not the same as revoking", "html": "<p><strong>After you disconnect a site, an approval you already gave is still there, and it has to be revoked separately.</strong></p>\n<p>Disconnecting only changes the current connection.<br />\nAn approval already written on-chain is still valid.<br />\nCheck it and revoke it on the matching chain, in the matching wallet.</p>\n<p>A revoke may cost a network fee.<br />\nIt also cannot guarantee that assets already moved will come back.<br />\nIf the seed phrase or private key has already leaked, revoking an approval is not enough.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Disconnecting does not cancel an on-chain approval. The approval has to be revoked on its own. If the private key has leaked, revoking alone is not enough.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Disconnecting is not the same as revoking an approval. After a private key leaks, revoking an approval does not solve it.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>After you disconnect a site in the wallet, is the old approval still there?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. No. Disconnecting is the same as revoking</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Yes. An approval has to be revoked separately, on the matching chain and in the matching wallet</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. If the private key has already leaked, revoking the approval is enough</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "After you disconnect a site, an approval you already gave is still there, and it has to be revoked separately. Disconnecting only changes the current connection. An approval already written on-chain is still valid. Check it and revoke it on the matching chain, in the matching wallet. A revoke may cost a network fee. It also cannot guarantee that assets already moved will come back. If the seed phrase or private key has already leaked, revoking an approval is not enough. Think it through After you disconnect a site in the wallet, is the old approval still there? A. No. Disconnecting is the same as revoking B. Yes. An approval has to be revoked separately, on the matching chain and in the matching wallet C. If the private key has already leaked, revoking the approval is enough"}, {"title": "Unlimited approval", "html": "<p><strong>An unlimited approval saves one confirmation, and if something goes wrong, a large amount of that coin can be moved.</strong></p>\n<p>An unlimited approval lets the other side use a very large token allowance, without a new approval each time.<br />\nIt is more convenient, and it also magnifies the loss from a contract bug or a malicious permission.<br />\nRead the scope of the permission first, then approve only the amount you need.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. The convenience costs you a very large allowance. The other side, or a contract with a bug, may use far more than this one trade.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. An unlimited approval is not risk-free, and it does not revoke itself. It lets the other side use a very large allowance.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>What is the main difference between an unlimited approval and approving a specific amount each time?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. It only saves one click, with no extra risk</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. It saves one approval, but the other side may use a very large allowance</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. An unlimited approval revokes itself the same day</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "An unlimited approval saves one confirmation, and if something goes wrong, a large amount of that coin can be moved. An unlimited approval lets the other side use a very large token allowance, without a new approval each time. It is more convenient, and it also magnifies the loss from a contract bug or a malicious permission. Read the scope of the permission first, then approve only the amount you need. Think it through What is the main difference between an unlimited approval and approving a specific amount each time? A. It only saves one click, with no extra risk B. It saves one approval, but the other side may use a very large allowance C. An unlimited approval revokes itself the same day"}, {"title": "Common scams", "html": "<p><strong>If a stranger starts by asking you to sign, to pay, or to hand over a seed phrase, treat it as a scam first.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fake airdrop: it asks you to sign, approve, or pay a fee first.</li>\n<li>Fake support: they message you first, then ask you to install remote software or hand over recovery information.</li>\n<li>Fake investment group: they show profit screenshots and ask you to send funds to a named platform.</li>\n<li>Cannot withdraw: they say you must pay more tax, an unfreeze fee, or a deposit before you can withdraw.</li>\n<li>Fake recovery: they claim stolen funds can definitely be recovered, and they want to be paid first.</li>\n<li>Address poisoning: a lookalike address is placed in your history so you will copy it.</li>\n<li>Malicious tokens and NFTs: the name or the note contains a claim link, and it pushes you to open it.</li>\n<li>Clipboard hijack: malware replaces the address you just copied with someone else's address.</li>\n<li>A third party reading the clipboard: an app or extension may read recovery information, a password, or a key you copied. Permissions and prompts differ by system.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A strange asset showing up in the wallet does not mean you have to do something with it.<br />\nDo not open a link inside it just because the page shows a high value.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A strange asset can sit there. A high displayed value is not a reason to open the link.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Do not open the link in the note, and do not approve it just to see a balance. A strange asset can be left alone.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>A strange coin suddenly appears in the wallet, shows a high value, and includes a claim link. What should you do?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Open the link and claim the coin</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Leave it alone, and do not open the link just because the value looks high</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Give that coin an unlimited approval immediately, or you cannot see the real balance</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "If a stranger starts by asking you to sign, to pay, or to hand over a seed phrase, treat it as a scam first. Fake airdrop: it asks you to sign, approve, or pay a fee first. Fake support: they message you first, then ask you to install remote software or hand over recovery information. Fake investment group: they show profit screenshots and ask you to send funds to a named platform. Cannot withdraw: they say you must pay more tax, an unfreeze fee, or a deposit before you can withdraw. Fake recovery: they claim stolen funds can definitely be recovered, and they want to be paid first. Address poisoning: a lookalike address is placed in your history so you will copy it. Malicious tokens and NFTs: the name or the note contains a claim link, and it pushes you to open it. Clipboard hijack: malware replaces the address you just copied with someone else's address. A third party reading the clipboard: an app or extension may read recovery information, a password, or a key you copied. Permissions and prompts differ by system. A strange asset showing up in the wallet does not mean you have to do something with it. Do not open a link inside it just because the page shows a high value. Think it through A strange coin suddenly appears in the wallet, shows a high value, and includes a claim link. What should you do? A. Open the link and claim the coin B. Leave it alone, and do not open the link just because the value looks high C. Give that coin an unlimited approval immediately, or you cannot see the real balance"}, {"title": "Three traps that show up constantly in crypto", "html": "<p><strong>Dirty-money freezes, pig-butchering scams, and letting someone use your account to collect funds are three of the most common traps.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1. Dirty money and a frozen card</strong></p>\n<p>In a P2P trade, if the money you receive has a dirty source, the bank card can be frozen, and you can be pulled into a case.<br />\nTrade only with merchants the platform has verified.<br />\nDo not take a payment from a third party, and do not collect money for someone else.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Pig-butchering</strong></p>\n<p>The other person starts with a personal relationship, or pulls you into a \"signals group\", and then steers you to deposit on a platform.<br />\nAt first you see a small profit. Once the amount is larger, you cannot withdraw.<br />\nIf someone you met online offers to invest for you, treat it as a scam first.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Buying your coins at a premium, and running funds through your account</strong></p>\n<p>Someone offers a high price for your coins, or asks to borrow your bank card or account to receive and forward money.<br />\nThat can be using your account to move money from a bad source.<br />\nHelping move funds, or lending a bank card to receive coins, can be the crime of assisting a criminal information network.<br />\nIn a serious case it can also be concealing or hiding criminal proceeds.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A premium offer for your coins, plus lending a bank card to receive funds, is not a normal trade. It can be the crime of assisting a criminal network, and in a serious case it can involve concealing criminal proceeds.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. This is not a normal P2P trade with a verified merchant, and it is not a risk-free favor. Lending an account to receive funds can be illegal.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Someone offers a high price for your coins and also asks to borrow your bank card to receive the money. Which situation is this closer to?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. A normal P2P trade with a merchant the platform verified</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. The risk of letting someone run funds through your account. Lending an account to receive money can involve assisting a crime</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Just helping a friend, with no legal risk</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Dirty-money freezes, pig-butchering scams, and letting someone use your account to collect funds are three of the most common traps. 1. Dirty money and a frozen card In a P2P trade, if the money you receive has a dirty source, the bank card can be frozen, and you can be pulled into a case. Trade only with merchants the platform has verified. Do not take a payment from a third party, and do not collect money for someone else. 2. Pig-butchering The other person starts with a personal relationship, or pulls you into a \"signals group\", and then steers you to deposit on a platform. At first you see a small profit. Once the amount is larger, you cannot withdraw. If someone you met online offers to invest for you, treat it as a scam first. 3. Buying your coins at a premium, and running funds through your account Someone offers a high price for your coins, or asks to borrow your bank card or account to receive and forward money. That can be using your account to move money from a bad source. Helping move funds, or lending a bank card to receive coins, can be the crime of assisting a criminal information network. In a serious case it can also be concealing or hiding criminal proceeds. Think it through Someone offers a high price for your coins and also asks to borrow your bank card to receive the money. Which situation is this closer to? A. A normal P2P trade with a merchant the platform verified B. The risk of letting someone run funds through your account. Lending an account to receive money can involve assisting a crime C. Just helping a friend, with no legal risk"}, {"title": "Stop the clipboard from leaking", "html": "<p><strong>Do not copy a seed phrase or a private key, and do not paste one into any chat or web page.</strong></p>\n<p>Do not copy a seed phrase, a private key, or an API key onto the clipboard.<br />\nDo not paste them into a chat, an AI, an online note, or a web page.<br />\nFor passwords, prefer autofill from a password manager you trust.</p>\n<p>Remove apps, keyboards, and browser extensions you do not need or cannot trace.<br />\nCheck clipboard, accessibility, and device permissions.<br />\nTurn off clipboard history, cloud sync, and sharing across devices when you do not need them.<br />\nKeep the system updated.</p>\n<p>Clearing the clipboard only reduces the chance it will be read again later.<br />\nIt cannot take back something that has already been read.<br />\nIf you suspect a secret has leaked, update the matching credential.<br />\nIf a seed phrase or private key has leaked, handle it as in Chapter 15.</p>\n<p>For how to guard against a replaced address, see Chapter 10, \"Four checks before you send\".</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Clearing the clipboard cannot take back something that was already read. A leaked seed phrase has to be handled as in Chapter 15.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Clearing it cannot undo a leak that already happened. Do not paste the seed phrase into a chat or a web page again.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Someone already read the seed phrase from the clipboard. If you clear the clipboard now, does that undo the leak?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. Clearing it means it never leaked</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. Clearing it only reduces the chance it will be read again later</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Paste the seed phrase into a chat once more, to check it, and that is safer</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Do not copy a seed phrase or a private key, and do not paste one into any chat or web page. Do not copy a seed phrase, a private key, or an API key onto the clipboard. Do not paste them into a chat, an AI, an online note, or a web page. For passwords, prefer autofill from a password manager you trust. Remove apps, keyboards, and browser extensions you do not need or cannot trace. Check clipboard, accessibility, and device permissions. Turn off clipboard history, cloud sync, and sharing across devices when you do not need them. Keep the system updated. Clearing the clipboard only reduces the chance it will be read again later. It cannot take back something that has already been read. If you suspect a secret has leaked, update the matching credential. If a seed phrase or private key has leaked, handle it as in Chapter 15. For how to guard against a replaced address, see Chapter 10, \"Four checks before you send\". Think it through Someone already read the seed phrase from the clipboard. If you clear the clipboard now, does that undo the leak? A. Yes. Clearing it means it never leaked B. No. Clearing it only reduces the chance it will be read again later C. Paste the seed phrase into a chat once more, to check it, and that is safer"}, {"title": "Protect the account and the device", "html": "<p><strong>If the email and the phone are held, the account has lost most of its front doors.</strong></p>\n<p>Protect the email.<br />\nUse a password that is not reused, and turn on a reliable second factor.<br />\nUpdate the system and the wallet.<br />\nDo not install a plugin you cannot trace.<br />\nDo not show recovery information on a shared screen.<br />\nIf the exchange offers a withdrawal allowlist and login alerts, turn them on.</p>\n<p>An API key is a secret too.<br />\nIf you automate later, use the smallest permission.<br />\nA trading bot usually does not need withdrawal permission.<br />\nDo not send the key to a stranger's bot, and do not publish it in a code repository.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A trading bot usually does not need withdrawal permission. Do not give the key to a stranger's bot, and do not publish it in a repository.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Do not turn on withdrawal permission just to save trouble, and do not publish the key or give it to a stranger's bot.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Does an API key used by a trading bot usually need withdrawal permission turned on?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes, or the bot cannot be used</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Usually not. Use the smallest permission, and do not send the key to a stranger's bot</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Publish the key in a code repository, so you have a backup</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "If the email and the phone are held, the account has lost most of its front doors. Protect the email. Use a password that is not reused, and turn on a reliable second factor. Update the system and the wallet. Do not install a plugin you cannot trace. Do not show recovery information on a shared screen. If the exchange offers a withdrawal allowlist and login alerts, turn them on. An API key is a secret too. If you automate later, use the smallest permission. A trading bot usually does not need withdrawal permission. Do not send the key to a stranger's bot, and do not publish it in a code repository. Think it through Does an API key used by a trading bot usually need withdrawal permission turned on? A. Yes, or the bot cannot be used B. Usually not. Use the smallest permission, and do not send the key to a stranger's bot C. Publish the key in a code repository, so you have a backup"}, {"title": "Privacy needs protecting too", "html": "<p><strong>Many people can look up an on-chain transfer, and a wallet that does not show a real name does not mean you cannot be found.</strong></p>\n<p>Transactions on many public chains can be looked up in public.<br />\nA public address can be tied to a balance, to habits, and to an identity.<br />\nThe wallet screen not showing a real name does not mean the record cannot be traced.</p>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. No real name on the screen does not mean the on-chain record is private. A public address can still be tied to behavior and to a person.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Many records on a public chain can be looked up. A wallet that does not show a real name is not the same as being untraceable.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>The wallet does not show a real name. Does that mean an on-chain transfer cannot be traced?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. No real name means it cannot be found</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. The record can be looked up in public, and the address can still be tied to a person</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Only an exchange account's records can be seen by anyone else</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Many people can look up an on-chain transfer, and a wallet that does not show a real name does not mean you cannot be found. Transactions on many public chains can be looked up in public. A public address can be tied to a balance, to habits, and to an identity. The wallet screen not showing a real name does not mean the record cannot be traced. Note: Safety is not just one cold wallet. It is a set of habits you keep. Think it through The wallet does not show a real name. Does that mean an on-chain transfer cannot be traced? A. Yes. No real name means it cannot be found B. No. The record can be looked up in public, and the address can still be tied to a person C. Only an exchange account's records can be seen by anyone else"}]}, {"id": "10", "title": "Your first on-chain actions", "emoji": "🔗", "intro": "Receive, send, gas, swaps, and bridges", "tags": ["How-to"], "tip": "Note: For a first on-chain practice, one correct small receive and one correct small send matter more than rushing to bridge.", "sections": [{"title": "Receiving", "html": "<p><strong>To receive, you give an address and a network. You do not give a seed phrase.</strong></p>\n<p>Open a wallet or platform you trust. Select the correct asset and network. Copy the receiving address. If a Memo / Tag is required, give that too. Confirm the sender supports that asset and that network.</p>\n<p>Do not give anyone a seed phrase in order to receive funds. Receiving usually needs only the public receiving details.</p>", "text": "To receive, you give an address and a network. You do not give a seed phrase. Open a wallet or platform you trust. Select the correct asset and network. Copy the receiving address. If a Memo / Tag is required, give that too. Confirm the sender supports that asset and that network. Do not give anyone a seed phrase in order to receive funds. Receiving usually needs only the public receiving details."}, {"title": "Four checks before you send", "html": "<p><strong>Before you send, check four things: asset, network, address, and Memo.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Asset: the name matches the real contract or the real native asset.</li>\n<li>Network: the recipient explicitly supports the network you selected.</li>\n<li>Address: check the full address against a source you trust, so a swapped clipboard does not win. After you paste, check the pasted result, and check again on the wallet or exchange confirmation screen. The first and last few characters are not enough. A lookalike address can copy those on purpose.<br />\n   If you are unsure, you can scan a receiving QR code from a channel the recipient actually controls, which skips some copy-and-paste. After the scan, still check the full address, the network, and whether the code prefilled an amount or some other action. Scanning does not rule out a fake or malicious QR code.</li>\n<li>Memo / Tag: if the receiving platform requires it, it has to be correct.</li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote>\n<p>📰 <strong>A real case: address poisoning, one wrong send of 68 million dollars (May 2024)</strong><br />\nThe scammer first sent the victim a tiny transfer, so a fake address with <strong>the same first 6 and last 6 characters as the real one</strong> landed in the transfer history. The victim copied that fake address and sent 1,155 WBTC (about 68 million dollars). After negotiation the scammer returned most of it. That outcome is extremely rare. In almost every other case the funds are not recovered.<br />\n<strong>The lesson: do not copy an address out of your transfer history. Check the full address every time.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Also check the minimum deposit, the fees, whether the network is paused, and how many confirmations are required. A small test transfer has to be above the relevant minimum. Move the rest only after it arrives.</p>", "text": "Before you send, check four things: asset, network, address, and Memo. Asset: the name matches the real contract or the real native asset. Network: the recipient explicitly supports the network you selected. Address: check the full address against a source you trust, so a swapped clipboard does not win. After you paste, check the pasted result, and check again on the wallet or exchange confirmation screen. The first and last few characters are not enough. A lookalike address can copy those on purpose. If you are unsure, you can scan a receiving QR code from a channel the recipient actually controls, which skips some copy-and-paste. After the scan, still check the full address, the network, and whether the code prefilled an amount or some other action. Scanning does not rule out a fake or malicious QR code. Memo / Tag: if the receiving platform requires it, it has to be correct. 📰 A real case: address poisoning, one wrong send of 68 million dollars (May 2024) The scammer first sent the victim a tiny transfer, so a fake address with the same first 6 and last 6 characters as the real one landed in the transfer history. The victim copied that fake address and sent 1,155 WBTC (about 68 million dollars). After negotiation the scammer returned most of it. That outcome is extremely rare. In almost every other case the funds are not recovered. The lesson: do not copy an address out of your transfer history. Check the full address every time. Also check the minimum deposit, the fees, whether the network is paused, and how many confirmations are required. A small test transfer has to be above the relevant minimum. Move the rest only after it arrives."}, {"title": "Why does the same coin have different networks?", "html": "<p><strong>USDT with the same name on a different network is a different thing. Do not pick the wrong one.</strong></p>\n<p>The name can be USDT,<br />\nand the network can still differ.</p>\n<p>Whichever network the recipient chose,<br />\nthat is the network you choose.</p>\n<p>Do not pick one<br />\nonly because the fee looks cheaper.</p>\n<p>An asset with the same name can be issued on different chains, and there are bridged or wrapped versions. The same name does not mean the same contract, the same issuer, or the same redemption path.</p>\n<p>TRON's TRC-20 and Ethereum's ERC-20 are token standards on different networks.</p>", "text": "USDT with the same name on a different network is a different thing. Do not pick the wrong one. The name can be USDT, and the network can still differ. Whichever network the recipient chose, that is the network you choose. Do not pick one only because the fee looks cheaper. An asset with the same name can be issued on different chains, and there are bridged or wrapped versions. The same name does not mean the same contract, the same issuer, or the same redemption path. TRON's TRC-20 and Ethereum's ERC-20 are token standards on different networks."}, {"title": "Address and contract address", "html": "<p><strong>A contract address is the token's identity. It is not your receiving address.</strong></p>\n<p>A receiving address is the account that receives funds. A contract address identifies a token or a contract. When you buy a token you may use the contract address to check identity. For an ordinary transfer, do not paste the token's contract address in as if it were your own receiving address.</p>", "text": "A contract address is the token's identity. It is not your receiving address. A receiving address is the account that receives funds. A contract address identifies a token or a contract. When you buy a token you may use the contract address to check identity. For an ordinary transfer, do not paste the token's contract address in as if it were your own receiving address."}, {"title": "Gas and network resources", "html": "<p><strong>Sending costs a network fee. Tokens with no gas beside them still cannot move.</strong></p>\n<h3>Why does the wallet show coins, and still refuse to send?</h3>\n<p>Sending also pays a \"fare\",<br />\nwhich is the network fee.</p>\n<p>To send USDT on Ethereum,<br />\nyou usually also need a little ETH for that fare.</p>\n<p>Some wallets can pay it for you.<br />\nRead the prompt in the wallet.</p>\n<p>A network fee is the cost of executing the transaction. Many chains pay it in the native asset: Ethereum commonly uses ETH, Solana commonly uses SOL, BNB Chain commonly uses BNB. TRON involves bandwidth, energy, and possibly TRX.</p>\n<p>Some wallets or apps support sponsored gas, resource rental, or another way to pay. The rules depend on that service.</p>", "text": "Sending costs a network fee. Tokens with no gas beside them still cannot move. Why does the wallet show coins, and still refuse to send? Sending also pays a \"fare\", which is the network fee. To send USDT on Ethereum, you usually also need a little ETH for that fare. Some wallets can pay it for you. Read the prompt in the wallet. A network fee is the cost of executing the transaction. Many chains pay it in the native asset: Ethereum commonly uses ETH, Solana commonly uses SOL, BNB Chain commonly uses BNB. TRON involves bandwidth, energy, and possibly TRX. Some wallets or apps support sponsored gas, resource rental, or another way to pay. The rules depend on that service."}, {"title": "How do you see whether a transaction went through?", "html": "<p><strong>Take the TxID to a block explorer. That tells you whether it succeeded.</strong></p>\n<p>A transaction hash / TxID is the identifier you use to look up one transaction. On the explorer for that network, check whether it succeeded, the receiving address, the asset, the fee, and the confirmations.</p>\n<p>Success on-chain and a credit on an exchange are two different events. The platform may also require a number of confirmations, a minimum amount, or manual processing.</p>", "text": "Take the TxID to a block explorer. That tells you whether it succeeded. A transaction hash / TxID is the identifier you use to look up one transaction. On the explorer for that network, check whether it succeeded, the receiving address, the asset, the fee, and the confirmations. Success on-chain and a credit on an exchange are two different events. The platform may also require a number of confirmations, a minimum amount, or manual processing."}, {"title": "Swap: exchanging on-chain", "html": "<p><strong>Before a swap, read how much you will receive and the slippage. Then sign.</strong></p>\n<p>A swap exchanges one asset for another. Confirm the token contract, the route, the amount you expect to receive, the price impact, the slippage, and the network fee before you decide to sign.</p>\n<p>On some protocols, approving and then swapping are two separate transactions. A successful approval is not a completed swap. Raising slippage does not make the trade safe. Set it too high and you accept a very bad fill.</p>", "text": "Before a swap, read how much you will receive and the slippage. Then sign. A swap exchanges one asset for another. Confirm the token contract, the route, the amount you expect to receive, the price impact, the slippage, and the network fee before you decide to sign. On some protocols, approving and then swapping are two separate transactions. A successful approval is not a completed swap. Raising slippage does not make the trade safe. Set it too high and you accept a very bad fill."}, {"title": "Bridges", "html": "<p><strong>A bridge is riskier than an ordinary transfer. Do not rush one on your first try.</strong></p>\n<p>A bridge moves assets or messages between networks. After the bridge, the asset may be a wrapped or mapped version. You need to understand who you are trusting and how redemption works.</p>\n<p>Check the source chain, the destination chain, the bridge protocol, the asset version, the fees, the wait, and gas on the destination chain. A bridge can involve contract risk, the verification mechanism, custody, liquidity, or the front end.</p>\n", "text": "A bridge is riskier than an ordinary transfer. Do not rush one on your first try. A bridge moves assets or messages between networks. After the bridge, the asset may be a wrapped or mapped version. You need to understand who you are trusting and how redemption works. Check the source chain, the destination chain, the bridge protocol, the asset version, the fees, the wait, and gas on the destination chain. A bridge can involve contract risk, the verification mechanism, custody, liquidity, or the front end. Note: For a first on-chain practice, one correct small receive and one correct small send matter more than rushing to bridge."}]}, {"id": "11", "title": "How to research a project", "emoji": "🔍", "intro": "Use, token, data, and risk", "tags": ["Advanced"], "tip": "Note: Not understanding it is a reason to keep reading. It is not a reason to buy because you are afraid of missing it.", "sections": [{"title": "Question 1: what does it do?", "html": "<p><strong>If you cannot say what the project does in one sentence, do not buy it yet.</strong></p>\n<p>Say, in one sentence, the problem it works on, who it is for, and what the product is. If all you can repeat is \"revolutionary\" and \"empowering the ecosystem\", and you cannot say how anyone uses it, keep researching.</p>", "text": "If you cannot say what the project does in one sentence, do not buy it yet. Say, in one sentence, the problem it works on, who it is for, and what the product is. If all you can repeat is \"revolutionary\" and \"empowering the ecosystem\", and you cannot say how anyone uses it, keep researching."}, {"title": "Question 2: what is the coin for?", "html": "<p><strong>A good project is not the same as a useful coin. Ask what the coin actually does.</strong></p>\n<p>A good product does not mean the token captures value. Check what the token is for, how supply and demand work, what rights holders actually have, where fees go, and who governs.</p>\n<p>\"Platform revenue is growing\" does not automatically mean that revenue is paid to token holders. A buyback plan also has to be checked: is it actually carried out, and can it continue?</p>", "text": "A good project is not the same as a useful coin. Ask what the coin actually does. A good product does not mean the token captures value. Check what the token is for, how supply and demand work, what rights holders actually have, where fees go, and who governs. \"Platform revenue is growing\" does not automatically mean that revenue is paid to token holders. A buyback plan also has to be checked: is it actually carried out, and can it continue?"}, {"title": "Question 3: who holds the coins, and when can they sell?", "html": "<p><strong>See who holds the coins and when they unlock, so you are not the one left under a dump.</strong></p>\n<p>Look at allocations to the team, investors, the community, and the treasury, the share already circulating, and the unlock schedule. Concentration of holdings has to be read with who the address is: an exchange's pooled address is not necessarily one whale.</p>", "text": "See who holds the coins and when they unlock, so you are not the one left under a dump. Look at allocations to the team, investors, the community, and the treasury, the share already circulating, and the unlock schedule. Concentration of holdings has to be read with who the address is: an exchange's pooled address is not necessarily one whale."}, {"title": "Extra check: which exchanges already list it?", "html": "<p><strong>Whether it is listed is decided by the exchange's own announcement.</strong></p>\n<p>Check the exchange's official asset page and announcements. Do not stop at the project's poster. Check the contract, the network, the pair, and whether trading, deposits, and withdrawals are actually open.</p>\n<p>Separate a spot listing, a contract listing, pre-market trading, and a token that some wallet's DEX aggregator happens to show. A token searchable in a brand's wallet is not the same as that brand's CEX having officially listed the spot market. Also check for recent delistings, pauses, or restriction notices.</p>", "text": "Whether it is listed is decided by the exchange's own announcement. Check the exchange's official asset page and announcements. Do not stop at the project's poster. Check the contract, the network, the pair, and whether trading, deposits, and withdrawals are actually open. Separate a spot listing, a contract listing, pre-market trading, and a token that some wallet's DEX aggregator happens to show. A token searchable in a brand's wallet is not the same as that brand's CEX having officially listed the spot market. Also check for recent delistings, pauses, or restriction notices."}, {"title": "If it is new: who led the round, and which institutions are in?", "html": "<p><strong>An institutional investment can be checked. It does not mean the price will rise.</strong></p>\n<p>Look up the round, the date, the amount that was made public, the lead, and the other investors. Cross-check announcements from the project and from the institution itself. Equity investment, a token investment, a grant, a technical partnership, and simply showing up at an event are not the same relationship.</p>\n<p>\"Institutional backing\" is best written as a fact you can verify, for example \"this institution joined this round\". Do not treat logos on a website as proof of a partnership. Do not treat an investor's name as a promise about the future price.</p>", "text": "An institutional investment can be checked. It does not mean the price will rise. Look up the round, the date, the amount that was made public, the lead, and the other investors. Cross-check announcements from the project and from the institution itself. Equity investment, a token investment, a grant, a technical partnership, and simply showing up at an event are not the same relationship. \"Institutional backing\" is best written as a fact you can verify, for example \"this institution joined this round\". Do not treat logos on a website as proof of a partnership. Do not treat an investor's name as a promise about the future price."}, {"title": "Question 4: what do the numbers say?", "html": "<p><strong>Before you trust a number, ask how it was counted. A marketing screenshot needs its original source.</strong></p>\n<p>TVL is the value of locked assets under a specific set of rules. The coin price and double counting can move it.<br />\nActive addresses are not the same as distinct real people.<br />\nVolume is not revenue.<br />\nProtocol fees, protocol revenue, and net profit are not the same metric.</p>\n<p>Before you compare, check the date, the unit, the time range, and the definition. For a screenshot in a pitch, try to find the original source.</p>", "text": "Before you trust a number, ask how it was counted. A marketing screenshot needs its original source. TVL is the value of locked assets under a specific set of rules. The coin price and double counting can move it. Active addresses are not the same as distinct real people. Volume is not revenue. Protocol fees, protocol revenue, and net profit are not the same metric. Before you compare, check the date, the unit, the time range, and the definition. For a screenshot in a pitch, try to find the original source."}, {"title": "Question 5: what powers exist, and how do you get out?", "html": "<p><strong>Check who can mint, freeze, or change the rules before you consider buying.</strong></p>\n<p>Who can upgrade the contract, pause it, mint more, freeze, or change fees? Can you sell normally? Is there a transfer tax, a blacklist, or a transfer restriction? Who controls the pool's liquidity?</p>\n<p>A safety scanner can only offer some clues. It cannot guarantee there is no malicious logic. A small test buy and sell also cannot prove you will be able to exit later.</p>", "text": "Check who can mint, freeze, or change the rules before you consider buying. Who can upgrade the contract, pause it, mint more, freeze, or change fees? Can you sell normally? Is there a transfer tax, a blacklist, or a transfer restriction? Who controls the pool's liquidity? A safety scanner can only offer some clues. It cannot guarantee there is no malicious logic. A small test buy and sell also cannot prove you will be able to exit later."}, {"title": "What does an audit mean?", "html": "<p><strong>An audit means someone reviewed a specific version of the code. It is not a promise of no bugs.</strong></p>\n<p>An audit inspects specific code and a specific scope. It may find problems. It is not proof that there are no bugs. Read what was audited, the version, the date, the issues still open, and whether the code that shipped matches the version that was audited.</p>", "text": "An audit means someone reviewed a specific version of the code. It is not a promise of no bugs. An audit inspects specific code and a specific scope. It may find problems. It is not proof that there are no bugs. Read what was audited, the version, the date, the issues still open, and whether the code that shipped matches the version that was audited."}, {"title": "Sources, and who is being paid to talk", "html": "<p><strong>Official docs and on-chain data come first. KOLs come after, and notice whether they are paid.</strong></p>\n<p>Read official docs, on-chain records, and independent data before community chatter. Fundraising, listings, celebrity attention, and partnership announcements all need checking. None of them is a promise of profit.</p>\n<p>When you read a KOL, notice whether they hold the asset, whether they are paid to promote it, or whether they use a referral link. Those interests change what they emphasize.</p>", "text": "Official docs and on-chain data come first. KOLs come after, and notice whether they are paid. Read official docs, on-chain records, and independent data before community chatter. Fundraising, listings, celebrity attention, and partnership announcements all need checking. None of them is a promise of profit. When you read a KOL, notice whether they hold the asset, whether they are paid to promote it, or whether they use a referral link. Those interests change what they emphasize."}, {"title": "A one-page research note", "html": "<p><strong>Write the research on one page. If you cannot, you have not thought it through.</strong></p>\n<p>Project name and official entrance:<br />\nProduct and users:<br />\nWhat the token is for:<br />\nCirculation, valuation, and unlocks:<br />\nContract and admin powers:<br />\nData you can verify, and the date:<br />\nMain risks and how you would exit:<br />\nWhat I still do not understand:</p>\n", "text": "Write the research on one page. If you cannot, you have not thought it through. Project name and official entrance: Product and users: What the token is for: Circulation, valuation, and unlocks: Contract and admin powers: Data you can verify, and the date: Main risks and how you would exit: What I still do not understand: Note: Not understanding it is a reason to keep reading. It is not a reason to buy because you are afraid of missing it."}]}, {"id": "12", "title": "A map of Web3 activities", "emoji": "🎮", "intro": "DeFi, airdrops, NFTs, memes, and RWAs", "tags": ["Advanced"], "tip": "Note: For every activity, ask four things: what it is, how you take part, what it costs, and how you can lose.", "sections": [{"title": "First you need a wallet: the start of every activity 👛", "html": "<p><strong>DeFi, airdrops, NFTs, memes: the first step for all of them is an on-chain wallet.</strong></p>\n<p>Every activity below needs an on-chain wallet to connect to an app, to sign, and to hold the assets. An exchange account cannot stand in for it.</p>\n<p>Beginners can start with Binance Wallet (keyless). It is easier at the start:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>You do not write down a traditional seed phrase.</li>\n<li>It uses MPC. The key is split into 3 shares. Any two are enough to use it, and Binance alone cannot move your money.</li>\n<li>Before a transfer it can show a risk reminder, which is one more check.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p>💬 From the person who runs this site: I once nearly sent money to a contract address. Binance Wallet popped up a warning and stopped me. For a beginner, that kind of reminder really helps.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For how it works and how to back it up, see <a href=\"#/c/08/4\">Chapter 08, &quot;A keyless wallet beginners can start with&quot;</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tool-grid\"><div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>Binance Wallet (keyless)</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://web3.binance.com/referral?ref=0XMAY\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>A self-custody on-chain wallet that suits a beginner. You can connect DApps, sign, and hold on-chain assets. It is not the same thing as the exchange account.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>web3.binance.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://web3.binance.com/referral?ref=0XMAY\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://web3.binance.com/referral?ref=0XMAY\">Open ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, web3.binance.com)</span></a></div></div>", "text": "DeFi, airdrops, NFTs, memes: the first step for all of them is an on-chain wallet. Every activity below needs an on-chain wallet to connect to an app, to sign, and to hold the assets. An exchange account cannot stand in for it. Beginners can start with Binance Wallet (keyless). It is easier at the start: You do not write down a traditional seed phrase. It uses MPC. The key is split into 3 shares. Any two are enough to use it, and Binance alone cannot move your money. Before a transfer it can show a risk reminder, which is one more check. 💬 From the person who runs this site: I once nearly sent money to a contract address. Binance Wallet popped up a warning and stopped me. For a beginner, that kind of reminder really helps. For how it works and how to back it up, see Chapter 08, \"A keyless wallet beginners can start with\" . Binance Wallet (keyless) ⏳ Not checked yet A self-custody on-chain wallet that suits a beginner. You can connect DApps, sign, and hold on-chain assets. It is not the same thing as the exchange account. Domain: web3.binance.com Open ↗ (new window, web3.binance.com)"}, {"title": "DeFi: financial apps on a chain", "html": "<p><strong>DeFi is banking and exchange functions on a chain. There is no support desk to catch you.</strong></p>\n<p>DeFi means apps that use smart contracts for swaps, lending, and similar financial functions. You usually interact with a wallet. Costs include network fees and protocol fees. Risk can come from the code, the collateral, oracles, governance powers, and the economic design.</p>", "text": "DeFi is banking and exchange functions on a chain. There is no support desk to catch you. DeFi means apps that use smart contracts for swaps, lending, and similar financial functions. You usually interact with a wallet. Costs include network fees and protocol fees. Risk can come from the code, the collateral, oracles, governance powers, and the economic design."}, {"title": "Lending", "html": "<p><strong>Lending is about collateral and liquidation. Do not look only at the yield.</strong></p>\n<p>Depositing an asset may earn a return. Borrowing an asset requires collateral and interest. If the collateral price falls, you can be liquidated. The deposit side also has bad-debt, liquidity, and contract risk. Do not look only at the yield.</p>", "text": "Lending is about collateral and liquidation. Do not look only at the yield. Depositing an asset may earn a return. Borrowing an asset requires collateral and interest. If the collateral price falls, you can be liquidated. The deposit side also has bad-debt, liquidity, and contract risk. Do not look only at the yield."}, {"title": "Liquidity pools", "html": "<p><strong>Providing liquidity earns fees, and you also take impermanent loss.</strong></p>\n<p>You put assets into a pool and may earn trading fees or rewards. Besides the assets' own price moves, there is impermanent loss, rewards that lose value, and contract risk. Impermanent loss describes the value difference versus simply holding the assets. It is not guaranteed to \"come back\".</p>", "text": "Providing liquidity earns fees, and you also take impermanent loss. You put assets into a pool and may earn trading fees or rewards. Besides the assets' own price moves, there is impermanent loss, rewards that lose value, and contract risk. Impermanent loss describes the value difference versus simply holding the assets. It is not guaranteed to \"come back\"."}, {"title": "Staking and yield products", "html": "<p><strong>If a product is called \"earn\", first ask what the money is actually doing.</strong></p>\n<p>Native staking on a proof-of-stake network supports validation and pays rewards. There can be an exit wait, validator risk, or a penalty mechanism.</p>\n<p>A product a platform labels \"staking\" or \"Earn\" may actually be lending or some other yield strategy. You have to read what the funds are used for, the redemption rules, and who bears the risk. The product name is not enough.</p>\n<p>APR is an annualized rate without compounding. APY includes a compounding assumption. An annualized number on the screen is not a guaranteed return for the next year.</p>", "text": "If a product is called \"earn\", first ask what the money is actually doing. Native staking on a proof-of-stake network supports validation and pays rewards. There can be an exit wait, validator risk, or a penalty mechanism. A product a platform labels \"staking\" or \"Earn\" may actually be lending or some other yield strategy. You have to read what the funds are used for, the redemption rules, and who bears the risk. The product name is not enough. APR is an annualized rate without compounding. APY includes a compounding assumption. An annualized number on the screen is not a guaranteed return for the next year."}, {"title": "Airdrops, points, and testnets", "html": "<p><strong>Airdrops and points do not guarantee a payoff. Do not approve things blindly for them.</strong></p>\n<p>You may get a chance at a future distribution by using a product or joining an activity. The cost includes time, network fees, and interaction risk. Points do not guarantee a token redemption. Test assets usually have no mainnet value, and there may be no reward.</p>", "text": "Airdrops and points do not guarantee a payoff. Do not approve things blindly for them. You may get a chance at a future distribution by using a product or joining an activity. The cost includes time, network fees, and interaction risk. Points do not guarantee a token redemption. Test assets usually have no mainnet value, and there may be no reward."}, {"title": "NFTs", "html": "<p><strong>Buying an NFT does not mean you bought the copyright to the picture.</strong></p>\n<p>An NFT is a token used to represent a unique identifier and related information. Buying an NFT does not automatically give you copyright in an image or other legal rights. The actual rights depend on the license and the terms. Price, liquidity, metadata, and whether the project continues are all risks.</p>", "text": "Buying an NFT does not mean you bought the copyright to the picture. An NFT is a token used to represent a unique identifier and related information. Buying an NFT does not automatically give you copyright in an image or other legal rights. The actual rights depend on the license and the terms. Price, liquidity, metadata, and whether the project continues are all risks."}, {"title": "On-chain games and DAOs", "html": "<p><strong>A game lasts if it is fun to play. Holding a governance token is not the same as being a shareholder.</strong></p>\n<p>An on-chain game puts some game assets or mechanics on a chain. Whether the game is fun, and whether the token economics can last, are separate questions.<br />\nA DAO is a way of organizing people with rules and a governance mechanism. Holding a governance token does not mean you own equity in a company. Governance power can also be highly concentrated.</p>", "text": "A game lasts if it is fun to play. Holding a governance token is not the same as being a shareholder. An on-chain game puts some game assets or mechanics on a chain. Whether the game is fun, and whether the token economics can last, are separate questions. A DAO is a way of organizing people with rules and a governance mechanism. Holding a governance token does not mean you own equity in a company. Governance power can also be highly concentrated."}, {"title": "Meme coins", "html": "<p><strong>A meme coin can surge, and it can go to zero. Only use money you can afford to lose.</strong></p>\n<p>Meme coins often grow around a culture, a community, and how the story spreads. They can rise by astonishing amounts, and a few participants make a lot. They can also go to zero quickly, or become impossible to sell, and participants lose most or all of the principal.</p>\n<p>People often compare buying a meme coin to \"buying a lottery ticket\", because the outcome is highly uncertain and it is easy to see only the get-rich stories. It does not have a lottery's fixed, transparent prize mechanism. Concentrated holdings, insider advantage, bots front-running, malicious contracts, and liquidity also change the result.</p>\n<p>So you can understand the upside, and you also have to hold the chance of going to zero, of being unable to sell, and of uneven information in the same picture. Do not treat a get-rich story as the expected result for an ordinary participant.</p>\n<div class=\"tool-grid\"><div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>Meme guide (DeBot)</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://debot.ai/r/0xMay\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>A tool for on-chain meme-coin activity and trading data. Being listed in the tool does not mean the token is safe. Before you join, accept that it may go to zero.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>debot.ai</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://debot.ai/r/0xMay\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://debot.ai/r/0xMay\">Open ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, debot.ai)</span></a></div></div>", "text": "A meme coin can surge, and it can go to zero. Only use money you can afford to lose. Meme coins often grow around a culture, a community, and how the story spreads. They can rise by astonishing amounts, and a few participants make a lot. They can also go to zero quickly, or become impossible to sell, and participants lose most or all of the principal. People often compare buying a meme coin to \"buying a lottery ticket\", because the outcome is highly uncertain and it is easy to see only the get-rich stories. It does not have a lottery's fixed, transparent prize mechanism. Concentrated holdings, insider advantage, bots front-running, malicious contracts, and liquidity also change the result. So you can understand the upside, and you also have to hold the chance of going to zero, of being unable to sell, and of uneven information in the same picture. Do not treat a get-rich story as the expected result for an ordinary participant. Meme guide (DeBot) ⏳ Not checked yet A tool for on-chain meme-coin activity and trading data. Being listed in the tool does not mean the token is safe. Before you join, accept that it may go to zero. Domain: debot.ai Open ↗ (new window, debot.ai)"}, {"title": "What is a prediction market? 🔮", "html": "<p><strong>A prediction market is trading on the outcome of a question.</strong></p>\n<p>For example:<br />\n\"Will this team win?\"<br />\n\"Will this product ship before the end of the year?\"</p>\n<p>You can buy \"yes\" or \"no\". If you are right, you are paid under the platform's rules. If you are wrong, the share you bought can go to zero.</p>\n<p>Believing the event will happen does not mean buying in is guaranteed to make money. The price you pay, the fees, and the rules that decide the outcome all change the result.</p>\n<p>Before you join, read:<br />\nwhen it closes, what counts as \"it happened\", and who decides.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ These products can lose money. In some places they may fall under gambling or financial rules. Check the rules where you are before you use one.</p>\n</blockquote>", "text": "A prediction market is trading on the outcome of a question. For example: \"Will this team win?\" \"Will this product ship before the end of the year?\" You can buy \"yes\" or \"no\". If you are right, you are paid under the platform's rules. If you are wrong, the share you bought can go to zero. Believing the event will happen does not mean buying in is guaranteed to make money. The price you pay, the fees, and the rules that decide the outcome all change the result. Before you join, read: when it closes, what counts as \"it happened\", and who decides. ⚠️ These products can lose money. In some places they may fall under gambling or financial rules. Check the rules where you are before you use one."}, {"title": "RWAs and tokenized stocks", "html": "<p><strong>A tokenized stock is not the same as you actually holding the stock.</strong></p>\n<p>RWA means an on-chain representation of a claim related to a real-world asset. A tokenized stock gives some exposure to a stock's price, or some related right. It is not the same as being the registered holder of the stock.</p>\n<p>Check the issuing entity, custody, the legal right, redemption, dividends, who is allowed to participate, and trading hours. A name that looks like a stock does not mean the rights are the same.</p>", "text": "A tokenized stock is not the same as you actually holding the stock. RWA means an on-chain representation of a claim related to a real-world asset. A tokenized stock gives some exposure to a stock's price, or some related right. It is not the same as being the registered holder of the stock. Check the issuing entity, custody, the legal right, redemption, dividends, who is allowed to participate, and trading hours. A name that looks like a stock does not mean the rights are the same."}, {"title": "DePIN and AI", "html": "<p><strong>For DePIN and AI projects, first look for real demand and real revenue.</strong></p>\n<p>DePIN usually means using a network and incentives to organize real-world infrastructure, such as compute, communications, or sensors. An AI and Web3 project may involve compute, data, or automated agents.</p>\n<p>Check real demand, hardware cost, where revenue comes from, and how permissions are set. If an AI agent can operate a wallet, be explicit about how much it may spend, what it can call, and how you revoke that permission.</p>\n", "text": "For DePIN and AI projects, first look for real demand and real revenue. DePIN usually means using a network and incentives to organize real-world infrastructure, such as compute, communications, or sensors. An AI and Web3 project may involve compute, data, or automated agents. Check real demand, hardware cost, where revenue comes from, and how permissions are set. If an AI agent can operate a wallet, be explicit about how much it may spend, what it can call, and how you revoke that permission. Note: For every activity, ask four things: what it is, how you take part, what it costs, and how you can lose."}]}, {"id": "13", "title": "Crypto slang translator", "emoji": "💬", "intro": "Serious terms and community jokes, kept apart", "tags": ["Glossary"], "sections": [{"title": "Serious terms", "html": "<p><strong>Serious terms are community shorthand, and you need the meaning before you can tell a slogan from a fact.</strong></p>\n<p>The table below lists common words in their existing wording.<br />\nKnowing the meaning is so you can understand what other people are saying.<br />\nIt does not turn a slogan into a fact.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Plain explanation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>DYOR</td>\n<td>Do your own research and check it yourself. Do not treat someone else's opinion as the conclusion.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FOMO</td>\n<td>Fear of missing out, which pushes you to jump in on impulse.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FUD</td>\n<td>Fear, uncertainty, and doubt. The word cannot be used to dismiss every criticism.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HODL</td>\n<td>Community slang for holding for a long time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ATH / ATL</td>\n<td>All-time high / all-time low. Pay attention to the range of the data.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bull / Bear</td>\n<td>Bullish / bearish. Also used for a bull market / a bear market.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Whale</td>\n<td>A participant with a large holding or a large trade.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Degen</td>\n<td>Someone aggressive about high-risk plays. Often said with self-mockery.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rug Pull</td>\n<td>The project side abandons participants by pulling funds or by some other exit.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Honeypot</td>\n<td>A trap that looks buyable but may block selling or transferring.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Airdrop</td>\n<td>Assets allocated to users or addresses that qualify.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Snapshot</td>\n<td>A record of state at one moment in time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vesting</td>\n<td>Assets released gradually over time or under conditions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TGE</td>\n<td>Token generation event. Whether it circulates immediately depends on the rules.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Whitepaper</td>\n<td>The project's write-up of its design and plan. It is not a promise that any of it will happen.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tokenomics</td>\n<td>The token's economics: issuance, allocation, use, and incentives.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TVL</td>\n<td>Total value locked. You need the scope and the definition behind the number.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FDV</td>\n<td>Fully diluted valuation. You need the supply definition behind it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gas</td>\n<td>The usual name for the network resources or the fee a transaction needs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TxID</td>\n<td>The transaction id / hash, used to look the transaction up.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Approve</td>\n<td>An approval that lets a named party use an asset under the rules.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Revoke</td>\n<td>Cancel that approval. It cannot recover assets already lost.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slippage</td>\n<td>The difference between the price you expected and the price you got.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LP</td>\n<td>Liquidity provider. Also often used for the liquidity position itself.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MEV</td>\n<td>Maximal extractable value: opportunities from ordering, including, or excluding transactions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CEX / DEX</td>\n<td>Centralized exchange / decentralized trading protocol or venue.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>KYC</td>\n<td>The identity-verification process.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>P2P</td>\n<td>A user-to-user trade. In crypto it often means buying and selling with fiat.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Long / Short</td>\n<td>Betting on a rise / betting on a fall.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Funding Rate</td>\n<td>The funding rate on a perpetual contract.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Liquidation</td>\n<td>Forced close-out. The exact meaning depends on the setting.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GM / GN</td>\n<td>Community greetings: good morning / good night.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>庄家 / 市场操纵 (market maker in the slang sense / manipulation)</td>\n<td>Casually called 狗庄. A person or group holding enough of a coin to move its price or its volume in an obvious way, for example by concentrated buying and selling, wash trading, or pumping and then distributing. Small-cap, illiquid coins are easier to push around.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Meme coin</td>\n<td>A token built around a culture, a community, and how it spreads. The small, high-risk ones are often called 土狗. They can surge, and they can go to zero or become impossible to sell.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Swap</td>\n<td>Exchange one asset for another. Watch price impact, slippage, and the network fee.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Private Key</td>\n<td>The secret that authorizes actions on an account. Like a seed phrase, never give it to anyone.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MPC wallet</td>\n<td>A multi-party computation wallet. The key is split into shares kept apart. Creating one usually does not mean writing down a traditional seed phrase (Binance Wallet (keyless) is an example). You still have to finish the backup and confirm how recovery works.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>WAGMI</td>\n<td>A community slogan, \"we're all gonna make it\". It is not a promise of a return.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. DYOR means you research and check it yourself. Someone else's opinion cannot be treated as the conclusion.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. DYOR is not 'if someone is bullish, you can buy,' and it is not acting because you see FOMO. It means you check it yourself.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>What is DYOR saying?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. If someone else is bullish, you can treat that as the conclusion</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Research and check it yourself, and do not treat someone else's opinion as the conclusion</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. If you see FOMO, you should buy immediately</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Serious terms are community shorthand, and you need the meaning before you can tell a slogan from a fact. The table below lists common words in their existing wording. Knowing the meaning is so you can understand what other people are saying. It does not turn a slogan into a fact. Term Plain explanation DYOR Do your own research and check it yourself. Do not treat someone else's opinion as the conclusion. FOMO Fear of missing out, which pushes you to jump in on impulse. FUD Fear, uncertainty, and doubt. The word cannot be used to dismiss every criticism. HODL Community slang for holding for a long time. ATH / ATL All-time high / all-time low. Pay attention to the range of the data. Bull / Bear Bullish / bearish. Also used for a bull market / a bear market. Whale A participant with a large holding or a large trade. Degen Someone aggressive about high-risk plays. Often said with self-mockery. Rug Pull The project side abandons participants by pulling funds or by some other exit. Honeypot A trap that looks buyable but may block selling or transferring. Airdrop Assets allocated to users or addresses that qualify. Snapshot A record of state at one moment in time. Vesting Assets released gradually over time or under conditions. TGE Token generation event. Whether it circulates immediately depends on the rules. Whitepaper The project's write-up of its design and plan. It is not a promise that any of it will happen. Tokenomics The token's economics: issuance, allocation, use, and incentives. TVL Total value locked. You need the scope and the definition behind the number. FDV Fully diluted valuation. You need the supply definition behind it. Gas The usual name for the network resources or the fee a transaction needs. TxID The transaction id / hash, used to look the transaction up. Approve An approval that lets a named party use an asset under the rules. Revoke Cancel that approval. It cannot recover assets already lost. Slippage The difference between the price you expected and the price you got. LP Liquidity provider. Also often used for the liquidity position itself. MEV Maximal extractable value: opportunities from ordering, including, or excluding transactions. CEX / DEX Centralized exchange / decentralized trading protocol or venue. KYC The identity-verification process. P2P A user-to-user trade. In crypto it often means buying and selling with fiat. Long / Short Betting on a rise / betting on a fall. Funding Rate The funding rate on a perpetual contract. Liquidation Forced close-out. The exact meaning depends on the setting. GM / GN Community greetings: good morning / good night. 庄家 / 市场操纵 (market maker in the slang sense / manipulation) Casually called 狗庄. A person or group holding enough of a coin to move its price or its volume in an obvious way, for example by concentrated buying and selling, wash trading, or pumping and then distributing. Small-cap, illiquid coins are easier to push around. Meme coin A token built around a culture, a community, and how it spreads. The small, high-risk ones are often called 土狗. They can surge, and they can go to zero or become impossible to sell. Swap Exchange one asset for another. Watch price impact, slippage, and the network fee. Private Key The secret that authorizes actions on an account. Like a seed phrase, never give it to anyone. MPC wallet A multi-party computation wallet. The key is split into shares kept apart. Creating one usually does not mean writing down a traditional seed phrase (Binance Wallet (keyless) is an example). You still have to finish the backup and confirm how recovery works. WAGMI A community slogan, \"we're all gonna make it\". It is not a promise of a return. Think it through What is DYOR saying? A. If someone else is bullish, you can treat that as the conclusion B. Research and check it yourself, and do not treat someone else's opinion as the conclusion C. If you see FOMO, you should buy immediately"}, {"title": "Community jokes", "html": "<p><strong>Community jokes are teasing, and they are not trading advice.</strong></p>\n<p>The lines below are teasing.<br />\nThey are not a judgment of everyone's real intent.<br />\nThey are also not trading advice.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phrase</th>\n<th>The joke, in plain words</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>长期看好 (long-term bullish)</td>\n<td>Don't ask me about the short term.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>健康回调 (healthy pullback)</td>\n<td>It dropped. I just want a nicer way to say that.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>钻石手 (diamond hands)</td>\n<td>You can hold. You also may not be able to sell for now.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>纸手 (paper hands)</td>\n<td>Sold, and it rose. Bought, and panicked.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>交学费 (paying tuition)</td>\n<td>The money is smaller. Hopefully the experience is larger.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>格局打开 (open up your mindset)</td>\n<td>Hold on. Don't sell yet.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>价值投资 (value investing)</td>\n<td>This was supposed to be a short-term trade. Now it is long-term.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>最后一跌 (the last drop)</td>\n<td>That is what was said last time too.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>抄底成功 (caught the bottom)</td>\n<td>So far, all that succeeded is the buy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>回本就走 (I'll leave when I'm back to even)</td>\n<td>The goal just changed from getting rich to getting back to even.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>社区很强 (the community is strong)</td>\n<td>The group chat is certainly lively. The product still needs another look.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>财富自由 (financial freedom)</td>\n<td>For now, the freedom is not looking at the account.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>梭哈 (all-in)</td>\n<td>Risk control is temporarily offline.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>上车 / 下车 (get on / get off)</td>\n<td>Buy / sell. Don't chase just because of the metaphor.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>报单 / 喊单 (calling a trade)</td>\n<td>Someone tells you what to buy. If it rises, it was their eye. If it loses, it is yours.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>接盘 (catching the bag)</td>\n<td>Buying what someone else is selling. Often a joke about buying the top.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. In this comparison, a healthy pullback is a nicer way of saying the price dropped. It is not proof that the price will rise, and it is not a price guarantee.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. This is teasing. It does not prove the price will rise, and it is not an official guarantee.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>In this comparison, which meaning is closer to \"healthy pullback\"?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. It already proves the price will rise afterward</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. It dropped, but the wording makes it sound nicer</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. An official price guarantee</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Community jokes are teasing, and they are not trading advice. The lines below are teasing. They are not a judgment of everyone's real intent. They are also not trading advice. Phrase The joke, in plain words 长期看好 (long-term bullish) Don't ask me about the short term. 健康回调 (healthy pullback) It dropped. I just want a nicer way to say that. 钻石手 (diamond hands) You can hold. You also may not be able to sell for now. 纸手 (paper hands) Sold, and it rose. Bought, and panicked. 交学费 (paying tuition) The money is smaller. Hopefully the experience is larger. 格局打开 (open up your mindset) Hold on. Don't sell yet. 价值投资 (value investing) This was supposed to be a short-term trade. Now it is long-term. 最后一跌 (the last drop) That is what was said last time too. 抄底成功 (caught the bottom) So far, all that succeeded is the buy. 回本就走 (I'll leave when I'm back to even) The goal just changed from getting rich to getting back to even. 社区很强 (the community is strong) The group chat is certainly lively. The product still needs another look. 财富自由 (financial freedom) For now, the freedom is not looking at the account. 梭哈 (all-in) Risk control is temporarily offline. 上车 / 下车 (get on / get off) Buy / sell. Don't chase just because of the metaphor. 报单 / 喊单 (calling a trade) Someone tells you what to buy. If it rises, it was their eye. If it loses, it is yours. 接盘 (catching the bag) Buying what someone else is selling. Often a joke about buying the top. Think it through In this comparison, which meaning is closer to \"healthy pullback\"? A. It already proves the price will rise afterward B. It dropped, but the wording makes it sound nicer C. An official price guarantee"}]}, {"id": "14", "title": "Tool guide", "emoji": "🧰", "intro": "Prices, trades, and on-chain data", "tags": ["Guide"], "sections": [{"title": "Prices and basic facts", "html": "<p><strong>For price, market cap, and on-chain data, use these sites.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tool-grid\"><div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>CoinGecko</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://www.coingecko.com/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Prices, market cap, assets, and market data. Being listed does not mean the project is safe. Check the data.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>www.coingecko.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://www.coingecko.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://www.coingecko.com/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, www.coingecko.com)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>CoinMarketCap</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://coinmarketcap.com/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Market data, supply, and trading venues. Supply definitions can differ across sites.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>coinmarketcap.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://coinmarketcap.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://coinmarketcap.com/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, coinmarketcap.com)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>DefiLlama</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://defillama.com/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>DeFi, TVL, exchange reserves, and related data. Before you compare, check the definition, the time, and whether anything is counted twice.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>defillama.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://defillama.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://defillama.com/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, defillama.com)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>TradingView</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://www.tradingview.com/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Charts and technical indicators. Check the exchange, the pair, and the timeframe. A community opinion is not a fact.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>www.tradingview.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://www.tradingview.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://www.tradingview.com/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, www.tradingview.com)</span></a></div></div>", "text": "For price, market cap, and on-chain data, use these sites. CoinGecko ⏳ Not checked yet Prices, market cap, assets, and market data. Being listed does not mean the project is safe. Check the data. Domain: www.coingecko.com Open site ↗ (new window, www.coingecko.com) CoinMarketCap ⏳ Not checked yet Market data, supply, and trading venues. Supply definitions can differ across sites. Domain: coinmarketcap.com Open site ↗ (new window, coinmarketcap.com) DefiLlama ⏳ Not checked yet DeFi, TVL, exchange reserves, and related data. Before you compare, check the definition, the time, and whether anything is counted twice. Domain: defillama.com Open site ↗ (new window, defillama.com) TradingView ⏳ Not checked yet Charts and technical indicators. Check the exchange, the pair, and the timeframe. A community opinion is not a fact. Domain: www.tradingview.com Open site ↗ (new window, www.tradingview.com)"}, {"title": "Block explorers, one per chain", "html": "<p><strong>To look up a transfer or an address, use the explorer for that chain.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tool-grid\"><div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>Etherscan</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://etherscan.io/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Ethereum transactions, addresses, and contracts.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>etherscan.io</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://etherscan.io/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://etherscan.io/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, etherscan.io)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>BscScan</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://bscscan.com/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>BNB Smart Chain transactions and contracts.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>bscscan.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://bscscan.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://bscscan.com/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, bscscan.com)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>Solscan</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://solscan.io/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Solana transactions and accounts.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>solscan.io</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://solscan.io/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://solscan.io/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, solscan.io)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>mempool.space</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://mempool.space/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Bitcoin transactions, blocks, and fees.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>mempool.space</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://mempool.space/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://mempool.space/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, mempool.space)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>TRONSCAN</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://tronscan.org/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>TRON transactions, accounts, and resources.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>tronscan.org</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://tronscan.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://tronscan.org/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, tronscan.org)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>Arbiscan</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://arbiscan.io/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Arbitrum One transactions and contracts.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>arbiscan.io</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://arbiscan.io/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://arbiscan.io/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, arbiscan.io)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>BaseScan</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://basescan.org/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Base network transactions and contracts.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>basescan.org</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://basescan.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://basescan.org/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, basescan.org)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>Robinhood Chain</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://robin.etherscan.io/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>The Etherscan-style explorer for Robinhood Chain mainnet.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>robin.etherscan.io</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://robin.etherscan.io/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://robin.etherscan.io/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, robin.etherscan.io)</span></a></div>\n</div><p>Before you use them: the explorer and the network have to match. Verified contract code is not a security audit. A label is not a substitute for checking identity.</p>", "text": "To look up a transfer or an address, use the explorer for that chain. Etherscan ⏳ Not checked yet Ethereum transactions, addresses, and contracts. Domain: etherscan.io Open site ↗ (new window, etherscan.io) BscScan ⏳ Not checked yet BNB Smart Chain transactions and contracts. Domain: bscscan.com Open site ↗ (new window, bscscan.com) Solscan ⏳ Not checked yet Solana transactions and accounts. Domain: solscan.io Open site ↗ (new window, solscan.io) mempool.space ⏳ Not checked yet Bitcoin transactions, blocks, and fees. Domain: mempool.space Open site ↗ (new window, mempool.space) TRONSCAN ⏳ Not checked yet TRON transactions, accounts, and resources. Domain: tronscan.org Open site ↗ (new window, tronscan.org) Arbiscan ⏳ Not checked yet Arbitrum One transactions and contracts. Domain: arbiscan.io Open site ↗ (new window, arbiscan.io) BaseScan ⏳ Not checked yet Base network transactions and contracts. Domain: basescan.org Open site ↗ (new window, basescan.org) Robinhood Chain ⏳ Not checked yet The Etherscan-style explorer for Robinhood Chain mainnet. Domain: robin.etherscan.io Open site ↗ (new window, robin.etherscan.io) Before you use them: the explorer and the network have to match. Verified contract code is not a security audit. A label is not a substitute for checking identity."}, {"title": "On-chain pairs", "html": "<p><strong>For new on-chain coins and pools, use this.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tool-grid\"><div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>DEX Screener</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://dexscreener.com/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>On-chain pairs, prices, and pool activity. Being listed in the tool does not mean the token can be bought and sold safely.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>dexscreener.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://dexscreener.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://dexscreener.com/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, dexscreener.com)</span></a></div></div>", "text": "For new on-chain coins and pools, use this. DEX Screener ⏳ Not checked yet On-chain pairs, prices, and pool activity. Being listed in the tool does not mean the token can be bought and sold safely. Domain: dexscreener.com Open site ↗ (new window, dexscreener.com)"}, {"title": "News", "html": "<p><strong>Read the news to follow the industry. For anything important, the official announcement wins.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tool-grid\"><div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>PANews</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://www.panewslab.com/zh\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Chinese-language blockchain and crypto-market news and briefs.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>www.panewslab.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://www.panewslab.com/zh\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://www.panewslab.com/zh\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, www.panewslab.com)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>Odaily</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://www.odaily.news/zh-CN\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Chinese-language blockchain and crypto news, briefs, and longer pieces.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>www.odaily.news</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://www.odaily.news/zh-CN\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://www.odaily.news/zh-CN\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, www.odaily.news)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>BlockBeats</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://www.theblockbeats.info/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Chinese-language crypto-industry briefs and longer reporting.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>www.theblockbeats.info</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://www.theblockbeats.info/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://www.theblockbeats.info/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, www.theblockbeats.info)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>TradingKey</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://www.tradingkey.com/zh-hans/news/cryptocurrencies\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Crypto-related news, in Chinese.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>www.tradingkey.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://www.tradingkey.com/zh-hans/news/cryptocurrencies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://www.tradingkey.com/zh-hans/news/cryptocurrencies\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, www.tradingkey.com)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>CoinDesk</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://www.coindesk.com/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Crypto news and market coverage, in English.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>www.coindesk.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://www.coindesk.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://www.coindesk.com/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, www.coindesk.com)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>The Block</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://www.theblock.co/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Crypto-industry news, data, and research, in English.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>www.theblock.co</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://www.theblock.co/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://www.theblock.co/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, www.theblock.co)</span></a></div>\n</div><p>Before you use them: news and opinion are not investment advice, and outlets also run sponsored content. For anything important, the project's or the institution's own announcement is the source that counts.</p>", "text": "Read the news to follow the industry. For anything important, the official announcement wins. PANews ⏳ Not checked yet Chinese-language blockchain and crypto-market news and briefs. Domain: www.panewslab.com Open site ↗ (new window, www.panewslab.com) Odaily ⏳ Not checked yet Chinese-language blockchain and crypto news, briefs, and longer pieces. Domain: www.odaily.news Open site ↗ (new window, www.odaily.news) BlockBeats ⏳ Not checked yet Chinese-language crypto-industry briefs and longer reporting. Domain: www.theblockbeats.info Open site ↗ (new window, www.theblockbeats.info) TradingKey ⏳ Not checked yet Crypto-related news, in Chinese. Domain: www.tradingkey.com Open site ↗ (new window, www.tradingkey.com) CoinDesk ⏳ Not checked yet Crypto news and market coverage, in English. Domain: www.coindesk.com Open site ↗ (new window, www.coindesk.com) The Block ⏳ Not checked yet Crypto-industry news, data, and research, in English. Domain: www.theblock.co Open site ↗ (new window, www.theblock.co) Before you use them: news and opinion are not investment advice, and outlets also run sponsored content. For anything important, the project's or the institution's own announcement is the source that counts."}, {"title": "Checking and revoking approvals", "html": "<p><strong>Check approvals now and then, and revoke the ones you no longer use. These tools can help you check and revoke approvals, but they cannot guarantee the assets will not be stolen.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tool-grid\"><div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>Revoke.cash</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://revoke.cash/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Check approvals and revoke them. It can inspect some asset approvals on supported networks, and revoke them when you choose. Network and permission coverage is limited. Check the domain and the signature before you use it. If a private key has leaked, revoking approvals is not enough on its own.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>revoke.cash</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://revoke.cash/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://revoke.cash/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, revoke.cash)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>GoPlus</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://gopluslabs.io/en\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Check approvals and revoke them, and it also offers a browser extension and similar risk prompts. Coverage is limited. A risk prompt is not a substitute for checking it yourself.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>gopluslabs.io</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://gopluslabs.io/en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://gopluslabs.io/en\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, gopluslabs.io)</span></a></div></div>", "text": "Check approvals now and then, and revoke the ones you no longer use. These tools can help you check and revoke approvals, but they cannot guarantee the assets will not be stolen. Revoke.cash ⏳ Not checked yet Check approvals and revoke them. It can inspect some asset approvals on supported networks, and revoke them when you choose. Network and permission coverage is limited. Check the domain and the signature before you use it. If a private key has leaked, revoking approvals is not enough on its own. Domain: revoke.cash Open site ↗ (new window, revoke.cash) GoPlus ⏳ Not checked yet Check approvals and revoke them, and it also offers a browser extension and similar risk prompts. Coverage is limited. A risk prompt is not a substitute for checking it yourself. Domain: gopluslabs.io Open site ↗ (new window, gopluslabs.io)"}, {"title": "Learning material", "html": "<p><strong>To go deeper, read these official sources.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tool-grid\"><div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>Binance Academy</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://academy.binance.com/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>A beginner crypto encyclopedia, friendly to Chinese readers.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>academy.binance.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://academy.binance.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://academy.binance.com/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, academy.binance.com)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>Ethereum security</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://ethereum.org/security/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Ethereum&#x27;s own safety and anti-scam guide.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>ethereum.org</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://ethereum.org/security/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://ethereum.org/security/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, ethereum.org)</span></a></div>\n</div><p>Before you use them: official material is good for checking concepts and product rules. A platform's own commercial description still needs to be read as marketing.</p>", "text": "To go deeper, read these official sources. Binance Academy ⏳ Not checked yet A beginner crypto encyclopedia, friendly to Chinese readers. Domain: academy.binance.com Open site ↗ (new window, academy.binance.com) Ethereum security ⏳ Not checked yet Ethereum's own safety and anti-scam guide. Domain: ethereum.org Open site ↗ (new window, ethereum.org) Before you use them: official material is good for checking concepts and product rules. A platform's own commercial description still needs to be read as marketing."}, {"title": "Unlocks and official information", "html": "<p><strong>For an unlock schedule, read the project's own docs first, then compare third-party data.</strong></p>\n<p>Start with the project's official documents for allocation, lockups, and unlock rules, then compare them with third-party data. Plans can change. A label that says \"estimated\" is not the same thing as tokens actually released on-chain.</p>\n<p>Every tool answers only part of the question. After you find a number, you still need to know what it is, how it was counted, and what is missing.</p>", "text": "For an unlock schedule, read the project's own docs first, then compare third-party data. Start with the project's official documents for allocation, lockups, and unlock rules, then compare them with third-party data. Plans can change. A label that says \"estimated\" is not the same thing as tokens actually released on-chain. Every tool answers only part of the question. After you find a number, you still need to know what it is, how it was counted, and what is missing."}]}, {"id": "15", "title": "Something went wrong", "emoji": "🆘", "intro": "Deposits, withdrawals, transfers, and suspected theft", "tags": ["Troubleshooting"], "sections": [{"title": "A deposit has not arrived", "html": "<p>Success on-chain is not the same as a credit at the exchange: check the network, the Memo, and the confirmations, then use the official channel, and do not send again or hand over a seed phrase.</p>\n<p><strong>The money has been sent, and the exchange is not showing a balance.</strong><br />\nDo not rush to send it again.</p>\n<p>First, open the withdrawal record and see whether the platform has already sent the transaction, and whether there is a TxID.<br />\nSecond, look it up in the block explorer for that network.<br />\nIf it is not on-chain yet, go to the platform that sent it.<br />\nIf it is on-chain and still waiting for confirmations, look at the confirmation status first.<br />\nIf it succeeded on-chain but has not been credited, check the receiving address, the Memo / Tag, the coin, the network, the minimum deposit, and the number of confirmations the platform requires.<br />\nIf the details are right and it still has not arrived, take the TxID and the order record to the receiving platform's official support.<br />\nA success on-chain and a credit on the platform are two different things. Arrival is not guaranteed.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A success on-chain is not the same as a credit on the platform. Check the network, the Memo, and the confirmations first, then submit the TxID through the official channel.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Sending again can make the loss larger. Giving a seed phrase to support hands over control of the wallet.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Fictional teaching example: the TxID already shows success, but the exchange balance is still 0. What should you do?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Send another transfer to the same address</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Check the network, the Memo, and the confirmations the platform requires</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Send the seed phrase to support so they can check the balance</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Success on-chain is not the same as a credit at the exchange: check the network, the Memo, and the confirmations, then use the official channel, and do not send again or hand over a seed phrase. The money has been sent, and the exchange is not showing a balance. Do not rush to send it again. First, open the withdrawal record and see whether the platform has already sent the transaction, and whether there is a TxID. Second, look it up in the block explorer for that network. If it is not on-chain yet, go to the platform that sent it. If it is on-chain and still waiting for confirmations, look at the confirmation status first. If it succeeded on-chain but has not been credited, check the receiving address, the Memo / Tag, the coin, the network, the minimum deposit, and the number of confirmations the platform requires. If the details are right and it still has not arrived, take the TxID and the order record to the receiving platform's official support. A success on-chain and a credit on the platform are two different things. Arrival is not guaranteed. Think it through Fictional teaching example: the TxID already shows success, but the exchange balance is still 0. What should you do? A. Send another transfer to the same address B. Check the network, the Memo, and the confirmations the platform requires C. Send the seed phrase to support so they can check the balance"}, {"title": "A withdrawal stays on \"processing\"", "html": "<p>While a withdrawal stays on processing, read the order and look for a TxID; if there is none, use the official entrance, and do not pay a speed-up fee to a private message.</p>\n<p><strong>The withdrawal page keeps spinning. Look at the order status first, then look for a TxID.</strong></p>\n<p>No TxID usually means you cannot yet confirm that the transaction has been broadcast to the chain.<br />\nIt may still be in review, in a queue, or being processed.<br />\nIn that case, read the platform notice and the order notes, and ask for help through the official entrance.<br />\nIf there is a TxID, check the confirmation status in the explorer for that network.<br />\nDo not send a so-called fee to someone who messages you and says they can speed the withdrawal up.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. When there is no TxID, read the platform order and notices first, then use the official entrance. Do not pay a speed-up fee to a private message.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Without a TxID you cannot yet confirm it is on-chain. Guessing a hash, or paying a speed-up fee, does not replace the official order.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Fictional teaching example: the order says processing, and there is no TxID. Which next step is the steadier one?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Guess a hash in a block explorer and look that up</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Read the platform order and notices, and contact official support</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Pay a speed-up fee to the support account that messaged you</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "While a withdrawal stays on processing, read the order and look for a TxID; if there is none, use the official entrance, and do not pay a speed-up fee to a private message. The withdrawal page keeps spinning. Look at the order status first, then look for a TxID. No TxID usually means you cannot yet confirm that the transaction has been broadcast to the chain. It may still be in review, in a queue, or being processed. In that case, read the platform notice and the order notes, and ask for help through the official entrance. If there is a TxID, check the confirmation status in the explorer for that network. Do not send a so-called fee to someone who messages you and says they can speed the withdrawal up. Think it through Fictional teaching example: the order says processing, and there is no TxID. Which next step is the steadier one? A. Guess a hash in a block explorer and look that up B. Read the platform order and notices, and contact official support C. Pay a speed-up fee to the support account that messaged you"}, {"title": "Sent to the wrong address or the wrong network", "html": "<p>If you sent to the wrong address or the wrong network, give the TxID to the receiver's official channel and ask whether recovery is possible; recovery is not guaranteed, and do not send again or give away a seed phrase.</p>\n<p><strong>Sending to the wrong address and sending on the wrong network are not the same problem.</strong></p>\n<p>Stop sending anything further.<br />\nSave the TxID, the receiving address, the coin, the network, and the time.<br />\nIf it went to an address you do not recognize, an on-chain transaction generally cannot be undone.<br />\nWhether it can be recovered depends on whether the other side cooperates.<br />\nIf it went to a network the exchange does not support, contact the receiving platform and ask whether a recovery service exists.<br />\nIf it is an address you control, first confirm that the wallet and the key support that network.<br />\nDo not casually import the private key into a tool you do not know.<br />\nIn every case, recovery is not guaranteed.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. When the network is not supported, give the TxID to the receiving platform's official channel and ask whether recovery is possible. Recovery is not guaranteed.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Sending again does not fix the previous transfer. Do not give a seed phrase to a site you do not know.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Fictional teaching example: the TxID shows success, but the receiving exchange does not support the network you chose. What should you do?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Send again, on a cheaper network</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Give the official channel the TxID and ask whether a recovery service exists</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Type the seed phrase into an unfamiliar recovery site</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "If you sent to the wrong address or the wrong network, give the TxID to the receiver's official channel and ask whether recovery is possible; recovery is not guaranteed, and do not send again or give away a seed phrase. Sending to the wrong address and sending on the wrong network are not the same problem. Stop sending anything further. Save the TxID, the receiving address, the coin, the network, and the time. If it went to an address you do not recognize, an on-chain transaction generally cannot be undone. Whether it can be recovered depends on whether the other side cooperates. If it went to a network the exchange does not support, contact the receiving platform and ask whether a recovery service exists. If it is an address you control, first confirm that the wallet and the key support that network. Do not casually import the private key into a tool you do not know. In every case, recovery is not guaranteed. Think it through Fictional teaching example: the TxID shows success, but the receiving exchange does not support the network you chose. What should you do? A. Send again, on a cheaper network B. Give the official channel the TxID and ask whether a recovery service exists C. Type the seed phrase into an unfamiliar recovery site"}, {"title": "You forgot the Memo / Tag", "html": "<p>If the Memo or Tag was left blank, keep the TxID and the order and use the official process; recovery follows the platform's rules and is not guaranteed.</p>\n<p><strong>Some platforms share one deposit address among many users. The Memo or Tag is what says which account the money belongs to.</strong></p>\n<p>The address and the network can both be right, and the chain can show success, and a missing Memo can still mean the money reached the platform's address without being credited to your account.<br />\nSave the TxID, the coin, the amount, the network, and the deposit order first.<br />\nThen apply through the platform's official process for a missing Memo / Tag.<br />\nWhether recovery is offered, how long it takes, and whether it costs money all depend on the platform's rules. It is not guaranteed.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A missing Memo means you keep the TxID and the order, and you use the platform's official process. Whether it can be recovered depends on the platform's rules, and it is not guaranteed.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Another deposit does not fill in the Memo on the previous one. You also cannot edit the record on a block explorer.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Fictional teaching example: the deposit address is correct, the Memo was left blank, and the chain already shows success. What next?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Deposit again immediately, and put the Memo on the next transfer</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Gather the TxID and the order, and submit them through the official missing-Memo process</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Edit the record on the block explorer</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "If the Memo or Tag was left blank, keep the TxID and the order and use the official process; recovery follows the platform's rules and is not guaranteed. Some platforms share one deposit address among many users. The Memo or Tag is what says which account the money belongs to. The address and the network can both be right, and the chain can show success, and a missing Memo can still mean the money reached the platform's address without being credited to your account. Save the TxID, the coin, the amount, the network, and the deposit order first. Then apply through the platform's official process for a missing Memo / Tag. Whether recovery is offered, how long it takes, and whether it costs money all depend on the platform's rules. It is not guaranteed. Think it through Fictional teaching example: the deposit address is correct, the Memo was left blank, and the chain already shows success. What next? A. Deposit again immediately, and put the Memo on the next transfer B. Gather the TxID and the order, and submit them through the official missing-Memo process C. Edit the record on the block explorer"}, {"title": "The wallet does not show the asset", "html": "<p>When the wallet screen shows no coins, check the account, the network, and whether this token is displayed, and do not upload a seed phrase.</p>\n<p><strong>A coin suddenly missing from the wallet screen does not mean the coin is definitely gone.</strong></p>\n<p>First check whether you switched account or network.<br />\nThen use a block explorer you trust and look at that address's balance and transfers on the matching network.<br />\nIf the balance is still on-chain, the wallet may simply not be displaying that token.<br />\nCheck the contract address from a trusted source, then add it so the wallet can show it.<br />\nIf the on-chain record shows the asset already left, treat it as a suspicious transaction.<br />\nDo not believe anyone who says you must enter a seed phrase to sync the balance.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. When the balance is still on-chain, check the account, the network, and whether the token is displayed. A seed phrase cannot be used to sync a balance.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. A screen showing 0 does not mean the chain is empty. Do not upload a seed phrase, and do not decide it was stolen from the screen alone.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Fictional teaching example: the block explorer shows 100 USDT at this address, and the wallet app shows 0. Which step is the more likely first one?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Check the account, the network, and whether this token is being displayed</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Upload the seed phrase so a site can sync the balance</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Decide immediately that the asset was stolen</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "When the wallet screen shows no coins, check the account, the network, and whether this token is displayed, and do not upload a seed phrase. A coin suddenly missing from the wallet screen does not mean the coin is definitely gone. First check whether you switched account or network. Then use a block explorer you trust and look at that address's balance and transfers on the matching network. If the balance is still on-chain, the wallet may simply not be displaying that token. Check the contract address from a trusted source, then add it so the wallet can show it. If the on-chain record shows the asset already left, treat it as a suspicious transaction. Do not believe anyone who says you must enter a seed phrase to sync the balance. Think it through Fictional teaching example: the block explorer shows 100 USDT at this address, and the wallet app shows 0. Which step is the more likely first one? A. Check the account, the network, and whether this token is being displayed B. Upload the seed phrase so a site can sync the balance C. Decide immediately that the asset was stolen"}, {"title": "A transaction failed or is stuck", "html": "<p>When a transaction fails or sticks, first check whether it is pending, failed, or never broadcast; a failure may already have spent the network fee, so do not keep tapping blindly.</p>\n<p><strong>An on-chain transaction has not finished. First find out whether it is pending, failed, or never broadcast.</strong></p>\n<p>While it is pending, some networks and wallets can speed it up or replace it, but not every chain works the same way.<br />\nA transaction that already failed may still have spent the network fee.<br />\nIf it was never broadcast, check the balance, the network, and the wallet's message.<br />\nDo not tap submit many times in a row.<br />\nRead the transaction record and the official instructions first, then decide whether to try again.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A failed transaction may already have spent the network fee. Read the reason and the official instructions first, and do not keep tapping.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. A failure does not mean the network fee comes back. Submitting again and again does not guarantee the next one succeeds.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Fictional teaching example: the explorer shows this transaction failed, and the network fee was already charged. What should you do?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. A failure always refunds the network fee</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Look up why it failed, then decide whether to try again</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Keep submitting until one succeeds</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "When a transaction fails or sticks, first check whether it is pending, failed, or never broadcast; a failure may already have spent the network fee, so do not keep tapping blindly. An on-chain transaction has not finished. First find out whether it is pending, failed, or never broadcast. While it is pending, some networks and wallets can speed it up or replace it, but not every chain works the same way. A transaction that already failed may still have spent the network fee. If it was never broadcast, check the balance, the network, and the wallet's message. Do not tap submit many times in a row. Read the transaction record and the official instructions first, then decide whether to try again. Think it through Fictional teaching example: the explorer shows this transaction failed, and the network fee was already charged. What should you do? A. A failure always refunds the network fee B. Look up why it failed, then decide whether to try again C. Keep submitting until one succeeds"}, {"title": "You suspect a bad approval or a bad signature", "html": "<p>If you suspect a bad signature or approval, stop first; disconnecting is not the same as revoking, so check and revoke it, then judge the risk that remains.</p>\n<p><strong>If you just signed or approved something on a site you do not know, stop. Do not click another popup on that site.</strong></p>\n<p>Save the site address, the transaction hash, or the contents of the signature.<br />\nCheck again from a device or an environment you trust.<br />\nIf it was a token approval, you can check it with a tool you trust and revoke it if you need to.<br />\nIf some other signature permission or control of the account is involved, revoking an ordinary approval may not be enough.<br />\nYou may then need to move the remaining assets to a new wallet that is safe.<br />\nDo not treat \"I disconnected the site\" as \"the risk is gone\".</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Disconnecting does not cancel an approval you already gave. Check it and revoke it, and decide whether the remaining assets should be moved.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Disconnecting is not the same as revoking. Do not go back to that site and sign again.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Fictional teaching example: you gave an unknown contract an unlimited allowance. Is disconnecting the site enough?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. Disconnecting is the same as revoking the approval</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. Check and revoke the approval, then judge the risk that remains</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Sign once more, to verify that the earlier one was fine</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "If you suspect a bad signature or approval, stop first; disconnecting is not the same as revoking, so check and revoke it, then judge the risk that remains. If you just signed or approved something on a site you do not know, stop. Do not click another popup on that site. Save the site address, the transaction hash, or the contents of the signature. Check again from a device or an environment you trust. If it was a token approval, you can check it with a tool you trust and revoke it if you need to. If some other signature permission or control of the account is involved, revoking an ordinary approval may not be enough. You may then need to move the remaining assets to a new wallet that is safe. Do not treat \"I disconnected the site\" as \"the risk is gone\". Think it through Fictional teaching example: you gave an unknown contract an unlimited allowance. Is disconnecting the site enough? A. Yes. Disconnecting is the same as revoking the approval B. No. Check and revoke the approval, then judge the risk that remains C. Sign once more, to verify that the earlier one was fine"}, {"title": "The seed phrase or private key leaked", "html": "<p>Once a seed phrase or private key has leaked, changing a password or reinstalling does not retire the old key, so create a new wallet before you judge how to move what remains.</p>\n<p><strong>If a seed phrase or private key has been given to someone else, uploaded to a web page, or exposed in an environment you do not trust, that wallet is no longer safe.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"keep-lines\">First, on a device you trust, create a wallet with a brand-new key.<br>\nThen judge how to move any remaining assets there safely.<br>\nChanging only the app password, uninstalling the wallet, or revoking an approval<br>\ncannot make a private key that has already leaked stop working.<br>\nIf you suspect a program is automatically sweeping assets, and you add network fee to the old wallet,<br>\nthat fee may be swept away immediately too.<br>\nIn that situation, seek trusted professional help first.</p>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A leaked private key does not stop working because you changed a password or reinstalled. Use a new key first, then consider how to move what remains.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Changing a password and uninstalling cannot make the old private key stop working. Do not keep treating that address as a safe wallet.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Fictional teaching example: the seed phrase was already sent to fake support. Can the old address still be used as a safe wallet?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. Change the login password and keep using it</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. Create a brand-new wallet, then judge how to move the remaining assets safely</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. After you uninstall and reinstall, the old private key stops working</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Once a seed phrase or private key has leaked, changing a password or reinstalling does not retire the old key, so create a new wallet before you judge how to move what remains. If a seed phrase or private key has been given to someone else, uploaded to a web page, or exposed in an environment you do not trust, that wallet is no longer safe. First, on a device you trust, create a wallet with a brand-new key. Then judge how to move any remaining assets there safely. Changing only the app password, uninstalling the wallet, or revoking an approval cannot make a private key that has already leaked stop working. If you suspect a program is automatically sweeping assets, and you add network fee to the old wallet, that fee may be swept away immediately too. In that situation, seek trusted professional help first. Think it through Fictional teaching example: the seed phrase was already sent to fake support. Can the old address still be used as a safe wallet? A. Yes. Change the login password and keep using it B. No. Create a brand-new wallet, then judge how to move the remaining assets safely C. After you uninstall and reinstall, the old private key stops working"}, {"title": "The exchange account may have been taken over", "html": "<p>If the exchange account may have been taken over, restrict it from the official entrance first, then check the email, the API, and the withdrawal settings, and do not give a verification code to a private message.</p>\n<p><strong>If you see an unfamiliar login, a withdrawal record, or an API action, restrict the account through the exchange's official entrance first.</strong></p>\n<p>Do not click a suspicious link in a text or an email.<br />\nProtect the linked email at the same time.<br />\nCheck login devices, sessions, API keys, and the withdrawal allowlist.<br />\nUpdate the password and the verification method through the official process.<br />\nIf funds are already gone, save the notices, the orders, the addresses, and the times.<br />\nThen report it through a proper channel where you live.<br />\nThe order of work is to stop further loss first.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Restrict the account from the official entrance first, protect the email, then check the API, the devices, and the withdrawal settings.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Changing only the trading password does not stop an unknown API. Do not give a verification code or login details to someone in a private message.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Fictional teaching example: the email warns of a login from somewhere else, and the account also shows an API you never created. What is the first step?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Change only the trading password, and leave the rest</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Restrict the account from the official entrance, and check the email and the API</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Reply to the stranger who sent the alert, and give them the verification code</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "If the exchange account may have been taken over, restrict it from the official entrance first, then check the email, the API, and the withdrawal settings, and do not give a verification code to a private message. If you see an unfamiliar login, a withdrawal record, or an API action, restrict the account through the exchange's official entrance first. Do not click a suspicious link in a text or an email. Protect the linked email at the same time. Check login devices, sessions, API keys, and the withdrawal allowlist. Update the password and the verification method through the official process. If funds are already gone, save the notices, the orders, the addresses, and the times. Then report it through a proper channel where you live. The order of work is to stop further loss first. Think it through Fictional teaching example: the email warns of a login from somewhere else, and the account also shows an API you never created. What is the first step? A. Change only the trading password, and leave the rest B. Restrict the account from the official entrance, and check the email and the API C. Reply to the stranger who sent the alert, and give them the verification code"}, {"title": "A second scam, after the first", "html": "<p>If someone asks you to pay first, hand over a seed phrase, or give remote control to recover funds, that is not a reliable method, so use only the official channel.</p>\n<p><strong>Right after a funds problem, you are easiest for a \"recovery expert\", \"technical support\", or a \"paid unfreeze\" pitch to find.</strong></p>\n<p>Be very wary of a private message that promises a one-hundred-percent recovery, asks for a deposit first, asks for a seed phrase, or asks to control your device remotely.<br />\nCheck the official channel, and keep the evidence.<br />\nReal official support should not ask you to hand over a seed phrase either.<br />\nDo not hand the remaining assets to a stranger just because you want the money back quickly.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Paying first, asking for a seed phrase, or asking for remote control is not a reliable way to recover funds. Use only the official channel.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Do not pay first, and do not give a private key to someone in a private message. Wanting it back quickly is not a reason to hand over what remains.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Fictional teaching example: someone messages you and says that paying 50 USDT first will recover the lost coins. What should you do?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Pay first, so you do not miss the recovery window</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Refuse to pay, and check only through the official channel</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Send them the private key, to prove the address is yours</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "If someone asks you to pay first, hand over a seed phrase, or give remote control to recover funds, that is not a reliable method, so use only the official channel. Right after a funds problem, you are easiest for a \"recovery expert\", \"technical support\", or a \"paid unfreeze\" pitch to find. Be very wary of a private message that promises a one-hundred-percent recovery, asks for a deposit first, asks for a seed phrase, or asks to control your device remotely. Check the official channel, and keep the evidence. Real official support should not ask you to hand over a seed phrase either. Do not hand the remaining assets to a stranger just because you want the money back quickly. Think it through Fictional teaching example: someone messages you and says that paying 50 USDT first will recover the lost coins. What should you do? A. Pay first, so you do not miss the recovery window B. Refuse to pay, and check only through the official channel C. Send them the private key, to prove the address is yours"}, {"title": "What to have ready when you ask for help", "html": "<p>When you ask for help, have the TxID, the order number, and what happened ready; use the official channel, and never provide a seed phrase or a private key.</p>\n<p><strong>Before you contact support, write down a short record of the problem.</strong></p>\n<p>Write the time, the coin and the amount, the sending and receiving networks, the address, the TxID, the platform order number, the error message, and what you have already checked.<br />\nThat cuts down the back and forth.<br />\nBefore a screenshot, cover the password, the verification code, and the ID number.<br />\nA seed phrase and a private key must never be provided.<br />\nIf this happened inside a platform account, use the official ticket.<br />\nDo not post a full set of personal details in a public group.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A TxID and an order number can help the official side check. A seed phrase and a private key must not appear in the material you send for help.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. A seed phrase must not be provided, and do not post full details in a public group. A TxID and an order number go through an official ticket.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Support asks for the TxID, the order number, and the seed phrase at the same time. Which choice is right?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Send all three into a public group so everyone can look</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. The TxID and the order number can be given through the official process. The seed phrase is never given</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Send only the seed phrase, and do not keep the order number</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "When you ask for help, have the TxID, the order number, and what happened ready; use the official channel, and never provide a seed phrase or a private key. Before you contact support, write down a short record of the problem. Write the time, the coin and the amount, the sending and receiving networks, the address, the TxID, the platform order number, the error message, and what you have already checked. That cuts down the back and forth. Before a screenshot, cover the password, the verification code, and the ID number. A seed phrase and a private key must never be provided. If this happened inside a platform account, use the official ticket. Do not post a full set of personal details in a public group. Think it through Support asks for the TxID, the order number, and the seed phrase at the same time. Which choice is right? A. Send all three into a public group so everyone can look B. The TxID and the order number can be given through the official process. The seed phrase is never given C. Send only the seed phrase, and do not keep the order number"}]}, {"id": "16", "title": "Common questions and practice", "emoji": "✅", "intro": "Check that the key ideas stuck", "tags": ["Review"], "sections": [{"title": "Do I have to buy a coin before I can learn Web3?", "html": "<p><strong>You can learn without buying.</strong></p>\n<p>No. You can read, watch the market, use a paper trade, or use a test network. Learning and investing are two different things.</p>", "text": "You can learn without buying. No. You can read, watch the market, use a paper trade, or use a test network. Learning and investing are two different things."}, {"title": "Is USDT the same as the US dollar?", "html": "<p><strong>USDT is not the dollar. It tries to stay near 1:1 with the dollar.</strong></p>\n<p>No. It is a stablecoin that aims to track the dollar's value. Issuance, redemption, reserves, and risks are not the same as dollar cash or a bank deposit.</p>", "text": "USDT is not the dollar. It tries to stay near 1:1 with the dollar. No. It is a stablecoin that aims to track the dollar's value. Issuance, redemption, reserves, and risks are not the same as dollar cash or a bank deposit."}, {"title": "BTC is too expensive. What if I cannot buy a whole one?", "html": "<p><strong>You can buy 0.001. You do not have to buy a whole coin.</strong></p>\n<p>You can usually buy a fraction. You do not have to buy one whole coin. You still have to meet the platform's minimum order, and you still have to understand the asset's risk.</p>", "text": "You can buy 0.001. You do not have to buy a whole coin. You can usually buy a fraction. You do not have to buy one whole coin. You still have to meet the platform's minimum order, and you still have to understand the asset's risk."}, {"title": "If it is in a cold wallet, can it never be stolen?", "html": "<p><strong>A cold wallet can still be stolen. The usual causes are the seed phrase and signing the wrong thing.</strong></p>\n<p>There is no guarantee. A leaked seed phrase, a device problem, a malicious signature, and a mistaken action can all cause a loss.</p>", "text": "A cold wallet can still be stolen. The usual causes are the seed phrase and signing the wrong thing. There is no guarantee. A leaked seed phrase, a device problem, a malicious signature, and a mistaken action can all cause a loss."}, {"title": "Is an exchange wallet the same as that brand's Web3 wallet?", "html": "<p><strong>Same brand, and the exchange account and the on-chain wallet are still two different things.</strong></p>\n<p>Not necessarily. The same brand does not mean the same custody. Read, for the specific product, who controls the keys, how recovery works, and how withdrawals work.</p>", "text": "Same brand, and the exchange account and the on-chain wallet are still two different things. Not necessarily. The same brand does not mean the same custody. Read, for the specific product, who controls the keys, how recovery works, and how withdrawals work."}, {"title": "Is an airdrop always free?", "html": "<p><strong>An airdrop is not always free. A \"claim it free\" page can also be a scam.</strong></p>\n<p>Not always. It can cost time, network fees, or even funds, and there may be no reward at the end. A \"claim it free\" page can also be a scam.</p>", "text": "An airdrop is not always free. A \"claim it free\" page can also be a scam. Not always. It can cost time, network fees, or even funds, and there may be no reward at the end. A \"claim it free\" page can also be a scam."}, {"title": "If a project was audited and listed, is it safe?", "html": "<p><strong>An audit and a listing are not a safety guarantee.</strong></p>\n<p>They can be part of the research. They do not replace a full judgment of risk, and they do not guarantee a return or that the assets are safe.</p>", "text": "An audit and a listing are not a safety guarantee. They can be part of the research. They do not replace a full judgment of risk, and they do not guarantee a return or that the assets are safe."}, {"title": "If I learn the indicators, can I make money steadily?", "html": "<p><strong>Learning the indicators does not mean steady profits.</strong></p>\n<p>You cannot infer that. Indicators have to be read against the market and against how you execute. Fees, risk management, and emotion also change the result.</p>", "text": "Learning the indicators does not mean steady profits. You cannot infer that. Indicators have to be read against the market and against how you execute. Fees, risk management, and emotion also change the result."}, {"title": "Does crypto have no weekends?", "html": "<p><strong>Crypto trades 24 hours. You do not have to watch it for 24 hours.</strong></p>\n<p>Many crypto markets trade continuously. Bank deposits, some assets, and platform maintenance can still have hours. The market being open does not mean a person has to stare at it.</p>", "text": "Crypto trades 24 hours. You do not have to watch it for 24 hours. Many crypto markets trade continuously. Bank deposits, some assets, and platform maintenance can still have hours. The market being open does not mean a person has to stare at it."}, {"title": "Safety quiz", "html": "<div data-widget=\"quiz\"></div>", "text": "1. \"Official support\" asks for your seed phrase to repair the wallet. Do you give it? Answer: No. Stop the conversation and check the channel. 2. An Ethereum address and a Base address can look the same. Can you pick the network at random? Answer: No. The recipient has to support that network and that asset. 3. If you disconnect a site, are previous token approvals revoked? Answer: Usually not. You have to check them separately. 4. A coin priced at 0.001 dollar is always cheaper than a coin priced at 100 dollars. True? Answer: You cannot judge it that way. You also have to look at supply, valuation, and the asset itself. 5. You set a stop. Is the real loss guaranteed to stay within the amount you planned? Answer: No. Slippage, a price jump, how the order works, and execution problems can all change the result. 6. A stablecoin shows 20% annualized. Does that mean you are guaranteed 20% more after one year? Answer: No. The annualized figure is an estimate under current conditions. It changes with the market. It is not a guaranteed return. Result copy: All correct: \"Basic shield unlocked 🛡️ Keep it up. Still check before you act.\" Any wrong: \"A few spots are worth another look 🧸 Follow the answer back to the chapter.\""}, {"title": "Practice spot P&L calculator", "html": "<div data-widget=\"calculator\"></div>", "text": "Enter the buy price, the sell price, and the cost (how much you spent to buy). A simplified spot example only: one buy and one sell, in the same quote coin. Quantity bought = cost ÷ buy price. Proceeds = quantity bought × sell price. Net profit or loss = proceeds − cost. Simplified return = net profit or loss ÷ cost × 100% (= sell price ÷ buy price − 1). Trading fees, slippage, and similar costs are not included. Invalid input: \"Enter valid numbers. Buy price and cost must be greater than 0. The sell price cannot be negative.\" Result note: \"This is a simplified learning result. Fees are not included. It does not apply to contracts, lending, trades in several fills, or fees in more than one coin. A full account can look different.\""}, {"title": "Checklist before a transfer", "html": "<div data-widget=\"checklist\"></div>", "text": "- I know which asset I am sending. - The recipient explicitly supports this network. - I checked the full address against a source I trust, and I checked the paste or the scan and the final confirmation screen. - I checked the Memo / Tag requirement. - I checked the minimum amount, the fees, and whether anything is paused. - I confirmed I have enough network resources, or a way for someone to pay the fee for me. - The first time I use a path, I send a small test that meets the minimum. Done message: \"Check finished. Check once more on the final confirmation screen. A ticked list cannot guarantee there is no risk.\""}]}, {"id": "17", "title": "Before you transfer: safety basics that are easy to skip", "emoji": "💸", "intro": "The same brand is not the same account. Confirm the platform, the payment details, and the network before you transfer", "tags": ["Must-read"], "sections": [{"title": "The same brand is not the same account", "html": "<p><strong>Platforms under the same brand do not mean they use the same account system.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"diagram\"><h3>See this first: a brand relationship is not shared accounts</h3>\n<div class=\"cosmic-scene compare-scene\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Two independent platform accounts under one brand. Do not assume an internal transfer between them\"><div class=\"scene-halo halo-violet\"></div><div class=\"scene-halo halo-cyan\"></div>\n<div class=\"brand-constellation\"><span class=\"star-glyph\">✦</span><span>Same brand · different account systems</span></div>\n<div class=\"account-worlds\"><div class=\"world\"><div class=\"planet planet-violet\"><img class=\"platform-app-icon\" src=\"assets/teach/binance-intl.png?v=20261108130000\" alt=\"Binance international app icon\"></div><div class=\"world-card\"><b>Binance international</b></div></div>\n<div class=\"world\"><div class=\"planet planet-cyan\"><img class=\"platform-app-icon\" src=\"assets/teach/binance-th.png?v=20261108130000\" alt=\"Binance TH app icon\"></div><div class=\"world-card\"><b>Binance TH</b></div></div></div>\n<div class=\"scene-divider\"><i></i><span>Do not assume they transfer internally</span><i></i></div>\n<div class=\"scene-bottom\">Same brand is not the same account · the transfer method follows the official rules</div></div>\n<div class=\"caption\">A diagram of the structure. It does not describe how the platform's database is actually built.</div></div>\n\n<p>A recent case: a user wanted to move assets from Binance international to Binance TH (the Thailand version). The transfer went wrong because they assumed the two platforms' accounts were connected.</p>\n<p>The person had not failed to check the receiving UID. They missed a different point: <strong>platforms under the same brand do not mean they use the same account system.</strong></p>\n<p>Take Binance international and Binance TH as the example.</p>\n<p>They both use the Binance brand, but they are different services, and the account systems do not connect directly.</p>\n<p>There is a simple way to judge it:</p>\n<p>If you already registered on platform A, and you try to register the same account system again with the same email, it usually says this email is already registered.</p>\n<p>If the same email can register an account in two apps under the same brand, and you even have to complete KYC again, that is already a clear signal: these two platforms' account systems do not connect directly.</p>\n<p>Because they are two independent accounts, you have to confirm before a transfer whether the platforms support moving funds between them. Do not look only at the brand name.</p>\n<div class=\"key\"><strong>Remember:</strong> two independent systems cannot send by UID, unless the official side has explicitly provided a dedicated transfer route.</div>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. The same brand and the same email do not mean the two platforms can send to each other directly by UID. Check first whether the official side offers that route.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. The same brand, the same email, and KYC on both still do not mean you can send by UID by default. Check the transfer route the official side supports.\"><h3>Decide</h3><p>I registered on Binance international and on Binance TH. Both accounts use the same email, and both completed KYC. Since both are Binance platforms, can I move funds between the two accounts directly by UID?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. The same brand means you can transfer</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Do not assume it is supported. Check the official transfer route first</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Platforms under the same brand do not mean they use the same account system. See this first: a brand relationship is not shared accounts ✦ Same brand · different account systems Binance international Binance TH Do not assume they transfer internally Same brand is not the same account · the transfer method follows the official rules A diagram of the structure. It does not describe how the platform's database is actually built. A recent case: a user wanted to move assets from Binance international to Binance TH (the Thailand version). The transfer went wrong because they assumed the two platforms' accounts were connected. The person had not failed to check the receiving UID. They missed a different point: platforms under the same brand do not mean they use the same account system. Take Binance international and Binance TH as the example. They both use the Binance brand, but they are different services, and the account systems do not connect directly. There is a simple way to judge it: If you already registered on platform A, and you try to register the same account system again with the same email, it usually says this email is already registered. If the same email can register an account in two apps under the same brand, and you even have to complete KYC again, that is already a clear signal: these two platforms' account systems do not connect directly. Because they are two independent accounts, you have to confirm before a transfer whether the platforms support moving funds between them. Do not look only at the brand name. Remember: two independent systems cannot send by UID, unless the official side has explicitly provided a dedicated transfer route. Decide I registered on Binance international and on Binance TH. Both accounts use the same email, and both completed KYC. Since both are Binance platforms, can I move funds between the two accounts directly by UID? A. Yes. The same brand means you can transfer B. Do not assume it is supported. Check the official transfer route first"}, {"title": "The UID is right — why can the transfer still go wrong?", "html": "<p><strong>A UID is only a user number inside one platform, not a receiving identity for the whole industry.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"diagram\"><h3>The same number does not mean the same recipient</h3>\n<div class=\"orbit-scene\" aria-label=\"Two platforms each manage their own UIDs. The same number does not mean the same recipient\"><div class=\"space-glow\"></div><div class=\"orbit-ring ring-a\"></div><div class=\"orbit-ring ring-b\"></div>\n<div class=\"orbit-account account-left\"><div class=\"orb orb-a\"><img class=\"platform-app-icon\" src=\"assets/teach/binance-intl.png?v=20261108130000\" alt=\"Binance international app icon\"></div><div class=\"glass-account\"><span class=\"micro\">PLATFORM A</span><strong>Binance international</strong><span class=\"uid\">UID · 123456</span><span class=\"identity\"><i></i> Separate account system</span></div></div>\n<div class=\"orbit-account account-right\"><div class=\"orb orb-b\"><img class=\"platform-app-icon\" src=\"assets/teach/binance-th.png?v=20261108130000\" alt=\"Binance TH app icon\"></div><div class=\"glass-account\"><span class=\"micro\">PLATFORM B</span><strong>Binance TH</strong><span class=\"uid\">UID · 123456</span><span class=\"identity\"><i></i> Separate account system</span></div></div>\n<div class=\"broken-route\"><span class=\"route-line\"></span><div class=\"route-lock\"><span class=\"lock-symbol\">✕</span><b>Same UID is not the same recipient</b><small>Across platforms, a number alone does not identify the person</small></div><span class=\"route-line\"></span></div>\n<div class=\"orbit-foot\"><div><span class=\"tiny-dot\"></span> Check identity inside each platform</div><div><span class=\"tiny-dot\"></span> Confirm the transfer route the platform supports</div></div>\n</div><p class=\"diagram-caption\">The sample number only explains that the accounts are separate. It is not a real user's UID or a real transfer result.</p></div>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><span class=\"num\">01 / PLATFORM</span><b>Look at the platform first</b><p>Are the sender and the receiver on the same system?</p></div><div class=\"step\"><span class=\"num\">02 / IDENTITY</span><b>Then look at the recipient</b><p>Do the UID, the username, and the avatar match what you expect?</p></div><div class=\"step\"><span class=\"num\">03 / ROUTE</span><b>Then look at the route</b><p>Does the platform explicitly support this kind of transfer?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"key\"><strong>The core:</strong> a correct UID only means you checked one number. It does not prove the transfer route is right.</div>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"c\" data-ok=\"Correct. Matching UID digits only means the number matches. You still have to confirm that both sides are the same system, and that the platform supports this way of receiving.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Matching digits, or an avatar that looks similar, cannot replace confirming the platform and the receiving method the official side supports.\"><h3>How would you judge this?</h3><p>The receiving UID matches the number you were sent, but you are not sure the two apps connect.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Send it. The number is right</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Only check whether the avatar looks right</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Stop and confirm the platform and the receiving method it supports</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A UID is only a user number inside one platform, not a receiving identity for the whole industry. The same number does not mean the same recipient PLATFORM A Binance international UID · 123456 Separate account system PLATFORM B Binance TH UID · 123456 Separate account system ✕ Same UID is not the same recipient Across platforms, a number alone does not identify the person Check identity inside each platform Confirm the transfer route the platform supports The sample number only explains that the accounts are separate. It is not a real user's UID or a real transfer result. 01 / PLATFORM Look at the platform first Are the sender and the receiver on the same system? 02 / IDENTITY Then look at the recipient Do the UID, the username, and the avatar match what you expect? 03 / ROUTE Then look at the route Does the platform explicitly support this kind of transfer? The core: a correct UID only means you checked one number. It does not prove the transfer route is right. How would you judge this? The receiving UID matches the number you were sent, but you are not sure the two apps connect. A. Send it. The number is right B. Only check whether the avatar looks right C. Stop and confirm the platform and the receiving method it supports"}, {"title": "Before you transfer, get used to a three-step check", "html": "<p><strong>Before a transfer, confirm the platform, then the receiving details and the network; the first time you send somewhere, test a small amount before a large one.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"diagram\"><h3>The safety check before a transfer</h3>\n<div class=\"cosmic-scene check-scene\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Three stages: confirm the platform, check the receiving details and the network, then test a small amount\"><div class=\"check-track\"><div class=\"track-line\"></div>\n<div class=\"check-node\"><div class=\"node-orb orb-blue\"><span class=\"planet-texture\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><span class=\"orb-number\">01</span></div><b>Confirm the platform</b><small>Does it support the transfer</small></div>\n<div class=\"check-node\"><div class=\"node-orb orb-rose\"><span class=\"planet-texture\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><span class=\"orb-number\">02</span></div><b>Check the details</b><small>Identity · coin · network</small></div>\n<div class=\"check-node\"><div class=\"node-orb orb-gold\"><span class=\"planet-texture\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><span class=\"orb-number\">03</span></div><b>Small test</b><small>Confirm it actually arrived</small></div>\n</div><div class=\"scene-bottom\">Finish the checks in order before you consider the real transfer</div></div></div>\n\n<p><strong>Step 1: confirm the platform</strong></p>\n<p>See clearly which platform the sender belongs to, and which platform the receiver belongs to.</p>\n<p>In general, different app entrances correspond to different account systems and databases, especially platforms run for different countries or regions.</p>\n<p>So do not recognize only the brand logo.<br />\nEven if the names are similar, confirm the specific platform, whether the accounts connect, and whether an internal transfer is supported.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: confirm the receiving details and the network</strong></p>\n<p>For an internal transfer, besides the UID, the email, and other receiving identifiers, also check whether the <strong>username and avatar</strong> shown for the recipient match what you expect.</p>\n<p>But a username and an avatar are only extra checks. They cannot by themselves prove who the recipient is.</p>\n<p>For an on-chain transfer, check the coin, the receiving address, and the blockchain network.</p>\n<p>For example, USDT on TRC20 and USDT on ERC20 are not networks you can mix freely.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: test a small amount, then send the large one</strong></p>\n<p>Especially the first time you transfer to a platform, an address, or an account, test with a small amount first.</p>\n<p>Confirm that the other side actually received it, and that the coin and the network are right, before you send the rest.</p>\n<p>A small test cannot guarantee that later transfers are absolutely safe, but it can at least stop one mistake from losing the whole amount.</p>\n<div class=\"checklist\"><label class=\"checkrow\"><input type=\"checkbox\"><span><b>Confirm the platform</b><small>Do not look only at the brand logo. Check the platform name and whether it supports this transfer.</small></span></label><label class=\"checkrow\"><input type=\"checkbox\"><span><b>Confirm the receiving details and the network</b><small>Internally, check the UID, username, avatar, and so on. On-chain, check the address, the coin, and the network. The avatar is only an extra check.</small></span></label><label class=\"checkrow\"><input type=\"checkbox\"><span><b>Test a small amount first</b><small>The first transfer to a place should be a small test. Confirm it really arrived before you consider the rest.</small></span></label></div>\n<p class=\"teach-status\" data-tpl=\"Checks {n} / {m} · scenario {quiz}\" data-yes=\"Passed\" data-no=\"Not yet\">Checks 0 / 3 · scenario Not yet</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Even after the UID, the username, and the avatar match, you still have to confirm that the two apps can transfer directly. If you are not sure, pause first.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Do not send a large amount as a test, and do not look only at the avatar. If you are not sure they connect, stop and check the method the official side supports.\"><h3>Check · scenario</h3><p>You have checked the UID, the username, and the avatar, and you are not sure the two apps can transfer directly. What should you do?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Send a large amount first, and see whether it arrives</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Pause the transfer, and check the transfer method the official side supports</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. If the avatars match, you can send</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>\n<div class=\"key\"><strong>Note:</strong> a small test does not replace checking the platform and the recipient, and it does not guarantee that later transfers are absolutely safe.</div>", "text": "Before a transfer, confirm the platform, then the receiving details and the network; the first time you send somewhere, test a small amount before a large one. The safety check before a transfer 01 Confirm the platform Does it support the transfer 02 Check the details Identity · coin · network 03 Small test Confirm it actually arrived Finish the checks in order before you consider the real transfer Step 1: confirm the platform See clearly which platform the sender belongs to, and which platform the receiver belongs to. In general, different app entrances correspond to different account systems and databases, especially platforms run for different countries or regions. So do not recognize only the brand logo. Even if the names are similar, confirm the specific platform, whether the accounts connect, and whether an internal transfer is supported. Step 2: confirm the receiving details and the network For an internal transfer, besides the UID, the email, and other receiving identifiers, also check whether the username and avatar shown for the recipient match what you expect. But a username and an avatar are only extra checks. They cannot by themselves prove who the recipient is. For an on-chain transfer, check the coin, the receiving address, and the blockchain network. For example, USDT on TRC20 and USDT on ERC20 are not networks you can mix freely. Step 3: test a small amount, then send the large one Especially the first time you transfer to a platform, an address, or an account, test with a small amount first. Confirm that the other side actually received it, and that the coin and the network are right, before you send the rest. A small test cannot guarantee that later transfers are absolutely safe, but it can at least stop one mistake from losing the whole amount. Confirm the platform Do not look only at the brand logo. Check the platform name and whether it supports this transfer. Confirm the receiving details and the network Internally, check the UID, username, avatar, and so on. On-chain, check the address, the coin, and the network. The avatar is only an extra check. Test a small amount first The first transfer to a place should be a small test. Confirm it really arrived before you consider the rest. Checks 0 / 3 · scenario Not yet Check · scenario You have checked the UID, the username, and the avatar, and you are not sure the two apps can transfer directly. What should you do? A. Send a large amount first, and see whether it arrives B. Pause the transfer, and check the transfer method the official side supports C. If the avatars match, you can send Note: a small test does not replace checking the platform and the recipient, and it does not guarantee that later transfers are absolutely safe."}, {"title": "What if you already sent it to the wrong place?", "html": "<p><strong>After a wrong transfer, save the record and contact official support, but do not put your hope in fixing it afterward.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"diagram\"><h3>A wrong internal transfer: which steps usually come next?</h3>\n<div class=\"cosmic-scene recovery-scene\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Steps after a wrong internal transfer: keep evidence, an official appeal, the platform checks, talk with the recipient, handle it under the rules\"><div class=\"recovery-track\"><div class=\"track-line\"></div>\n<div class=\"recovery-node\"><div class=\"recovery-orb\">01</div><b>Keep evidence</b><small>Records and screenshots</small></div>\n<div class=\"recovery-node\"><div class=\"recovery-orb\">02</div><b>Official appeal</b><small>Submit the details</small></div>\n<div class=\"recovery-node\"><div class=\"recovery-orb\">03</div><b>Platform check</b><small>Verify the trade</small></div>\n<div class=\"recovery-node\"><div class=\"recovery-orb\">04</div><b>Talk it through</b><small>Contact the recipient</small></div>\n<div class=\"recovery-node\"><div class=\"recovery-orb\">05</div><b>Follow the rules</b><small>The result is not guaranteed</small></div>\n</div><div class=\"scene-bottom\">The platform cannot move another user's assets on one person's word alone</div></div></div>\n\n<p>First save the transfer record, the order number, the receiving UID, the transaction hash if there is one, and the related screenshots, and contact official support as soon as you can.</p>\n<p>If it was a wrong internal transfer, you usually have to file an appeal or a report under the platform's rules. The platform checks the transaction record and, depending on the case, helps contact the actual recipient to talk it through.</p>\n<p>One basic principle:</p>\n<p><strong>Once the funds are in another user's account, the exchange cannot take them back out of that account on the sender's word alone.</strong></p>\n<p>Even if the transfer really was a mistake, it still has to go through identity checks, an investigation of the transaction, and the matching process.</p>\n<p>If the other side will not cooperate, recovering the funds can be very hard, and you may need a legal route.</p>\n<p>If it was a wrong on-chain transfer, a transaction that is already confirmed usually cannot be undone.<br />\nIn particular, if it went to an address you cannot control, or onto the wrong network, the chance of recovery can be very small.</p>\n<p>So do not put your hope in fixing it afterward.</p>\n<p>A last reminder</p>\n<p>The easiest place for a crypto transfer to go wrong is sometimes not a complicated technique. It is a piece of common sense you thought was obvious.</p>\n<p>Two extra minutes of checking before you send is much easier than several days of looking for a fix after it is wrong.</p>\n<div class=\"compare\"><div><b>Wrong internal transfer</b><p>The platform can check the record and handle it under the rules, but it cannot take another user's assets on the sender's word alone. Another person's account usually needs verification, contact, and the proper process.</p></div><div><b>Wrong on-chain transfer</b><p>A transaction that is confirmed on-chain usually cannot be undone. If it went to an address you cannot control, or onto the wrong network, recovery can be extremely hard, or impossible.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"key\"><strong>A plain reminder:</strong> after a wrong transfer there is no one-tap undo. What you can do is use the formal channel quickly. Fixing it afterward is not a safety net.</div>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Once the money is in someone else's account, the exchange cannot pull it back just because you described what happened. It has to be checked, and it has to follow the process.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Explaining that it was a mistake does not mean the platform can pull the funds straight back from the other account. It has to be checked, and it has to follow the process.\"><h3>If the funds are already in someone else's account?</h3><p>Can the exchange pull the money back from the other account on your description alone?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes, as long as you explain it was a mistake</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. It has to be checked, and it has to follow the relevant process</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "After a wrong transfer, save the record and contact official support, but do not put your hope in fixing it afterward. A wrong internal transfer: which steps usually come next? 01 Keep evidence Records and screenshots 02 Official appeal Submit the details 03 Platform check Verify the trade 04 Talk it through Contact the recipient 05 Follow the rules The result is not guaranteed The platform cannot move another user's assets on one person's word alone First save the transfer record, the order number, the receiving UID, the transaction hash if there is one, and the related screenshots, and contact official support as soon as you can. If it was a wrong internal transfer, you usually have to file an appeal or a report under the platform's rules. The platform checks the transaction record and, depending on the case, helps contact the actual recipient to talk it through. One basic principle: Once the funds are in another user's account, the exchange cannot take them back out of that account on the sender's word alone. Even if the transfer really was a mistake, it still has to go through identity checks, an investigation of the transaction, and the matching process. If the other side will not cooperate, recovering the funds can be very hard, and you may need a legal route. If it was a wrong on-chain transfer, a transaction that is already confirmed usually cannot be undone. In particular, if it went to an address you cannot control, or onto the wrong network, the chance of recovery can be very small. So do not put your hope in fixing it afterward. A last reminder The easiest place for a crypto transfer to go wrong is sometimes not a complicated technique. It is a piece of common sense you thought was obvious. Two extra minutes of checking before you send is much easier than several days of looking for a fix after it is wrong. Wrong internal transfer The platform can check the record and handle it under the rules, but it cannot take another user's assets on the sender's word alone. Another person's account usually needs verification, contact, and the proper process. Wrong on-chain transfer A transaction that is confirmed on-chain usually cannot be undone. If it went to an address you cannot control, or onto the wrong network, recovery can be extremely hard, or impossible. A plain reminder: after a wrong transfer there is no one-tap undo. What you can do is use the formal channel quickly. Fixing it afterward is not a safety net. If the funds are already in someone else's account? Can the exchange pull the money back from the other account on your description alone? A. Yes, as long as you explain it was a mistake B. No. It has to be checked, and it has to follow the relevant process"}]}]};
